window.TEAMS = [{"team":"UOregon","id":"6041","section":"Overgrad","country":"United States","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/","projectTitle":"AquaFlock","tagline":"E. coli engineered to sequester groundwater nitrate as cyanophycin, then flocculate so the nitrogen can be harvested back as fertilizer","summary":"UOregon engineered E. coli to pull excess environmental nitrate into the nitrogen storage polymer cyanophycin and, once a cyanophycin threshold is reached, to express flocculation proteins so cells sink and can be harvested as a fertilizer substitute, closing the nitrogen loop. The target users are rural well owners and farmers in Oregon whose water is rarely tested. The education practice is the standout: three original curricula built for three audiences with increasing prior knowledge, developed through an explicit Design-Build-Test-Learn loop and grounded in Universal Design for Learning plus STEAM. The team also built an offline desktop search engine indexing the full text of 1,354 iGEM team wikis and 20,761 pages from 2022-2025.","awards":["Best Education — Nominee (Overgrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Education itself was run as a DBTL engineering cycle, with a documented Reflective Theory of Change diagram and a standardised success rubric (engagement, science understanding, creativity), both adapted from an Oregon State University STEM Research Center presentation at the Polar STEAM Summit 2026. Learning objectives were written against the measurable verbs of Bloom's Taxonomy (Remember, Understand, Apply, Analyze, Evaluate, Create).","dataviz":"Hand-illustrated scientific diagrams throughout: all educational materials were digitally illustrated by hand by the team, including a central-dogma comic, illustrated workbooks, and illustrated synthetic-biology-tool cards used as a tactile matching activity.","datascience":"Post-workshop feedback surveys with section-level ranking; for Workshop 3, 8 of 15 respondents had heard of synthetic biology before attending, and bioethics and science communication ranked as the favourite sections, with the illustrated workbook, real-world examples and small-group discussions named the most effective learning tools.","software":"iGEM Wiki Search: a desktop application that searches the full text of 1,354 team wikis and 20,761 pages from the 2022-2025 competitions from a single search box, running offline with no server. It automates wiki collection and processing, builds a full-text index, ranks whole projects rather than individual pages, crawls responsibly, handles JavaScript-only wikis, and was tested cross-platform. The team benchmarked it explicitly against iGEM's Phoenix Project (metadata only) and iGEM Munich 2024's Data Explorer (2019 wikis only, no source links), and links every result back to its original page.","stack":["desktop application (offline)","full-text indexing","web crawler with JavaScript rendering","Universal Design for Learning (UDL) framework","STEAM curriculum design","Bloom's Taxonomy"],"techniques":["cyanophycin nitrogen-storage pathway engineering","flocculation-protein inducible circuit","split biosensor","nitrate sequestration assays","AquaFlock hardware settling system"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"UOregon's central design principle is quotable and transferable: 'Accessibility should lower the barrier of entry into complex science, not the complexity of the science itself.' Instead of one generic outreach talk, the team wrote three original curricula for three audiences with increasing prior knowledge and ran them as three DBTL iterations across June, July and October 2026, carrying the lessons of each into the next. Workshop 1 ('Intro to Synthetic Biology') was a one-hour session plus an illustrated take-home worksheet delivered to 30 high school students at the Oregon Humane Society summer camp; students reviewed the central dogma and plasmid parts and then designed their own engineered bacterium to solve a problem of their choosing. Post-event debriefs with students and camp staff produced four concrete fixes (establish prior knowledge first, add more real-world relevance, shorten transitions to reduce cognitive load, create more peer-interaction points), which were fed directly into Workshop 2. Workshop 2 ('Engineering SynBio Solutions') was a two-hour session for 20 high school students at SAIL (Student Academy to Inspire Learning), an existing University of Oregon STEM summer camp for students from diverse learning backgrounds; materials were a fully illustrated workbook, a hand-drawn comic that told the central dogma as a story, slides, and hands-on activities, with each of five sections framed by its own guiding question and sequenced to scaffold from foundational biology to CRISPR and plasmids to genetic circuits to experimental design. Workshop 3 ('SynBio Solutions to Real-World Problems') was a two-hour session for 20 first- and second-year undergraduate science students at the University of Oregon, funded by a University of Oregon Knight Campus Graduate Student Association outreach mini-grant; because this audience already had foundational biology, the team swapped the comic for a matching-game refresher, added directed evolution and de novo protein design alongside CRISPR and plasmids, and then extended into two sections the earlier workshops could not reach: small-group bioethics analysis of three real-world synthetic biology applications, and a science-communication activity in which students decorated tote bags to explain what they had learned to an audience of their own choosing. Evidence of impact is tied to the standardised rubric rather than headcount alone: engagement, understanding and creativity were tracked across iterations through direct post-workshop feedback forms and indirect observation, Workshop 2 students asked unprompted questions beyond the taught material (one asked whether CRISPR and plasmids could be used at once), and the Workshop 3 survey ranked bioethics and science communication highest. Beyond workshops, the team published a comic illustrating the AquaFlock project's journey and released the iGEM Wiki Search desktop tool specifically to make it easier for future teams to find and build on predecessors' work - an education contribution aimed at the iGEM community itself.","ihp":"Human Practices was framed around rural equity: people living outside major cities receive less financial backing, less testing of wells and waterways, and less proactive healthcare, so the project is designed as a circular chain of agricultural, health and economic support for communities marginalised by municipal systems. The team mapped a four-step implementation path (in the home, then at the agricultural source, then harvest, then reduce/reuse/recycle) and stated the striking design goal that 'our product is made to eventually become obsolete' once nitrate levels fall out of the unsafe range. A field trip to the Metropolitan Wastewater Management Commission regional wastewater treatment facility serving Eugene and Springfield let the team interview plant operators, who confirmed nitrate control (alongside PFAS and silicon) is an unsolved challenge, validating a gap their smaller-scale system could fill. Four UN SDGs were adopted as focal areas: 3 (good health), 4 (quality education), 6 (clean water and sanitation) and 12 (responsible consumption and production).","realWorld":"The intended users are rural Oregon well owners and farmers; harvested flocs become a local fertilizer substitute, localising fertilizer access and cutting nitrate at source. Context established on the description page: roughly half of applied nitrogen fertilizer is taken up by plants and the rest leaches as nitrate, driving eutrophication, harmful algal blooms and hypoxia as well as direct human health risk from contaminated drinking water.","partners":["Oregon Humane Society (summer camp, Workshop 1 venue and audience)","SAIL - Student Academy to Inspire Learning, University of Oregon STEM summer camp (Workshop 2)","University of Oregon Knight Campus Graduate Student Association (outreach mini-grant funding Workshop 3)","Metropolitan Wastewater Management Commission (MWMC) Regional Wastewater Treatment Facility, Eugene-Springfield","Oregon State University STEM Research Center (Reflective Theory of Change and success-measurement frameworks)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/edu-wksp1-main-photo.avif","caption":"Workshop 1: one-hour intro to synthetic biology for 30 high school students at the Oregon Humane Society camp","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/edu-wksp2-main-photo.avif","caption":"Workshop 2 at the SAIL STEM summer camp: 20 high school students working through the illustrated workbook and comic","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/edu-wksp1-wkst.avif","caption":"The hand-illustrated take-home worksheet from Workshop 1, covering the central dogma and plasmid parts","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/wksp3-1.avif","caption":"Workshop 3 for 20 undergraduate science students, adding bioethics discussion and a tote-bag science-communication activity","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6041/wiki/pages/education/measuring-success.avif","caption":"The team's standardised measure of success across workshop iterations: engagement, science understanding and creativity","credit":"Team UOregon, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Run outreach as a DBTL cycle with a fixed success rubric: build three differentiated versions of the same curriculum for audiences at different prior-knowledge levels, debrief with the host staff after each delivery, and score every iteration on the same three criteria (engagement, understanding, creativity) so improvement is demonstrable rather than asserted.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/field-trip","https://2026.igem.wiki/uoregon/sustainability"],"rank":7},{"team":"UOttawa","id":"6077","section":"Undergrad","country":"Canada","village":"Bioremediation","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/","projectTitle":"Nulfur","tagline":"Break bonds, not the fuel: engineered biodesulfurization as a polishing step for refractory sulfur in diesel.","summary":"Nulfur rebuilds the four-enzyme 4S biodesulfurization pathway (DszA, DszB, DszC plus a flavin reductase) from Rhodococcus erythropolis inside Pseudomonas putida KT2440, targeting dibenzothiophene (DBT) — the refractory sulfur compound that conventional hydrodesulfurization struggles to remove without running at 300-400 C and 3-6 MPa. Rather than replacing hydrodesulfurization, the team reframed the project after industry consultation as a modular side-stream polishing step on diesel. The project won on the strength of its alternative chassis work: because P. putida is a non-model organism, the team built the missing toolkit from scratch (a Golden Gate-compatible broad-host-range vector, a measured four-promoter set, and a working heat-shock transformation protocol) and published it as a handbook for other teams. That platform contribution, paired with an unusually deep dry lab (COBRApy genome-scale modelling, a FoldX/ThermoMPNN protein engineering pipeline, and an agentic AI research platform called Cofactor), won Best Alternative Platform and Best Wiki and Best Bioremediation Project nominations.","awards":["Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Alternative Platform — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Bioremediation Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Three distinct computational layers. (1) Genome-scale metabolic modelling: the published P. putida KT2440 model iJN1463 was extended in COBRApy with the heterologous 4S pathway reactions (DszA/B/C/D), and flux balance analysis solved through GLPK over a 1000-point sweep of the DBT exchange flux bound showed metabolism never bottlenecks uptake, while forcing DszB flux revealed an NADH-limited ceiling of 9.80 mmol gDCW-1 h-1 that formate co-feeding raises 25.5% to 12.30. (2) A first-principles mass-transfer model of the biphasic reactor combining Hinze-Kolmogorov droplet-breakup scaling, film theory at the stagnant-sphere limit (Sh = 2), micellar solubilization above the CMC and Michaelis-Menten uptake, solved by bisection for the steady-state free DBT concentration; it predicts rhamnolipid raises effective DBT availability 2.8- to 34-fold. (3) A protein engineering pipeline that screened over 1,100 candidate substitutions in DszB and DszC using ConSurf conservation grades, MD-derived backbone RMSF, and an ensemble of FoldX (empirical force field) plus ThermoMPNN (deep learning on ProteinMPNN embeddings) for ddG, then validated catalytic integrity with explicit-solvent GROMACS MD (amber99sb-ildn, TIP3P) and AutoDock Vina ligand docking before shortlisting five mutants per enzyme.","dataviz":"The wiki is a server-rendered-feel React site with a genuinely engineered visual layer: a WebGL/Three.js distillation-tower scene and animated pathway loop on the landing page, every equation rendered as its own labelled SVG so the derivation reads as a chain, and predictor-agreement scatter plots (Figure 9) that shade the quadrant where FoldX and ThermoMPNN both call a substitution stabilising and enlarge it to label the five mutations actually sent to the bench. The Human Practices page is built as a clickable stacked column where each band is one of seven kinds of conversation across 21 entries, so a judge can jump straight to the stakeholder group they care about. Every figure carries a long descriptive alt attribute that states the figure's actual numbers, not just its subject.","datascience":"The headline data-science contribution is turning the protein engineering loop into a measured, auditable artefact. The team built a six-arm evaluation suite for Cofactor with four ablation regimes (bare, bare_prompt, tools, full) that add exactly one harness layer each, then scored outcomes: 37 deterministic probes in Arms A and B all pass, Arm C recorded zero workflow violations across 27 scorable runs under the phase machine versus two in the bare condition, and Arm D found the ungrounded condition hallucinated amino-acid positions while both grounded conditions did not. Arm E asked the agent to reconstruct the initial experimental designs of six published papers (three postdating the model's knowledge cutoff) and audited tool transcripts for contamination. On the wet-lab side, the promoter library was quantified as log-normalized mScarlet-i fluorescence per OD600 corrected for media background and P. putida autofluorescence, which revealed only three distinct expression tiers rather than the four reported in the literature.","software":"Cofactor, an agentic protein engineering platform released under MIT licence at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uottawa/cofactor. It wraps a general-purpose LLM in an explicit phase state machine whose preconditions are checked against a 16-table SQLite store (.cofactor/research.sqlite3, WAL journaling, foreign keys) rather than against the model's claims, so runs resume across sessions and the research record stays readable without the tool. A deterministic tool layer covers sequence operations, RFC10 compliance checking, codon optimisation, restriction analysis, solubility scanning, and retrieval from UniProt, NCBI, RCSB PDB, AlphaFold, PubMed and Europe PMC; an ExecutionManager submits jobs to local, SSH or Slurm backends. Three frontends ship: a FastAPI web UI, a Textual TUI and a CLI, all bring-your-own-key and model-agnostic against any OpenAI-compatible endpoint. The team also released the P. putida handbook with protocols and the sanitized Golden Gate-ready pBBR1MCS-2 vector to the iGEM Registry.","stack":["COBRApy","GLPK","iJN1463 genome-scale model","FoldX","ThermoMPNN","ProteinMPNN embeddings","ConSurf","GROMACS","amber99sb-ildn / TIP3P","AutoDock Vina","Python 3.11","SQLite (WAL)","FastAPI","Textual TUI","Slurm / SSH job submission","React","Three.js / WebGL","Open Babel"],"techniques":["Flux balance analysis","Genome-scale metabolic model extension","Hinze-Kolmogorov droplet-breakup scaling","Film-theory interfacial mass transfer","Micellar solubilization modelling","Michaelis-Menten uptake kinetics","Bisection root-finding","Sensitivity analysis over droplet size","ddG ensemble prediction","Molecular dynamics (RMSF, native-contact persistence)","Ligand docking","LLM agent harness with phase-gated state machine","Ablation-based evaluation (4 regimes, 6 arms)","Rubric scoring by two independent judges","Techno-economic analysis","Life cycle assessment","HAZOP / P&ID process safety analysis"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education is organised around four stated principles - Access, Adapt, Engage, Extend - and each initiative is written up against one of them. The team ran bilingual (English/French) sessions for 370+ children aged 4-13 in the Ottawa-Carleton District School Board's International and Indigenous Languages Program summer school, reached 70 more through the Ottawa Catholic School Board, and visited AcadeCap International School for ages 4-7. Original hands-on activities include Lava Lamps (oil properties), The Art of Consortia (a collaborative mural standing in for microbial division of labour), Mix and Match Genes, The Power of Bacterial DNA, and a Heavy vs Light Oil worksheet. For durability they published a free interactive children's magazine, Bio-Builder, distributed through local Ottawa public libraries explicitly to reach low-income families who cannot buy such resources, plus a demo list, infographics, a science and research podcast, and a full downloadable resource library of presentations and worksheets so the activities run without the team present. They also hosted the Great Canadian iGEM Incubator poster competition (with QGEM) and the Great Canadian Meetup pitch competition, both with published scoring rubrics.","ihp":"21 conversations across seven categories, each logged in the same three-part structure: why we went, what we heard, what we changed. The changes are concrete. Dr. Jinwen Chen (Natural Resources Canada, CanmetENERGY-Devon) and Dr. Stephen Larter (Calgary, petroleum geochemist) both told the team to abandon heavy diluted bitumen for diesel because of viscosity, mass-transfer and emulsion-separation barriers, and because diesel carries a hard <10 ppm sulfur limit and concentrated refractory DBTs - the team moved its entire target stream to diesel polishing. Dr. Rasha Maal-Bared warned that viscous crude mixes in localised laminar flow, which put static mixing elements and bioreactor sizing into the continuous-flow design. Patrick Lumley (Safe Sulphur Systems, 33 years in sulfur recovery) said the aqueous sulfite byproduct needs pH control to prevent SO2 release and that Claus-plant load reduction is economically worthless - so a pH control loop went into the P&ID and the business case was rebuilt around hydrogen savings and reduced hydrotreating severity instead of sulfur throughput. Larter's warning about microbially influenced corrosion added corrosion and material-compatibility criteria to the reactor design. Faculty of Engineering Design Day feedback forced the pivot from a full HDS-replacement narrative to the complementary polishing model. Dr. Corrie DaCosta, a protein chemist, was consulted specifically about what Cofactor's output report must contain to be usable by the person doing the engineering, and the report format changed as a result.","realWorld":"A fully worked commercialization case. Market sizing: TAM ~$3.1B (global HDS catalyst market), SAM ~$66M (Canada's 2.2% share, 16 refineries, ~2M bbl/day), SOM ~$1.4M from a single-refinery side-stream demo at a $0.40/bbl royalty on a 10,000 bbl/day stream. Revenue model is per-barrel licensing royalties, validated in conversation with an operations and project engineer. A techno-economic analysis compares BDS against HDS CAPEX of $16-42M USD for a 20,000-25,000 bbl/day refinery and projects 50-60% CAPEX reduction from eliminating high-pressure hydrogen hardware; a life cycle assessment with a declared system boundary and BDS/HDS inventories backs the environmental claim rather than asserting it. A staged timeline runs from proof of concept (2026) through publication and NSERC Alliance / Mitacs Accelerate / CRIN non-dilutive funding (2027), pilot demonstration on real feedstock plus formal pre-engagement with Environment and Climate Change Canada under New Substances Notification regulations for contained LMO deployment (2028-29), to licensing (2030+) and expansion into biodenitrogenation. The team deliberately targets smaller, older refineries facing Euro V limits with constrained hydrogen capacity rather than major oil sands operators, on Lumley's advice, and flags biogas producers as a faster early-revenue path. Risk analysis, SWOT, Business Model Canvas and a competitive positioning matrix are all published, and the team is explicit that Nulfur is harm reduction during the energy transition - \"a bridge, not a destination\".","partners":["Natural Resources Canada (CanmetENERGY-Devon)","Environment and Climate Change Canada, Low Carbon Fuels Division","Safe Sulphur Systems Ltd.","CDM Smith","Spanish National Research Council (CSIC), Systems Biology Program","Bedford Institute of Oceanography, Fisheries and Oceans Canada","Institute for Oil Sands Innovation / University of Alberta","University of Calgary","Queen's University","Arabian Gulf University","Hawk & Squirrel Innovation Inc.","Ottawa-Carleton District School Board (IILP Summer Program)","Ottawa Catholic School Board","AcadeCap International School","Ottawa Public Libraries","uOttawa eHub RevUp Circuit","MindFuel Tech Futures Challenge","QGEM (Great Canadian iGEM Incubator)","Clean Resource Innovation Network (CRIN)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/dry-lab/protein-engineering/fig-3.svg","caption":"Predictor-agreement scatter for 437 scored DszB mutations: the shaded quadrant is where FoldX and ThermoMPNN both call a substitution stabilising, enlarged to label the five variants advanced to the wet lab.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/dry-lab/cofactor/figures/fig-4b.avif","caption":"The Cofactor GUI - a local FastAPI web interface over the agentic protein engineering platform, showing chat, hypothesis and evidence views, job tracking and artifact browsing.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/dry-lab/consortia/fig-3.avif","caption":"Rate gain in DBT uptake from interfacial-area enhancement and micellar solubilization in isolation and combined; the shaded region is the synergy in excess of the product of the two isolated effects.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/education/adapt/art-consortia/pxl-20260716-173536329-mp.avif","caption":"The Art of Consortia activity in the Ottawa-Carleton District School Board summer language programme, where children build a shared mural to learn how a microbial consortium divides labour.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6077/wiki/process/igem-p-id-final.avif","caption":"Process and instrumentation diagram for the full-scale biodesulfurization plant, including the pH control loop added after Patrick Lumley warned that uncontrolled sulfite pH would release SO2.","credit":"Team UOttawa, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy uOttawa's human-practices template literally: a single page where every one of 21 stakeholder conversations is logged in the same three fields - why we went, what we heard, what we changed - with the change written as a specific design edit (a pH loop in the P&ID, a switch of feedstock, static mixers). It costs nothing to adopt, makes a student team's reasoning auditable, and is the clearest way a school research centre can show that outside input actually moved the project rather than decorating it.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/alternative-platform","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/process-design-and-analysis","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/parts","https://2026.igem.wiki/uottawa/gcm","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uottawa/cofactor"],"rank":110},{"team":"UPF-Barcelona","id":"6078","section":"Undergrad","country":"Spain","village":"Food & Agriculture","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/","projectTitle":"Vinesys","tagline":"Where the rain runs out, the roots hold on: engineering root-associated Bacillus subtilis to hold water around grapevine roots.","summary":"Vinesys engineers Bacillus subtilis to colonise the grapevine rhizosphere and overexpress gamma-PGA, a water-retaining biopolymer, plus ACC deaminase to trim the drought-stress hormone precursor ACC, forming a thin hydration shell where the bacterium attaches to the root. Drought is detected by a two-layer switch: an osmolarity-responsive promoter plus a salt-dependent intein (HutMCM2) that the team re-engineered in silico to splice at the roughly 600-725 mM intracellular K+ that B. subtilis actually reaches, rather than its native 1.5 M. The dry lab produced three distinct deliverables: RK4 solver, a standalone no-code genetic-circuit designer and simulator; a documented molecular-dynamics pipeline for tuning salt-dependent proteins; and iGEMforge, a Claude Code plugin of four research skills that they evaluated honestly and then declined to use for project decisions. Awards came for Software, Model and Education.","awards":["Best Software — Winner (Undergrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"RK4 solver is a desktop Python application with a GUI built in Dear PyGui, structured as three partially independent modules (GUI, compiler, solver) so the solver can be driven from Python without drawing anything on screen. It ships for Windows, macOS and Linux. Interchange is handled by two formats: a readable personal JSON format and SBML Level 3, which gives round-trip compatibility with COPASI and Tellurium. The molecular-dynamics pipeline runs ColabFold/AlphaFold for structures, FoldX for stability screening, MAFFT and BLAST for candidate selection, UCSF Chimera for inspection, and OpenMM with Amber force fields (tleap setup) for the simulations. iGEMforge is a Claude Code plugin: four Markdown SKILL files plus a couple of Python scripts in a folder, deliberately with no subagents, hooks or custom MCP servers.","dataviz":"RK4 solver's results screen plots species concentrations while every parameter has a live slider that takes effect during the running simulation, which is the core interaction idea. The editor uses blocks whose icons follow SBOL Visual glyphs, with input pins always on the left and output pins on the right so links read left to right, collapsible blocks, and a diagnostics/warnings pane with connection rules that refuse to let you build an invalid circuit. The wiki itself is built as explanatory interactive JavaScript rather than static figures: an in-browser Euler-vs-RK4 comparison on x'' = -x, a step-through animation of the solver's operator stack, a replayable eval harness showing the same prompt answered with and without an iGEMforge skill, and a hoverable stakeholder map on the entrepreneurship page.","datascience":"The solver was validated numerically against COPASI: three example circuits were exported to SBML and re-simulated in COPASI under LSODA with identical parameters, initial concentrations and simulation time, and every quantity that means the same thing in both tools agreed within 0.21%, most within 0.01%. The apparent 13.4% and 98.5% discrepancies on free mazF and mazE were traced to a labelling difference in rapid-equilibrium binding, not integration error, and resolved by comparing totals (mazF + 2*FEF, mazE + FEF), which then matched within 0.01% and 0.09%. iGEMforge was evaluated in two rounds: 11 hand-graded assertion tests in round one, then 28 cases run three times per arm (with-skill vs. without-skill) using Claude Code's `claude plugin eval` with an LLM-as-a-judge against team-written rubrics, including real cases, synthetic cases with deliberately planted flaws, and 'silence' cases where a skill should stay out of the way. The MD work used replicas, a wild-type positive control at its known 1.5 M threshold, an uncharged no-effect control, and re-ran its analysis under several different attack-distance cutoffs to show the conclusion did not depend on one arbitrary value.","software":"RK4 solver is the main tool: a free, open-source, cross-platform application that turns a hand-drawn genetic circuit into a running ODE simulation with no code at all, aimed squarely at iGEM teams, high-school and undergraduate students, and explicitly not at replacing COPASI or SimBiology. Eight block types are implemented (production with Hill activation/repression, degradation, complex formation in rapid-equilibrium or kinetic mode, extracellular species with six dosing regimes, cell entry, export, catalysis with Michaelis-Menten or mass action, and a link probe), and the compiler translates the drawing into a flat list of stack operators which the solver evaluates with Euler or 4th-order Runge-Kutta. The architecture is worth copying: the translator sorts each block's contribution by the species it points to rather than by the block's own species, a redesign forced by discovering that complex formation was writing its negative terms into the wrong derivative. It was validated twice, against COPASI numerically and against the team's own biocontainment toxin-antitoxin model biologically, and it was user-tested, with non-expert testers building and tuning a toggle switch in 5 to 20 minutes after a short introduction to the notation. Limitations are stated plainly on the wiki (deterministic only, partial SBML import, custom block unfinished, GUI not yet as intuitive as they want) with a three-horizon roadmap including SBOL-based integration with the iGEM Registry that would pull part parameters in and mark any user override as traceably overwritten. Source: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/upf-barcelona/upf-software (team repo https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/upf-barcelona). It is distributed as a downloadable cross-platform application, not a hosted web service, so there is no click-and-use URL. The second tool, iGEMforge, is the most interesting negative result of the season: a Claude Code plugin of four skills (scientific-evidence, experimental-design, mathematical-modeling, bio-wiki-animations) built over two DBTL cycles, in which round-one hand-grading suggested the skills helped (bio-wiki-animations 12/13 assertions with the skill against 5/12 without) but round two with three runs per arm and an LLM judge showed no clear average gain (experimental-design -0.11, scientific-evidence +0.04, mathematical-modeling no clear difference), at substantially higher token cost (309k vs 224k; 468k vs 201k). Testing revealed that three of four skills intermittently stalled waiting on user confirmation and returned nothing; cycle two fixed it with a single rule written into the skills ('state the assumption or caveat and continue'), which restored output in 3 of 3 runs but still did not beat the base model. The team's conclusion, published on the wiki, is that the evidence was too thin to use iGEMforge for Vinesys decisions, and they list cost, privacy, over-trust and installation risks explicitly.","stack":["Python","Dear PyGui","SBML Level 3","SBOL Visual","JSON","COPASI (validation target)","Tellurium","OpenMM","Amber force field / tleap","ColabFold","AlphaFold","FoldX","MAFFT","BLAST","UCSF Chimera","Claude Code plugin (Markdown skills + Python)","claude plugin eval","iGEM GitLab","JavaScript (interactive wiki figures)"],"techniques":["ODE modelling of genetic circuits","4th-order Runge-Kutta and Euler integration","stack-machine operator compilation of differential equations","Hill activation/repression kinetics","rapid-equilibrium and kinetic complex formation","Michaelis-Menten catalysis","molecular dynamics with near-attack-conformation (NAC) readout","ddG stability screening","pLDDT/PAE structure confidence assessment","LLM-as-a-judge evaluation with pre-written rubrics","A/B testing with planted-flaw synthetic cases","SBML round-trip cross-validation"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education is organised around an explicit six-step method the team named WONDER (Who, Objectives, Notice, Dialogue, Evidence, Record), whose last step feeds back into the first like a DBTL cycle, and which is rooted in Catalan educacio del lleure (leisure education) practice that several team members came from through scouting. Audiences were plotted on two axes, age and prior synbio interest, and the session language (Catalan, Spanish or English) was chosen from the barrier they wanted to lower. Each session required a before-and-after measure, at least one artefact participants made themselves, a rubric completed within 24 hours, and a written summary within a day covering what actually happened and what they would change, published on the wiki so other teams can run the session without them. Activities spanned a MiniGEM where pre-university students designed their own synthetic biology projects, an escape room at CosmoCaixa, La Nit de la Recerca, the UPF Summer School, a role-play at the PRBB, SynBYSS, Centre Civic Guinardo, scout groups (Cau La Floresta) and several schools, plus a round table with iGEM UB.","ihp":"Human practices is written as three acts with a stated principle of intentionality: every design choice should have a reason statable to an outsider, and where there is none they say so. 32 people outside the team across 8 countries, 52 documented exchanges and 103 survey respondents, with 9 of 10 project phases opened to outside input. The first grower they met corrected their founding premise, that drought was killing Catalan vineyards, by explaining that summer water stress is necessary for proper ripening and the real damage is to next season's crop; a second grower said vines did die in the first drought year, and the team kept both rather than reconciling them. A gamma-PGA geneticist in Pavia shrank the central claim from bulk soil water retention to a local hydration shell at the root. A Bordeaux researcher set a condition the design cannot meet (no deficit at flowering, moderate deficit later) and the team publishes it as a limit, not a feature. Two further conventions are worth copying: advice they chose not to follow stays on the page with the reason, and disagreements they could not resolve are printed unresolved. They also disclose that one advisor, Dr. Carolina Elizondo, holds an iGEM role, so readers can weigh her input.","realWorld":"The entrepreneurship page is explicit that the current value is knowledge, education and visibility rather than product sales, with the project at contained laboratory proof of concept and no environmental release. More than 50 organisations were contacted since June 2026; wineries Clos Montblanc (DO Conca de Barberà), Sumarroca and Vallformosa (DO Penedès) were interviewed about how a new input would fit existing practice, with Vallformosa noting that acting at root level could mean lower adoption friction. IRTA, the Catalan government's agrifood research institute, reviewed the approach against its own Penedès soil-and-water initiative and provided winery contacts. The regulatory finding was that certification, not GMO status, is the binding constraint, and the business work reached a cost per hectare and a route to a field trial. Scale context: 52,000 ha of Catalan vineyard, 8,500 viticulturists, EUR 3.27 bn annual turnover, reservoirs at 14.3% in 2024.","partners":["IRTA (Institut de Recerca i Tecnologia Agroalimentàries)","Celler Clos Montblanc","Sumarroca","Vallformosa","Ajuntament de Barcelona (Departament de Ciència i Innovació)","CosmoCaixa","PRBB","iGEM UB","Anthropic (sponsor)","University of Pavia (gamma-PGA genetics)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6078/wiki/dry-lab/software/figure6.avif","caption":"Screenshot of the RK4 solver editor with the biocontainment toxin-antitoxin circuit built from linked blocks.","credit":"Team UPF-Barcelona, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6078/wiki/dry-lab/software/figure7.avif","caption":"RK4 solver output: internal resveratrol, mazF and FEF levels over time under sinusoidal resveratrol production.","credit":"Team UPF-Barcelona, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6078/wiki/dry-lab/software/figure4.avif","caption":"The first hand-drawn sketches of the RK4 solver blocks, designed in the style of electronic circuit diagrams.","credit":"Team UPF-Barcelona, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Validate a simulator against the established tool in the field and publish the discrepancy table, including the one that looked like a 98% error and turned out to be a labelling difference, because that table is what makes a student-built solver trustworthy. Their AI work is the sharper lesson for a research centre: build the agent plugin, then actually A/B evaluate it with planted-flaw cases and an LLM judge, and be willing to publish that it did not beat the base model and that you therefore did not use it.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/igem-forge","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/dry-tips","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/upf-barcelona/description","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/upf-barcelona/upf-software"],"rank":26},{"team":"BOKU-Vienna","id":"6090","section":"Overgrad","country":"Austria","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/","projectTitle":"Algaeneering","tagline":"Engineering the microalga Chlamydomonas reinhardtii into a vitamin C and folate factory to fight hidden hunger","summary":"BOKU-Vienna overexpressed GGP (vitamin C) and GTPCHI plus ADCS (folate) in Chlamydomonas reinhardtii using Golden Gate Level 0/1/2 assembly, reaching a 4.5x increase in vitamin C in the GGP strain. The project was framed around hidden hunger, which affects an estimated two billion people, and was linked to UN SDGs 2, 3 and 12. Its education practice was built on an explicit didactic framework (the Didactic Triangle) and spanned four goals: sparking curiosity, classroom science, public dialogue and educational media. A genome-scale metabolic model was coupled to a calibrated bioreactor model and a nutritional impact model to translate lab titres into how many people a production plant could feed.","awards":["Best Education — Nominee (Overgrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Genome-scale metabolic modelling of C. reinhardtii (iCre1355, mixotrophic version) with flux balance analysis (FBA), flux variability analysis (FVA), in-silico knockouts and enzyme protein-cost ranking; dynamic FBA bioreactor model calibrated and validated against the team's own flask cultures; a nutritional impact model converting measured vitamin content into daily requirements and people supplied.","dataviz":"Interactive 'Try the trade-off' widget on the wiki: sliders let a visitor pick a vitamin and see (left) maximum vitamin flux versus growth rate from the genome-scale model and (right) the calibrated flask culture's biomass, ammonium, acetate and vitamin titre over up to 120 h, with the team's own measured points overlaid and a harvest-point marker. Each graph carries an explicit 'How to read this graph' panel and a parameter-provenance table.","datascience":"Lab-to-model feedback loop: flask measurements (biomass, ammonium, acetate, vitamin titre) were used to calibrate model parameters, and the model's pre-registered 1-10x overexpression band was compared against the measured 4.5x vitamin C result. Known model gaps were stated openly (measured ammonium ~2 mM below model; no cell-death term).","software":"Custom interactive children's learning platform (web app, guided by an algae mascot, virtual-microscope zoom from cell to molecule, 3D DNA double helix with VR headset support, base-pairing puzzle game) and a separate 13-module self-paced interactive learning platform with mini-games (lac operon switch, PCR exponential-growth slider, mRNA translation, Cas9 cut-site finder), instant feedback, no sign-up, and a teacher preview mode that unlocks all modules for classroom prep.","stack":["genome-scale metabolic model iCre1355","flux balance analysis","flux variability analysis","dynamic FBA","web-based interactive learning platform","WebVR / 3D DNA model","Kahoot","Mentimeter / QR-code live surveys"],"techniques":["Golden Gate Assembly (Level 0 to Level 2)","C. reinhardtii transformation (UVM4)","vitamin C and folate assays","microscopy","protein-cost analysis of enzyme targets","bioreactor scale-up scenario modelling"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"BOKU-Vienna ran a four-pillar education strategy explicitly grounded in the Didactic Triangle (learner-educator-content), and designed every child-facing print and digital resource in the dyslexia-friendly ABeeZee font. Pillar 1 (sparking curiosity) included a three-day Children's University (KinderUNI) booth built as a six-station progressive learning path: project poster, live Chlorella tubes and Chlamydomonas microscope images, a handcrafted 3D algae organelle model, a custom web learning platform plus a VR headset for exploring a 3D DNA double helix, an interactive genetic-engineering poster, and a gamified 'plasmid toss' into a large algae model to represent transformation; gummy-bear DNA building ran alongside. The same station set was then carried to Karwan Haus, a Caritas Wien refugee shelter in Vienna, on 17 August 2026 for children aged 4-12, with the flyer translated into four languages, Arabic- and Russian-speaking team members explaining stations, illustration-heavy materials, child-sized lab coats and goggles lent by KinderUNI BOKU, Lego-brick Golden Gate Cloning, stamps and science badges; in the exit survey most children chose 'I would love to work in science in the future'. Pillar 2 put a three-week, four-visit curriculum into two primary schools (Ruckergasse in Vienna and Neuarzl in Tyrol) with strawberry DNA extraction, lab-safety and pipetting sessions, a construct-assembly exercise for the GGP enzyme, vitamin C detection, Kahoot recaps and a field trip to the ZOOM Children's Museum where pupils wrote, drew and voiced two stop-motion films about algae and genetic modification; a middle-school workshop ('Code of Life - How We Program Cells') added a red-cabbage anthocyanin biosensor experiment, and a high-school visit to BG/BRG Tulln had students identify C. reinhardtii against Spirulina and Chlorella under the microscope. Measured impact: in the primary-school pre/post evaluation, zero children named science as a desired profession at the start versus five at the end, and 75.0% said they had become curious about programming cells (16.7% wanted to learn more first, 8.3% said they definitely want to do it in future). Pillar 3 (public dialogue) comprised a booth at Austria's Long Night of Research (24 April 2026) with a live visitor survey board on GMO acceptance and awareness that over 5 billion people face micronutrient deficiency, the Muthgassen Summer Festival, a 'Future of Food' panel discussion at TU Wien's Boecklsaal on 3 October 2026 with about 60 attendees and four panelists spanning algae cosmetics (Rita Mota), UNICEF Austria malnutrition work (Michael Blauensteiner), algae production engineering (Lukas Neuwirth, ex-Ecoduna) and microalgae research (Johannes Stadlmann), run in three blocks with anonymous QR-code audience polling and spirulina cotton candy, plus a pub quiz for about thirty STEM and non-STEM students. Pillar 4 produced durable media: an illustrated German children's book 'Welche Sprache spricht das Leben?' co-developed with KinderBOKU (characters such as 'Grandma Polymerase' who knits DNA together), launched at Vienna's Pappenheimgasse public library for a 4th-grade and a 5th-grade class with a copy donated to the library's lending collection and a copy given to every child; a comic advised by Martina Froehlich of KinderBOKU; the German-language podcast 'AlgaeHearing' on Spotify, deliberately in German because accessible German science content is scarce; the 13-module interactive learning platform with a teacher preview mode; and a feature article in the German-language life-science magazine BioSpektrum (issue 32, 4 June 2026). Critically, all tested worksheets, workshop guides and experiment protocols were published as downloadable Open Educational Resources on eduvidual.at, the official learning platform of the Austrian Federal Ministry of Education, Science and Research, so Austrian teachers nationwide can reuse them. Named-attribution testimonials were collected from the teacher at Ruckergasse, the head of the Pappenheimgasse library, the head of KinderBOKU and a social worker at Karwan Haus.","ihp":"Human Practices was structured into three sequential phases, each with its own stakeholder set: (1) lab experts for technical feasibility, which changed key design decisions; (2) outside critics on GMOs, sustainability and ethics, where the team explicitly sought disconfirming rather than validating views; (3) nutrition and product-design conversations about who should be prioritised as a target group and how the product would be delivered. The team framed iHP against the reflective/responsible/responsive triad, mapped the project to SDGs 2, 3 and 12, and used the 'Future of Food' panel plus audience polling as a live iHP instrument. The panel's main takeaway was recorded honestly as 'our work is meaningful, but GMO-based projects are not without risk and open questions, especially when implemented at a larger scale'.","realWorld":"The impact model translated measured vitamin content into concrete figures: 25 g dry algae per day covers one daily folate requirement and 116 g covers vitamin C; a hypothetical 100 m3 production plant at wild-type folate content could cover the yearly folate need of roughly 900 adults or 490 pregnant women. The design reactor scenario produced 0.46 g/L/day, nitrogen-capped, at about 1.5x the flask vitamin titre. The team also published a separate 'iGEM guide' page for teams starting out.","partners":["KinderBOKU / KinderUNI BOKU (BOKU University children's science programme)","Caritas Wien - Karwan Haus refugee shelter","ZOOM Children's Museum, Vienna","Pappenheimgasse public library, Vienna","Ruckergasse Primary School, Vienna","Neuarzl Primary School, Tyrol","BG/BRG 21 bilingual middle school","BG/BRG Tulln high school","TU Wien (Boecklsaal venue)","eduvidual.at - Austrian Federal Ministry of Education, Science and Research learning platform","BioSpektrum magazine","UNICEF Austria (panelist)","Stroeck (sponsor, panel catering)","Polymun"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/kinderuni/kinderuni-02.avif","caption":"Children at the Children's University (KinderUNI) six-station synthetic biology booth in Vienna","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/karwan-haus/karwan-haus-golden-gate-assembly-via-lego-bricks.avif","caption":"Teaching Golden Gate assembly with Lego bricks at Karwan Haus, a Caritas Wien refugee shelter","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/childrensbook/childrensbook-front.avif","caption":"The team's illustrated German children's book, co-developed with KinderBOKU and donated to a Vienna public library","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/lernplattform/1-overview-of-our-interactive-learning-platform-with-13-modules.avif","caption":"The free 13-module interactive learning platform, with no sign-up and a teacher preview mode","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/panel-discussion/panel-discussion-people.avif","caption":"The 'Future of Food' public panel discussion at TU Wien, about 60 attendees with live QR-code polling","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6090/wiki/hp-and-education/primary-school/primary-school-02.avif","caption":"Pupils extracting DNA during the three-week primary-school curriculum","credit":"Team BOKU-Vienna, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Publish every tested worksheet, workshop guide and experiment protocol as open educational resources on the national ministry education platform (as BOKU did on eduvidual.at), so school use continues after the project ends, and measure the same cohort before and after (career aspirations plus curiosity items) so learning gain is a number, not an anecdote.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/","https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/inclusivity","https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/boku-vienna/model"],"rank":7},{"team":"XJTLU-China","id":"6096","section":"Undergrad","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/","projectTitle":"dogmaOS — programming translation rhythm for soluble protein expression","tagline":"Design where and for how long the ribosome should pause, then re-engineer the host tRNA pool to make that pause possible.","summary":"XJTLU-China asked whether soluble yield of hard-to-express recombinant proteins in E. coli can be improved by deliberately programming *where* translation slows down, rather than by maximising expression. Their dry-lab system catmaOS (Codon-Aware Translation Modeling and Analysis Operating System) chains four models: structural co-translational folding priority (M1), evolutionary conserved slow-codon evidence (M2), a five-layer translation-kinetics and ribosome-traffic engine with constrained Pareto selection (M3-A to M3-E), and host tRNA-pool intervention design (M4). The framework picked a single synonymous G-CSF design (W1-S) out of six candidates and an engineered BL21-AlaPro chassis, both of which went to the bench; Four-Leaf Clover RT-qPCR then measured the actual tRNA knockdown and that number was fed back into M3-A as an updated host context. It won Best Model (Undergraduate) and Best Education (Undergraduate).","awards":["Best Measurement — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Model — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Education — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"catmaOS is a four-model, five-layer multiscale pipeline and is unusually explicit about what each layer is and is not. M1 is a structural-bioinformatics scoring model: it builds a residue-level evidence table from PDB coordinates (G-CSF, PDB 1RHG chain B, UniProt P09919, RefSeq NM_000759.4), runs DSSP for secondary structure and relative solvent accessibility, solves a self-consistent native-contact occupancy model as chain length grows (Jacobs-Shakhnovich) to detect folding events at a 1 kBT free-energy-drop threshold with a 4 Angstrom contact cutoff, adds Kyte-Doolittle hydrophobic exposure, cysteine-neighbourhood scores, DSSP transition and beta-initiation tracks and an AGGRESCAN-style aggregation propensity, then fuses them with availability-aware weights into a 0-100 priority score and shifts it downstream by a 35-codon ribosome exit-tunnel offset (scanned 30-40 codons for sensitivity). M2 is a comparative-genomics model: MAFFT L-INS-i protein alignment projected back to codons with PAL2NAL, GtRNAdb/tRNAscan-SE high-confidence tRNA gene copy numbers per species, a frozen codon-anticodon wobble-penalty table, a tRNA-adaptation-style effective supply W_s(c) converted to within-species relative 'resistance', a top-25%-resistance slow-codon label, a strict same-amino-acid filter, and Henikoff position-based sequence weighting to produce a weighted slow-codon conservation score with an explicit effective-coverage track. M3 is the kinetic core: M3-A decomposes intrinsic codon dwell into decoding (inverse effective cognate/wobble supply with a near-cognate competition term, K_dec = 0.80, gamma_near = 0.50), peptidyl-transfer (proline/EF-P arrest context), basal translocation and ViennaRNA RNAplfold local-unpaired-probability RNA resistance, with four ablation configurations A0-A3 to attribute mechanism; M3-B feeds that rate vector into TWO independent ribosome-traffic backends — a deterministic Ribosome Flow Model with Extended Objects (RFMEO) steady state and a stochastic Gillespie kinetic Monte Carlo extended-object TASEP (5 replicates, SEM bands, warm-up discarded) — and cross-validates them with Pearson, Spearman, concordance and top-k hotspot overlap plus an alpha-beta boundary-sensitivity grid; M3-C maps traffic-safe dwell envelopes, exit-tunnel availability gates, relative contact-order folding demand, a finite-state productive-pathway propagator p_{L+1} = p_L exp(s Q_L dt_L) run over a 0.5/1/2 scale grid (direction called 'robust' only when the sign holds across scales), and a time-weighted unsatisfied-native-contact exposure proxy E_U = sum_L U(L) tau(L); M3-D closes a first-order steady-state mRNA balance M_ss = k_tx / (k_deg,eff + mu) with a scikit-learn mRNA-decay predictor trained on 5,486 curated E. coli half-life records (Chen 2015, Selinger 2003) split gene-grouped 3,883/765/838; M3-E generates synonymous candidates under reading-frame, GC, motif, restriction-site and homopolymer constraints and selects by constrained Pareto dominance over four frozen objectives (maximise terminal flux J, minimise ribosome load R, maximise productive-pathway C4, minimise exposure C5), with exact enumeration for small spaces and NSGA-II for large ones, scored for precision/recall against exact fronts. M4 extends design to the host: it maps target codons to BL21 tRNA genes, clusters and promoters with a weighted K-12-to-BL21 transfer score (S_map = 0.45 S_signature + 0.20 S_ordered + 0.15 S_count + 0.15 I_80 + 0.05 I_200), proposes copy-number reduction or 1-3 substitutions in a 29-bp promoter core against 15/30/50% weakening tiers, and ranks them with a three-model ML ensemble trained on 112,955 measured core promoters from the published MBRS L4 library (Zhou 2025), held out by -35/-10 motif pairs (67,978/7,680/37,297) achieving r = 0.677, MAE = 0.506 on log2(1+strength).","dataviz":"Eighteen numbered model figures that read as a single argument. Aligned multi-track profiles put six normalised structural feature tracks (F1-F6) above the availability-aware composite score so a reader can see which feature drove a peak; priority profiles are paired with an evidence-coverage track underneath so a high score with thin evidence cannot be misread as a finding. Figure 8 is a tunnel-length sensitivity heatmap (rows = assumed exit-tunnel length 30-40 codons, columns = codon position, fixed 0-100 teal scale, light-grey cells for genuinely unavailable data rather than zero) so the reader can watch high-priority bands migrate as an assumption changes. Figure 11 is a codon-level audit matrix (18 homolog rows x codons 110-121, green = slow in that species' tRNA context, white = comparable, grey = non-comparable). Figure 14 is a log2(candidate/WT) dwell heatmap restricted to edited positions only, with the x-axis explicitly flagged as non-continuous and the legend explaining that +1 means 2x. Figure 16 is a three-panel dot plot of WT-normalised flux, ribosome load and exposure on matched candidate rows, deliberately NOT collapsed into one score. KMC traces carry +/- SEM bands and separate unblocked residence from blocked waiting. Captions state the direction of preference and whether a quantity is measured or a relative model proxy.","datascience":"Two supervised models were trained from public measurement data and one from their own: an mRNA degradation proxy (D2) on 5,486 curated E. coli half-life records with gene/protein/CDS-grouped splits, a 29-bp promoter-strength ensemble on 112,955 measured core promoters held out by -35/-10 motif pairs (a genuinely out-of-distribution split, r = 0.677), and a codon-occupancy regression on 107,627 codon-level Ribo-seq observations across 125 genes with a documented 14-nt P-site offset, first/last 10 codons excluded, and gene-held-out evaluation (22,025 test observations). The team audits its own provenance in public: several dataset source fields are annotated 'unconfirmed', 'remains to be confirmed' or 'not an attributed published dataset size' rather than quietly cited. Model 4's proL promoter screen predicted 0.727 (native) vs 0.386/0.366/0.358 (P1-P3); the built strain BL21-AlaPro was then measured by Four-Leaf Clover RT-qPCR, showing rpoD-normalised mature tRNA falling from 63.98 to 23.42 (alaXW) and 6.828 to 2.254 (proL), and those measured numbers were returned to M3-A as a new effective-supply table for a full A-E rerun. The headline wet-lab readout: TolB-G-CSF model-designed sequence in the engineered host reached a mean soluble/insoluble ratio of 0.140 vs 0.075 for the commercial sequence in WT host (DnaK-normalised, n = 3 biological replicates, ~1.87-fold). Crucially the team also reports the negative result honestly — for PfCHT1 and leptin, insoluble bands weakened but no clear soluble enrichment appeared, and the results figure is literally titled 'Solubility Gains Remain Unclear'.","software":"catmaOS 4.02.06 ships as a downloadable Windows x64 source release with Setup.cmd/Start.cmd, a pinned requirements-lock.txt, a private .venv, a local browser workspace on 127.0.0.1:8767 (sequences never leave the user's machine), a 28-page PDF user manual, and four precomputed read-only interactive demos embedded in the wiki with JSON/CSV export. Every run emits a reproducibility bundle: request.json (typed input, label, source, parameters), result.json on a versioned schema 1.0.0 with input hash, module status and reproducibility record, curves.csv, metrics.csv, exact_candidates.csv, native/evidence-result.json and run.log, all zipped per run_id. The design rule stated on the page — 'missing values stay missing' and 'a completed run confirms the requested task finished; it does not imply every model was executed' — is the discipline most teams lack.","stack":["Python 3.14","NumPy","SciPy","pandas","Biopython","scikit-learn","Matplotlib","DSSP","MAFFT L-INS-i","PAL2NAL","ViennaRNA / RNAplfold","AGGRESCAN","GtRNAdb / tRNAscan-SE","NSGA-II","custom RFMEO solver","custom Gillespie KMC"],"techniques":["co-translational folding free-energy model","structural bioinformatics / DSSP feature fusion","exit-tunnel coordinate mapping with sensitivity scan","comparative genomics codon alignment","tRNA adaptation index-style supply model","Henikoff sequence weighting","ODE/steady-state ribosome flow model (RFMEO)","stochastic Gillespie kinetic Monte Carlo TASEP with extended objects","finite-state Markov folding-pathway propagation","steady-state mRNA mass balance","multi-objective constrained Pareto optimisation","NSGA-II genetic algorithm","machine-learning regression (promoter strength, mRNA decay, codon occupancy)","out-of-distribution held-out validation","ablation studies","backend cross-validation","parameter-scenario robustness analysis","model-to-measurement feedback loop"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Best Education winner (Undergraduate). The team built an explicit education framework rather than a list of events: a four-tier 'Lifelong Learning Pathway' (interest inspiration, knowledge building, interdisciplinary co-creation, professional impact) that replaces age-banding with depth of engagement, crossed with a four-stage activity structure (Reach, Construct, Dialogue, Extension) and Gardner's theory of multiple intelligences as the design lens for choosing an activity format. They reached over 2,000 people through 21 activities — workshops, exhibitions and the Learning Mall Premium online platform — and produced 18 reusable education toolkits, with the Extension stage deliberately designed so each activity leaves behind a game, handbook or toolkit rather than ending at the event.","ihp":"A CARE framework distilled from stakeholder conversations: Create Accessibility, Accountable Storytelling, Real-world Alignment, Ethical Design. It originated in a question from Dr Lanlan Han about who actually benefits and how strong the evidence is. The interview timeline is structured as Problem / Design / Scope, and the team documents how it narrowed its own claim: starting from 'boost heterologous protein expression in E. coli', literature and interviews with Dr Antony Kam and industry expert Dr Xijun Piao (CATUG Biotechnology, Suzhou) pushed them to redefine success as soluble usable protein rather than total expression, and to state explicitly that dogmaOS is 'not positioned as a universal protein-production platform'. Mid-stage consultations with Dr Yongtao Zhu, Dr Chun Chan, Prof Sheng Yang (Shanghai Institutes for Biological Sciences, CAS), Prof Zhiliang Lu, Dr Avinash Pandreka (Oxford Suzhou Centre for Advanced Research), Dr Kozo Hamada and Prof Zhongmei Liu (Jiangnan University) convinced them that translation speed alone could not explain folding outcomes, which is what created Model 3 and Model 4.","realWorld":"Target selection was driven by real demand: G-CSF, leptin, PfCHT1 and FGF2 were chosen with researchers and protein-production companies as proteins that are hard to express in E. coli and have clear research or clinical uses spanning public health, rare disease and basic research. The team maps the project to SDGs 3, 9, 12 and 17. Accessibility is the commercial thesis — rather than selling a service, catmaOS is released as free local software so academic labs and iGEM teams with only basic E. coli facilities can do upstream optimisation instead of paying for repeated trial-and-error. Safety is built in: non-pathogenic strains, BSL-1 practice, autoclaved waste, and FLP-FRT removal of the kanamycin cassette after lambda-Red genome editing.","partners":["Xi'an Jiaotong-Liverpool University","CATUG Biotechnology (Suzhou) Co., Ltd.","Shanghai Institutes for Biological Sciences, Chinese Academy of Sciences","Oxford Suzhou Centre for Advanced Research","Jiangnan University","GenScript","SnapGene"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6096/wiki/assets/drylab/model/figure-08.svg","caption":"Figure 8: exit-tunnel sensitivity heatmap. Rows are assumed ribosome exit-tunnel lengths of 30-40 codons, columns are G-CSF codon positions, and the fixed 0-100 teal scale shows structural priority. Light-grey cells mark genuinely unavailable structural data rather than a score of zero, so the reader can watch high-priority bands migrate as a modelling assumption changes.","credit":"Team XJTLU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6096/wiki/assets/drylab/model/figure-11.svg","caption":"Figure 11: codon-level audit matrix for reference positions 110-121. Each row is a retained homolog; green marks codons called slow in that species' own tRNA context, white marks other comparable synonymous codons, grey retains non-comparable cells. Positions 115 and 121 were slow in all 18 homologs while 118 was slow in none.","credit":"Team XJTLU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6096/wiki/assets/merge-20261020/catmaos-figure-12.svg","caption":"Figure 12: the full 'how long to pause' panel, linking synonymous candidate generation to intrinsic dwell (M3-A), kinetic Monte Carlo ribosome traffic with SEM bands (M3-B), folding trajectories and contact exposure (M3-C), the mRNA decay proxy (M3-D) and the constrained objective screen (M3-E) in which four of six candidates pass the flux and ribosome-burden gates.","credit":"Team XJTLU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6096/wiki/assets/drylab/model/figure-16.svg","caption":"Figure 16: throughput, ribosome load and exposure trade-offs for wild type plus six synonymous candidates, shown as three WT-normalised panels on matched rows and deliberately not collapsed into a single score. W1-F had flux 0.781 and load 1.079, failing the flux >= 0.95 constraint; W1-S passed on all four objectives and went to the bench.","credit":"Team XJTLU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Pair every model score with an explicit evidence-coverage track and run a sensitivity scan over your least-defensible assumption (here, exit-tunnel length), so a figure shows not just the answer but how much of it is real data; and keep competing objectives as separate aligned panels rather than summing them into one invented score. Both are achievable with matplotlib by a motivated 16-year-old and immediately make a model honest.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/measurement/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/xjtlu-china/education/"],"rank":66},{"team":"ICJFLS","id":"6098","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/icjfls/","projectTitle":"ORA-GUARD","tagline":"An engineered oral probiotic that degrades the sulfur compounds causing halitosis instead of sterilising the mouth","summary":"ICJFLS engineered Streptococcus salivarius K12, a GRAS oral probiotic, to express a Sox enzyme cluster plus an MTO enzyme that catalytically degrade the volatile sulfur compounds (H2S, methanethiol, dimethyl sulfide) responsible for bad breath, positioning it as a Live Biotherapeutic Product rather than a mouthwash. Plasmid expression was abandoned after stability tests showed near-total plasmid loss within five days without selection, so the cassettes were moved into the chromosome by CRISPR-Cas9, and a four-layer biosafety system (D-alanine auxotrophy via alr knockout, a PcspA RNA-thermometer suicide switch, no antibiotic resistance markers) was added in direct response to public fear of live GMO bacteria. The entrepreneurship case is numerically specific, with per-dose cost, retail price, gross margin and a named contract manufacturer. They were nominated for Best Entrepreneurship in the High School section.","awards":["Best Entrepreneurship — Nominee (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"TAM-SAM-SOM with figures carried through to a revenue target, Business Model Canvas, three-tier business model (B2C subscription, B2B2C through dental clinics, technology licensing), cost structure and pricing with gross margin, a capability gap analysis, a 2026-2035 development roadmap, a risk register, and a comparative regulatory table for LBPs across the US, EU and China. Seven named appendices are listed, including a five-year financial projection model and a patent landscape and IP strategy.","dataviz":"Twelve entrepreneurship figures plus six data tables: TAM/SAM/SOM breakdown, business model canvas, revenue projection chart, product-format panel, roadmap, capability-gap chart and the three-jurisdiction LBP regulatory comparison. Survey results are charted as public attitudes, concerns and information needs.","datascience":"A 745-response questionnaire analysed by age, gender and region, with roughly one third of respondents having experienced halitosis, and results segmented into pain points, safety concerns and information needs. In-vitro degradation efficiency in artificial saliva was benchmarked against purified enzymes. Plasmid-stability passaging produced the five-day loss figure that drove the architecture change.","software":"","stack":["S. salivarius K12 chassis","CRISPR-Cas9 genomic integration","Sox enzyme gene cluster","MTO enzyme","PcspA RNA thermometer","artificial saliva in-vitro system"],"techniques":["chromosomal integration instead of plasmid expression","D-alanine auxotrophy by alr knockout","temperature-inducible toxin kill switch","VSC quantification in artificial saliva","plasmid retention passaging assay"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A 745-response online questionnaire spanning ages, genders and regions, surfacing the three questions the public actually asks about live engineered bacteria: will it get out of control in the body, will there be side effects, could it be transmitted to others. They then reported a finding most teams would bury, that patients care about what is in it for me and how it works rather than technical sophistication, and rewrote their communication from 'the technology is advanced' to 'it is safe and effective for you'. They also ran public education activities and attended CCIC, the Conference of China iGEMer Community.","ihp":"An explicit dialogue-reflection-iteration-redialogue loop with six documented stakeholder groups, and in each case the design consequence is named. Public fear turned a single safety mechanism into the four-layer defence system. Practising dentists and an oral microbiology researcher killed the original oral-capsule concept on the grounds that capsules have limited local effect in the mouth, which is what produced the mouthwash, lozenge, chewing gum and toothpaste format set and raised the priority on mucosal adhesion and colonisation. At CCIC, senior teams warned that gene editing in non-model organisms would be a bottleneck, so L. reuteri was adopted as a backup chassis, and they shared kill-switch leakage data that caused the team to drop a high-leakage design for the temperature-inducible one. A bioethics professor and a biosafety policy advisor are the reason plasmid expression was abandoned for CRISPR-Cas9 chromosomal integration and why genome-stability and animal toxicology studies were pre-planned.","realWorld":"This is a genuine business case with real numbers, and it is the unit economics that separate it from the typical canvas exercise. They state a production cost of roughly RMB 0.5 per dose for freeze-dried bacterial powder plus RMB 0.3 for packaging and filling, totalling about RMB 0.8 per dose falling to RMB 0.3-0.5 at volume; retail pricing of RMB 199 for a 30-dose mouthwash bottle and RMB 159 for a 30-tablet box; and a resulting gross margin of 70-80 percent benchmarked against high-end probiotics. The SOM is built bottom-up rather than taken as a share of the TAM: 100,000 halitosis sufferers at an average RMB 200 per month giving roughly RMB 20 million (about USD 2.8 million) in year one or two, through Tmall, JD.com, Douyin and dental-clinic channels. Market research is partly real and partly desk work: the 745-response questionnaire and dentist interviews are theirs, while the USD 13.44 billion global halitosis market and the RMB 77.9 billion China oral-care figure come from Insight Partners, Frost and Sullivan and similar reports. The competitive analysis is specific and fair, naming BLIS Technologies' BLIS K12 and correctly observing that its deodorising mechanism is indirect because it works through salivaricin bacteriocins, whereas ORA-GUARD catalytically destroys the VSC molecules. The regulatory analysis is the strongest part and is genuinely credible: they identify that NMPA's 2020 biological-products registration classification treats LBPs as therapeutic biological products containing microorganisms, note honestly that neither NMPA nor the FDA has yet issued complete LBP-specific technical guidance, and draw the correct strategic conclusion that this is both policy risk and a chance to help set the standard, with early regulator engagement as the stated tactic. Partners are named rather than implied: Shandong Bona Technology Group for probiotic fermentation and freeze-drying, with industrialisation discussions already begun, and Shijia Doctor Dental Hospital as a clinical and channel partner. Be blunt about the gaps, though. There is no registered company, no incubator place, no grant and no investor commitment; investment partners are described only as a category ('biotechnology VCs and oral care industry funds'). The IP position is weaker than it first reads: the strain construction strategy and genetic circuits are said to form technical barriers that are 'planned to be protected through patent applications', so nothing is filed. The revenue projection figure is labelled as internal estimates, and the appendices (five-year financial model, patent landscape) are listed as titles rather than linked documents, so the deepest financial work is asserted rather than shown. Continuation is a licensing-and-partnering intent rather than a funded plan, and an LBP approval route of that length will outlast any student team unless the Bona relationship becomes a formal transfer.","partners":["Shandong Bona Technology Group Co., Ltd. (probiotic fermentation, freeze-drying, technology transfer and industrialisation discussions)","Shijia Doctor Dental Hospital and dental clinics (clinical trials and channel)","Practising dentists and an oral microbiology researcher (dosage form and colonisation advice)","A bioethics professor and a biosafety policy advisor","CCIC (Conference of China iGEMer Community) experts and past award-winning teams","University synthetic biology laboratories"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6098/wiki/entrepre/fig-3-entrepre.avif","caption":"TAM, SAM and SOM analysis for ORA-GUARD, with the SOM built bottom-up from 100,000 target users at RMB 200 per month","credit":"Team ICJFLS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6098/wiki/entrepre/fig-11-entrepre.avif","caption":"Comparative overview of Live Biotherapeutic Product regulatory pathways in the US, EU and China","credit":"Team ICJFLS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6098/wiki/entrepre/fig-10-entrepre10.avif","caption":"ORA-GUARD development roadmap from 2026 proof of concept to 2035","credit":"Team ICJFLS, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6098/wiki/entrepre/fig-12-entrepre.avif","caption":"Gap analysis between current team capabilities and the capabilities needed to commercialise ORA-GUARD","credit":"Team ICJFLS, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Insist on three numbers before any market slide: cost per dose, price per unit and resulting gross margin, built from the actual process (freeze-drying plus fill-and-finish) rather than from a market-report percentage. And copy their SOM discipline, where the obtainable market is users times spend per user per month, not a fraction of a global TAM.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/icjfls/","https://2026.igem.wiki/icjfls/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/icjfls/human-practices/"],"rank":7},{"team":"BNDS-China","id":"6115","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/bnds-china/","projectTitle":"OrbitColi","tagline":"An AND-logic-gate engineered probiotic that senses oxidative stress in space and produces vitamin B2 and trehalose to protect astronauts","summary":"BNDS-China built OrbitColi, an engineered E. coli probiotic designed to sense oxidative conditions arising from cosmic radiation and microgravity and respond by producing vitamin B2 and trehalose, replacing fixed-dose supplements that degrade over multi-year missions. The scientific framing is quantitative: ISS crews receive 100-200 mSv per year against a 20 mSv occupational limit, and a three-year Mars round trip may accumulate 300-450 mSv. The team's distinguishing contribution is methodological: they invented and applied a five-step education framework, R.E.A.C.H. (Recognize, Experience, Apply, Communicate, Harvest), to every single outreach activity, with a pre-event diagnostic survey and a matched post-event survey, and they report their own failures against it. Integrated Human Practices used a parallel STAR framework (Scan, Test, Assemble, Reflect) across five cycles.","awards":["Best Education — Nominee (High School)","Best Wiki — Nominee (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Two self-designed frameworks, both documented and reusable: R.E.A.C.H. for education (Recognize the audience's pre-existing attitudes before teaching; Experience through interactive formats; Apply by moving the audience from listeners to designers; Communicate to close the loop by harvesting informed project feedback into Integrated Human Practices; Harvest by quantifying the event's success and planning the next iteration) and STAR for iHP (Scan, Test, Assemble, Reflect) across five cycles: problem validation, end-user and market needs, technical design, biosafety and ethics, and production and final-product development. The REACH structure is published in detailed appendices so other teams can reuse it.","dataviz":"Before-and-after bar charts of self-reported technique familiarity and interest; questionnaire-result figures published for each activity (pre and post); a timeline figure of all educational activities; pre-interview and final stakeholder influence/relevance maps for iHP, with the honest caveat printed on the figure that inclusion in the pre-interview map does not mean an interview took place.","datascience":"Systematic paired pre/post surveying with reported N and percentages, e.g. kindergarten cohort N = 14 matched responses, 21 student athletes aged 10-13 surveyed before and after, 16 valid responses per question from the elderly session, eight older adults aged 68-82 interviewed at the scoping stage, and two school-wide perception surveys on synthetic biology and on attitudes to engineered-bacteria consumption and space microbial applications, all published as downloadable files.","software":"The project includes hardware and software components presented to audiences for feedback; the team learned an AI protein-design workflow from team NBJF (generate structure, fill amino-acid sequence, in-silico screening, wet-lab validation) and explicitly reserved wet-lab capacity because high in-silico scores can lose function in reality.","stack":["R.E.A.C.H. education framework","STAR iHP framework","paired pre/post questionnaires","downloadable lesson slide decks and protocols","AND logic-gate genetic circuit design"],"techniques":["oxidative-stress-responsive promoter sensing","AND logic gate circuit","vitamin B2 and trehalose biosynthesis","protein thermal and radiation resistance engineering","strawberry DNA extraction","fluorescent E. coli painting"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"BNDS-China's practice is distinctive because every activity is written up against the same five-step template, R.E.A.C.H., which forces a pre-event diagnostic, an interactive delivery, a design task, a project-feedback loop and a quantified post-event evaluation - and the team reports honestly where a step was skipped. The highest-value example is a kindergarten programme for pre-literate 5-6 year olds. The Recognize step established through a pre-event survey that children already saw microbes as invisible enemies (hand-washing framing) and that 71.43% preferred interactive games to lectures. The team then rendered BioBrick standard assembly as a colour-coded tactile block system: a blue 'switch' promoter block that must come first, a red 'function' coding-sequence block compared to a light bulb, a yellow 'stop' terminator block compared to a vehicle brake, and a green 'little house' vector and chassis block, with assembly order taught as a chanted mantra ('Flip open the blue switch, let the red function bloom, halt with the yellow brake, and live inside the green room!'). Children worked in pairs and trios called 'syndicates' with visual challenge cards posing local problems (make plants glow to light a path to a playground at night; make a flower that masks the smell of garbage). The cleverest design move: the team deliberately rationed parts, giving one table switches but no coding blocks and the next table the reverse, so children had to discover what they lacked, approach other groups and barter - a lived introduction to the open-source iGEM Registry. A five-act puppet show, 'Microscopic Defense Line', dramatised a grazed arm at cell scale, and deliberately avoided weapons or war imagery: the White Blood Cell puppet hugged the bacterium to represent phagocytosis, two red blood cell actors held hands to bridge a vessel, and platelet actors pressed yellow paper against stretchy cloth to show clotting. Measured results from matched surveys (N = 14): before, 78.57% did not know what it meant to pass a function from one organism to another and 64.29% did not know or only vaguely knew what a gene is; after, 92.86% described synthetic biology as 'building blocks', 92.86% knew the promoter starts expression and 100% knew the coding sequence determines function; 42.86% had thought the body fights invaders like 'little soldiers' and none knew clots form, versus 100% afterwards understanding both white blood cell clearance and platelet repair; 100% enjoyed the activities and 92.86% wanted more science stories. The feedback even changed the project: children asked why immune clearance has to kill ('Can't they just change them? Make them friends?') and worried what happens if the yellow stop block falls off, which the team mapped onto transcriptional runaway, biocontainment and biocontamination and used to question their own antagonistic-clearance therapeutic model. Other formats, each with REACH write-ups: a BioCamp lecture on the whole molecular toolkit (PCR, gel electrophoresis, Southern/Northern/Western blotting, qPCR, rtPCR, FISH, CRISPR-Cas9, gene circuits) where each technique was framed by the question it answers, with the team candidly recording that the Apply and Communicate steps did not happen and that students still found the techniques complicated without hands-on work, and publishing the fix (fewer techniques per session, add pipetting and gel loading, one workflow diagram); a formal debate on 9 March 2026 with the BNDS British Debate Club on whether synthetic biology should modify human traits unrelated to serious disease, supported by a purpose-written Synthetic Bio Debate Information Packet covering definitions, design principles, systems thinking, intervention targets, risks, ethics and governance, with pre- and post-debate questionnaires; a campus art exhibition on environmental protection with a pop-art digital painting and an explanatory signboard, made with a visual-arts student, where the recorded criticism was that the symbols were too abstract; a bilingual comic published in the school newspaper BNDS Review and released as downloadable Chinese and English digital versions; and merchandise-as-conversation-starter at the Hongchuanghui Fair and school carnival, with original E. coli, yeast and budding-yeast cartoon designs on postcards, stickers and acrylic hair clips, where the team explicitly explained which features were biological and which were artistic licence. The inclusivity work is specific about barriers. At Tianyun Rehabilitation Center in Liulihe, Fangshan District, Beijing, working with hearing-impaired children and children with genetic conditions, the team discovered the children had no independent electronic devices and that a conventional questionnaire did not suit their communication needs, so after consulting the centre's teachers they switched to an illustrated storybook, 'Little Chef Mimi and Her Magical Yeast Friends', plus a colouring activity, and relied on the teachers to interpret the children's sign language - concluding that inclusive science education is about making sure every child's voice can be heard, not just making knowledge easier. For children with osteogenesis imperfecta ('porcelain doll' patients), the team first interviewed directors of the Sun and Rain Center rare-disease organisation, who explicitly advised against treating participants as fragile or showing pity; sessions were kept short, and when the team noticed holding pens was physically hard for some children, they abandoned the planned drawing activity mid-session for an open discussion and a 'wishing' segment, then shifted their long-term approach to accessible online workshops and distributing game materials directly to hospitals. For 21 student athletes aged 10-13 with limited access to systematic biology teaching or lab equipment, three experiments (strawberry DNA extraction, saliva pH around exercise, ruler-drop reaction tests) tied biology to their own bodies; afterwards all 21 reported confidence in understanding and completing experiments and all answered the DNA and synthetic-biology questions correctly. A lecture for older adults, built on scoping interviews with eight people aged 68-82 whose concerns were medical safety, side effects and the reliability of health information, used genetically modified food, vaccines and microbial insulin as familiar anchors, taught how to spot misleading health advertising and how AI can help check claims, and included making DNA-themed bracelets and illustrated tote bags carrying health-scam reminders; of 16 post-event responses, 15 (93.75%) rejected a misleading miracle-product claim, all 16 answered the GM food, vaccine, insulin and AI-checking items as intended, 81.25% found explanations easy to understand and 87.5% called the activity very helpful.","ihp":"Five iHP cycles (problem validation, end-user and market needs, technical design, biosafety and ethics, production and final product) were each run through the team's STAR loop. A pre-interview stakeholder map linked each cycle to the expert groups whose input it needed, and a final map recorded each stakeholder's influence on the project and the project's relevance to them. The team documented a hard limit transparently: regulatory restrictions prevented them from interviewing astronauts or national regulatory authorities. Aerospace food and nutrition experts defined the nutritional context; space medicine and clinical experts shaped the health-need and biosafety assessment. Education fed directly back into design, most notably the kindergarten children's biocontainment and mutualism questions. A cross-team exchange with team NBJF on AI protein design taught the team to compartmentalise the workflow and to avoid over-reliance on in-silico prediction, and they adopted NBJF's 'phenomenon-first, jargon-later' principle for explaining their AND logic-gate probiotic to lay audiences.","realWorld":"The project targets a documented gap in astronaut health protection: pre-packaged vitamins and antioxidants provide fixed dosing that cannot track real-time biochemical change, and shelf-life degradation makes them unreliable over multi-year missions. Education outputs were deliberately packaged for reuse: an openly available children's book, downloadable comic in Chinese and English, the debate information packet, lecture slide decks, bilingual activity PowerPoints, the strawberry DNA extraction protocol, and detailed REACH appendices so other teams and educators can replicate the structure rather than just the activities.","partners":["BNDS British Debate Club","Tianyun Rehabilitation Center, Liulihe, Fangshan District, Beijing (hearing-impaired and genetic-disease children)","Sun and Rain Center (rare-disease organisation, osteogenesis imperfecta families)","BNDS Review (International Department student newspaper)","iGEM team NBJF (AI protein design exchange)","Hongchuanghui Fair","local kindergarten","youth sports school / student athlete programme","senior citizens' club ('iGEM and seniors together club')"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6115/wiki/edu/image20.webp","caption":"Kindergarten children with the BioBrick 'building block' constructs they assembled in small 'syndicates'","credit":"Team BNDS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6115/wiki/edu/image22.webp","caption":"The five-act puppet show 'Microscopic Defense Line', which showed phagocytosis as a hug rather than combat","credit":"Team BNDS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6115/wiki/edu/image24.webp","caption":"Guiding children at Tianyun Rehabilitation Center through the storybook 'Little Chef Mimi and Her Magical Yeast Friends'","credit":"Team BNDS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6115/wiki/edu/image29.webp","caption":"Student athletes aged 10-13 extracting strawberry DNA during the under-resourced-children workshop","credit":"Team BNDS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6115/wiki/edu/image36.webp","caption":"Older adults showing the DNA-themed bracelets they made during the health-literacy lecture","credit":"Team BNDS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Adopt a single named template for every outreach event - BNDS-China's R.E.A.C.H. (Recognize with a pre-event diagnostic, Experience, Apply, Communicate project feedback back into design, Harvest with a matched post-event survey) - and publish the results including the steps you failed to complete; the paired pre/post numbers and the honest gaps are what turn a scrapbook of events into evidence.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/bnds-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/bnds-china/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/bnds-china/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/bnds-china/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/bnds-china/contribution"],"rank":14},{"team":"Cornell","id":"6122","section":"Undergrad","country":"United States","village":"Fashion & Cosmetics","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/","projectTitle":"Klothos","tagline":"A smart bandage: engineered probiotics inside electrospun alginate nanofibers that release an anti-fibrotic peptide only where a wound is scarring badly.","summary":"Every scar treatment on the market today starts after the scar has formed — silicone sheeting, intralesional steroid, laser and grafts are all passive or reactive, and none addresses fibrosis biology during the critical healing window. Klothos intervenes during healing instead: engineered bacteria secrete 810A, a 12-amino-acid peptide that binds the exposed C-terminal domain of CTGF (CCN2) and blocks fibroblast-to-myofibroblast activation, and expression is held behind a two-input AND gate built from the Pseudomonas syringae hrpR/hrpS/hrpL system so the peptide is only made when reactive oxygen species (via TrxC) and hypoxia (via Pfnr) are both present — the signature of unchecked remodelling. The bacteria are encapsulated in an alginate/PEO nanofiber matrix spun on a coaxial electrospinner the team designed and built themselves, because commercial instruments cost far more than an undergraduate team can reach. It won Best Hardware and Best Presentation on the strength of that open, flat-packable, interlock-protected electrospinner plus a modular 3D-printed coaxial spinneret adapter, and Best Fashion & Cosmetics on a project that is unusually complete end to end: 20-plus risk-rated commercialisation analysis, a six-phase FDA path, and a human practices programme built around people who actually live with scars.","awards":["Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Hardware — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Education — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Presentation — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Fashion & Cosmetics Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Cornell's modelling splits cleanly into wet lab and product development. On the biology side: a production-degradation ODE (dF/dt = alpha*X - delta_F*F) for SUMO-His-810A accumulation against biomass; a Hill function for Tet and future inducible promoter activity; Michaelis-Menten kinetics for SUMO protease cleavage to recover active peptide; an ELISA-based Hill-Langmuir binding isotherm for 810A against CTGF/C, fixed at n=1 because there is a single site, with an explicit note that a fitted n far from 1 would indicate error rather than cooperativity; and a quadratic ligand-depletion model for the regime where accessible CTGF and peptide concentrations are comparable and the ordinary Langmuir assumption breaks. Downstream of binding they built a reduced-order dynamic systems model of fibroblast state transition, parameterised from a protein interaction network assembled in STRING v12.5 — filtered on the Scarring Human Phenotype Ontology term HP:0100699, recovering 48 weighted edges, merged with CCN2's 20 nearest first-degree and 10 nearest second-degree neighbours, surfacing SMAD3, FN1 and TGFB2 as major nodes. Those nodes and weights were then run in Netflux as a logic-based ODE network (normalised node activity with a time constant, modified Hill activation and inhibition functions) using COL1A1 accumulation as the proxy for extracellular matrix hardening, and the simulation tracks the downstream TGF-beta inhibition reported in Xiao et al. 2020. Wound environment is modelled explicitly: ROS as a transient peak function calibrated against Carney et al. 2019 time-series qRT-PCR of SOD1, MGST1 and PRDX6 in hypertrophic scars, oxygen as an exponential recovery function, each feeding a Hill-function promoter. AlphaFold was used to render 810A and the CTGF/C-810A-6xHis complex. On the hardware side: a semi-empirical predictor of shell-solution viscosity (polymer overlap and entanglement statistics via Mark-Houwink), conductivity (mobile ion contribution) and surface tension (surfactant adsorption with a CMC/CAC plateau), the Fridrikh scaling law for terminal jet radius from flow rate, jet current and surface tension, plus continuity-based flow rate and stepper-motor plunger speed models.","dataviz":"The standout is the Unified Readiness Classifier, which collapses three separate property predictions into a single green/yellow/red call on whether a formulation should go to the electrospinner at all — green means proceed to voltage-window testing, yellow allows one short trial or minor reformulation, red means reformulate first. It is rendered as a spinning-viability contour map over sodium alginate and PEO weight percentages produced by Monte Carlo sampling against literature results, so a reader can see the spinnable region rather than read a table of numbers. The alginate molecular-weight analysis is similarly well-framed: one plot puts viscosity on the left axis spanning more than four orders of magnitude while conductivity and surface tension on the right axis stay flat, which makes the whole argument visible at a glance — conductivity caps how much alginate you can dissolve, molecular weight decides whether that limit is reachable, and the optimum is interior rather than monotonic. The STRING network is shown as a force-directed layout and the Netflux run as normalised activity curves for CTGF, TGFB1, FN1, COL1A1, SMAD2 and SMAD3 together. The wiki itself is a scroll-driven 3D island journey ('scroll to travel, click an island to explore') with an interactive AND-gate widget letting the reader switch each input signal on and off, and a WebGL model of the electrospinner.","datascience":"The classifier is the clearest case of turning data into a predictive tool: rather than measuring solutions after purchase, Cornell predicts viscosity, conductivity and surface tension from formulation inputs (alginate concentration, PEO concentration and molecular weight, surfactant type and concentration, salt or buffer content, temperature) and uses the prediction to avoid spinning runs that cannot work. They name candidate implementations — logistic regression, random forest, or Gaussian process classification, favouring the Gaussian process on a small dataset because it returns uncertainty — and define a two-level validation plan: property-level (mean absolute error for viscosity, percent error for conductivity, absolute error in mN/m for surface tension against held-out measured formulations) and outcome-level (whether predicted-green formulations actually produce stable shell-only fibres, judged on Taylor cone formation, current stability, bead formation, dry deposition and fibre diameter distribution). They say outright that the classifier matters more than exact numerical accuracy, and that literature Mark-Houwink, CMC and adsorption constants are starting estimates to be recalibrated against the team's own measurements. Fibre morphology was quantified by image analysis: tortuosity as the ratio of fibre contour length to end-to-end distance, with a >1.10 threshold defining 'curly', giving 8% curly and 92% straight. Failure diagnosis was also model-driven — droplet breakup was traced to insufficient PEO chain entanglement at 100 kDa, leading them to raise PEO from 1.5% to 2% and cut back PS80 surfactant.","software":"No standalone software release; the released artefact is the hardware. The electrospinner is published openly as a bill of materials, CAD files, circuit diagrams, assembly documentation, an operation manual and a dedicated safety manual, designed to be disassembled and flat-packed for transport by teams in shared or temporary workspaces. Control is an ESP32 DevKitC microcontroller driving an AHV24V30KVR5MAW high-voltage module (24 V in, up to 30 kV out) for digitised control in place of a potentiometer, with an access-door interlock and emergency stop that cut power and ground the high-voltage circuit. A separate contribution is the modular 3D-printed coaxial needle adapter, which keeps metal needle tips for conductivity while 3D-printing the structure, adaptable across needle gauges and core-shell flow configurations. They also publish a disulfide-bond-optimised SHuffle expression protocol (6xHis-SUMO fusion, SUMO as chaperone and clean cleavage handle) framed for reuse on other hard-to-express proteins such as insulin-like growth factors, antibody fragments and venom peptides, plus protocols for alginate preparation, crosslinking viability testing, escape testing, light microscopy ImageJ analysis and spectrographic bacterial settling.","stack":["STRING v12.5","Netflux (logic-based ODE network simulation)","AlphaFold","ImageJ","Monte Carlo sampling","Gaussian process / random forest / logistic regression classification","ESP32 DevKitC microcontroller","AHV24V30KVR5MAW 30 kV high-voltage module","CAD and 3D printing (coaxial spinneret, enclosure, extruder)","SDS-PAGE, Ni-NTA affinity chromatography, LC-MS, ELISA","16S rRNA sequencing against the NIH database"],"techniques":["Production-degradation ODE modelling","Hill function promoter induction modelling","Michaelis-Menten enzyme kinetics","Hill-Langmuir / one-site Langmuir binding isotherm fitting to ELISA dose-response","Quadratic ligand-depletion equilibrium modelling","Reduced-order signalling network modelling","Protein-protein interaction network assembly filtered by Human Phenotype Ontology term","Logic-based network simulation with modified Hill activation and inhibition","Transient-peak and exponential-recovery environmental signal models","Mark-Houwink viscosity scaling and polymer entanglement statistics","Fridrikh scaling law for terminal jet radius","Semi-empirical property prediction with a green/yellow/red readiness classifier","Fibre tortuosity quantification by image analysis","AND-gate transcriptional logic (hrpR/hrpS/hrpL)"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Cornell ran an unusually wide and genuinely iterative education programme. Plasmid Pals, their own educational game, was playtested with Big Brothers Big Sisters participants who then suggested how to make it more interactive. Plasmid bracelets plus an open-ended idea board asking visitors how they would use synthetic biology to change the world evolved into age-adapted versions for daycare, the Sciencenter and the State Fair — and when reading and spelling turned out to be barriers for roughly 20 preschoolers, the team switched to a cooking analogy (DNA as cookbook, RNA as copied recipe, protein as finished dish), writing up the lesson that activities must match developmental ability. A summer-camp session had about 80 students debate which scientific problems deserve funding using live polls, and a 30-minute lesson drew another 20-25 minutes of student-led questions about research careers and contributing without lab access. STEM Her Way was a four-session series in which high school girls learned synthetic biology and presented their own research proposals, rated 4.7/5 by 15 respondents across seven countries with every respondent recommending it. A shadowing programme drew more than 260 applications from the US and 17 countries for six high school scholars, later extended to five international students whose own project proposals the team helped assess for feasibility — a participant from Uzbekistan brought soil salinity as a community problem and the students developed Salt Guardians around it. They also ran a worldwide synthetic biology governance competition (regulatory pathways, biosafety and containment, access and equity, plus an open governance challenge), produced a narrative synthetic biology podcast, taught Lambert iGEM how to prepare stakeholder questions by sharing their own Human Practices deck and brainstorming guide, and swapped children's books with NUGEM — NUGEM translating Cornell's into Kazakh and Russian while Cornell translated NUGEM's into Mandarin, Spanish and French.","ihp":"Several interviews changed the project rather than decorating it. The clearest is hardware: advised not to build their own high-voltage power supply (especially from microwave parts) because the electrical risk is serious, the team dropped the homemade supply entirely, consulted Cornell's Afridi Lab, and bought a commercial regulated unit while sourcing other parts through Digi-Key and Mouser to hold cost down — the advice was that boosting voltage is easy but a stable regulated DC output is the hard part. Dr Insoo Hyun reframed safety itself: he introduced weighing risks and benefits against existing alternatives rather than assuming absolute safety, and warned that familiarity with a microorganism from food use does not demonstrate safety in an open wound, also pressing them to choose experimental models that resemble real wound conditions rather than the most convenient ones. Dr Joseph Fins pushed on preclinical evidence and asked whether delivering the peptide directly, without living bacteria, would be safer and simpler — which the team now carries explicitly as a documented de-risking architecture, with the trade-off (a pre-loaded matrix gives a fixed dose and loses the AND-gated release) stated rather than hidden. Dr Andrew Leask's input on CCN2/CTGF signalling shaped the target rationale, and reviewer pressure to confirm H2O2 is still detectable at the wound stage they target led them to work on sensor sensitivity and add the hypoxia AND gate for specificity. Burn survivor and former burn nurse Amy Acton and Andrea at the Phoenix Society for Burn Survivors changed how the team talks: survivors need support long after wounds heal — financial help for compression garments and procedures, emotional support, connection with others — treatments are costly, uncomfortable and variable, so do not promise how much a treatment will reduce scarring. Roughly 20 older adults and visitors at open events taught them that a scar's size or appearance does not predict its personal impact, and that open conversation surfaced concerns their structured survey questions missed; they concluded existing scales describe the scar but not what the person would want improved. Alison Bateman-House at NYU identified composition and method-of-production as their strongest patent claims and walked them through the one-year US grace period and PCT economics, which fed directly into the commercialisation plan. A kelp-farming conversation moved the sustainability case from biodegradability to sourcing, noting that North American kelp farmers' binding constraint is finding buyers rather than growing kelp since wild-harvested seaweed is substantially cheaper — a point that complicates rather than flatters their own narrative.","realWorld":"This is the most developed commercial package of the five. Target market is quantified: burn centres and academic hospitals in the American Northeast, where 2,255 medical institutions — 26 verified burn centres, 604 hospitals (31 with ACGME dermatology residencies) and 1,625 medical spas — serve an estimated 1.8 million addressable patients per year, or 2.2 patients per institution per day, with academic hospitals and verified burn centres targeted first as likeliest to trial new technology. The regulatory analysis identifies the real hazard: Klothos is a combination product (alginate nanofiber device plus engineered live bacteria), so FDA assigns a primary mode of action deciding whether CBER leads via IND/BLA or CDRH via 510(k)/PMA, and misclassification could force a restart costing an estimated 18-24 months and $500K-plus — hence a formal Pre-RFD consultation with the Office of Combination Products before committing development resources. They cite Azitra's engineered S. epidermidis with an open IND and FDA Fast Track as precedent that CBER will accept an IND for topical engineered bacteria. A six-phase plan runs from prototype and fibroblast assays, through an IND/IDE-enabling package with dermal safety, immunogenicity, biodistribution and microbial containment, to Phase I (with viable-organism monitoring because it is a live biotherapeutic) and a Phase II randomised controlled trial in surgical, burn, hypertrophic-scar-risk and revision-scar settings scored on POSAS, VSS and SCAR-Q. A freedom-to-operate analysis covers ten patents across four technology areas, with specific flags (the University of Manitoba colour-changing antibacterial nanofiber US20220047523A1 and NYCU's pending pH-sensitive electrospun bandage would need review before adding a pH indicator), plus PCT costs of roughly $17K to hold in 150 signatories and $20K per country thereafter. They map regulatory strategy across seven regions, including the MHRA consultation on modernising the GTMP definition (closed 22 June 2026) and Korea's MFDS review reforms cutting target timelines from about 420 to 295 days. Over 20 risks across technical, market, financial and regulatory categories are rated on severity, likelihood and detectability with mitigations and rendered as a heat map; a skill gap radar names what the 45-plus member team cannot yet do (pricing, physician adoption, launch strategy, clinical trial design) and how they would fill it. Reimbursement is treated honestly: novel combination products often lack an established billing code, so they model unit economics under both self-pay and reimbursed scenarios. The team won an ANSA grant and flagged Cornell's technology transfer office licensing terms as a Year 1 gating factor for any spinout.","partners":["Dr Andrew Leask (CCN2/CTGF signalling and pathological scarring)","Dr Insoo Hyun (bioethics)","Dr Joseph Fins (medical ethics)","Alison Bateman-House, NYU Grossman School of Medicine (medical ethics and IP)","Amy Acton, Phoenix Society for Burn Survivors","Lisa Placanica (prescription/outpatient model)","Afridi Lab, Cornell (high-power electronics)","Cornell technology transfer office and biosafety office / IBC","Lambert iGEM (human practices mentoring)","NUGEM (children's book translation exchange)","STEM Her Way","Big Brothers Big Sisters","Ithaca Sciencenter","Cornell Division B Science Olympiad","ANSA grant programme"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6122/wiki/modeling/productdev-14-spinning-viability-alginate-peo-classifier-map.avif","caption":"Predicted spinning viability across sodium alginate and PEO weight percentages, generated by Monte Carlo sampling over the viscosity, conductivity and surface tension models — the map behind the green/yellow/red readiness classifier that decides whether a formulation reaches the electrospinner.","credit":"Team Cornell, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6122/wiki/modeling/wetlab-17-fig4-string-ctgf-scarring-network.avif","caption":"Force-directed CTGF (CCN2)-centred protein interaction and scarring regulatory network built in STRING v12.5 by filtering on Human Phenotype Ontology term HP:0100699, with SMAD3, FN1 and TGFB2 as major nodes. Its 48 weighted edges became the parameters of the Netflux fibroblast simulation.","credit":"Team Cornell, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6122/wiki/engineering-success/hardware-extruder-reservoir-coaxial-needle-collection-plate/eng-suc-hardware-web-2-0-fig-1.avif","caption":"The custom coaxial electrospinner: extruder, reservoir, coaxial needle and collection plate, built from inexpensive stock parts and 3D-printed components and designed to flat-pack for teams working in shared or temporary spaces.","credit":"Team Cornell, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6122/wiki/hardware/microscope/gfp-coaxial.avif","caption":"Micrograph of coaxially electrospun fibres containing GFP-expressing engineered E. coli, showing that cells remain fluorescent and therefore viable after encapsulation.","credit":"Team Cornell, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Build the cheap version of the instrument and publish it properly. Cornell's winning move was deciding that an electrospinner — normally a specialised commercial purchase — could be made from stock parts and 3D prints, then releasing the bill of materials, CAD, circuit diagrams, assembly guide, operation manual and a separate high-voltage safety manual with interlocks and an emergency stop designed in. For a school-based centre the paired lesson is the readiness classifier: predict a formulation's viscosity, conductivity and surface tension from its recipe, label it green/yellow/red, and spend scarce lab time only on green — a cheap model that saves expensive experiments is worth more than an elaborate one that does not change what you do next.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/circuitry","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/extrusion-mechanism","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/encasement","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/fibers","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/wet-lab","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/measurement","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/notebook","https://2026.igem.wiki/cornell/timeline"],"rank":155},{"team":"MIT-MAHE","id":"6145","section":"Undergrad","country":"India","village":"Food & Agriculture / Manufacturing","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/","projectTitle":"MAHEK","tagline":"Sustainable cuminaldehyde from Pseudomonas putida, and NetFlux, a web tool that turns a genome-scale metabolic model into a drawable pathway and a ranked list of knockouts.","summary":"MAHEK addresses the fragrance industry's dependence on petrochemical synthesis and wasteful seed extraction by producing cuminaldehyde biologically. The chassis is Pseudomonas putida F1, a solvent-tolerant soil bacterium that already makes cuminaldehyde as an intermediate in p-cymene degradation; CRISPR-Cas9-mediated homologous recombination knocks out cymC, the p-cumic aldehyde dehydrogenase, arresting the pathway exactly at the target compound. The dry lab's deliverable is NetFlux, a React + FastAPI web application that compresses a genome-scale metabolic model into a readable 'production corridor' using a novel extraction algorithm (SCOPE) and then pools OptKnock and OptForce into one co-ranked list of knockout and regulation strategies, each traced to the gene and linked to BiGG. The team also won the Inclusivity Award for work with visually impaired learners that ended in Vision Empower adopting their tactile models for use in schools across India.","awards":["Best Software — Nominee (Undergrad)","Inclusivity Award — Winner (Undergrad)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"React + TypeScript single-page frontend split into three modules (parameter panel, pathway map viewer, interventions table) sharing one run state; FastAPI backend with Pydantic request/response schemas, auto-generated OpenAPI 3.1 documentation and a Swagger UI at /docs; a JobManager running strain design on a bounded background worker pool so heavy requests do not time out; SQLite (mfghub.db) as the model/reaction/metabolite/gene/medium catalogue, rebuilt from the GEMs on every server image build; and Docker Compose bringing up database, API and web client together. Core science runs on COBRApy.","dataviz":"Pathways are drawn as interactive metabolic maps flowing strictly left to right, with line width proportional to mass flow, metabolites as nodes carrying compartment suffixes, reactions as arrows colour-coded by their FVA annotation, and the substrate-to-target corridor explicitly highlighted. Hovering a reaction opens an inspection card with reaction name and ID, knockout-candidate status, flux and FVA range, coupling and bounds, subsystem, gene associations and gene rules, and its producers and consumers. Each click of Generate opens its parameters in a new tab so settings can be compared side by side. Results export as GraphML, JSON and CSV straight from the browser. The interventions table shows a baseline header (wild-type growth rate, yield floor and ceiling) against which every ranked design is judged, and separates committed 'Ranked Designs' from weaker single-reaction 'Hypotheses (leads)', with the wiki telling users not to compare yield numbers across the two tables.","datascience":"Benchmarked without a ground truth by defining what a useful pathway must do and measuring that instead. SCOPE was run on 9 GEMs with up to 5 target products each, 40 organism-product pairs in total: 100% of extracted pathways were fully connected from substrate to target, and the mean corridor was 55.1 reactions against a mean model size of 934, i.e. 7.8% of the network (models up to 1,762 reactions reduced to about 55). Essential reactions were identified independently with FVA and then checked for recall: 20.3 essential reactions per product on average, 17.5 retained, overall recall 0.863, above 0.90 in five of nine organisms, with the weakest cases (iJN1463 at 0.616, iJN1026 at 0.719) reported rather than hidden. Eleven curated GEMs ship with the tool, covering bacteria, a cyanobacterium, an archaeon, yeast and a diatom. The metabolic model behind the wet lab extended iJN1463 with the full p-cymene degradation route (cymA, cymB, cymC, cmtA-cmtE), linking to native metabolism through OP4ENH.","software":"NetFlux solves two linked problems in metabolic engineering: genome-scale models are too large to read, and strain-design algorithms waste most of their compute on reactions that can never carry flux. Its five-stage pipeline is the substance. Stage 1 solves FBA then pFBA and converts the flux distribution into a Mass Flow Graph (nodes = reactions, edges = metabolite mass flow). Stage 2 applies FASTCC against a fixed consistency threshold to strip blocked reactions, which the team identifies as the single largest source of computational expense. Stage 3 is SCOPE, their GUDIE-derived extractor: log-normalise the heavy-tailed edge weights, compute a flux-coupling score per reaction against the target, seed an interest signal at the target and propagate it with Personalised PageRank, combine coupling and interest into a per-edge score, expand outward over a bounded number of hops admitting reactions above a threshold anchored to the target's immediate neighbourhood, then an optional repair step that closes a broken corridor with a flux-weighted shortest path. Stage 4 annotates the corridor with FVA so each reaction is typed as essential-and-fixed, flexible, or a natural up/downregulation candidate. Stage 5 pools OptForce and OptKnock and co-ranks their proposals into one ordered list, resolving each to a gene linked to BiGG. Two design decisions stand out for reuse: the REST API is versioned, OpenAPI 3.1-specified and language-agnostic with async job polling (submit, get a job_id, poll), and all compute is deliberately RAM-only, with uploaded models referenced by short-lived tokens, a 30-minute SessionCache, and nothing a client submits ever written to disk or database. Users can upload their own GEM as .json or .sbml. It is self-hosted rather than publicly deployed: 'git clone https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/mit-mahe/netflux && docker compose up --build', then http://localhost:5173/, with stated system requirements of 2-4 GB RAM, any modern multi-core CPU and no GPU. A video guide and a step-by-step interpretation guide are on the wiki, and the limitation is stated plainly: constraint-based predictions describe what the network allows at steady state, not kinetics or regulation, so every strategy is a wet-lab hypothesis. Repo: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/mit-mahe/netflux (team repo https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/mit-mahe).","stack":["React","TypeScript","FastAPI","Pydantic","Python","COBRApy","SQLite","Docker","Docker Compose","OpenAPI 3.1 / Swagger UI","REST / JSON-over-HTTP","GraphML","SBML","BiGG database","KaTeX (wiki maths)","iGEM GitLab"],"techniques":["Flux Balance Analysis (FBA)","parsimonious FBA (pFBA)","Flux Variability Analysis (FVA)","Mass Flow Graph representation of metabolism","FASTCC flux-consistency pruning","flux coupling analysis","Personalised PageRank network propagation","GUDIE-style user-defined subgraph extraction","flux-weighted shortest-path repair","OptKnock bilevel knockout search","OptForce intervention search","co-ranking of competing optimisation outputs","recall benchmarking against independently derived essential reactions","CRISPR-Cas9 homologous recombination (wet lab)"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Sessions were run across India in English, Hindi, Telugu, Kannada and Marathi so language would not be a barrier, and deliberately extended beyond students: the team visited old age homes to learn how residents' associations with traditional scents had changed over their lifetimes, then introduced them to synthetic biology. Workshops included household-materials DNA extraction from bananas at Purple Space Library Manipal (50 students, grades 1-7, split by age group), recombinant DNA via insulin at GenWise Camp, and deeper project discussion with grade 11-12 biology students at Vidya Mandir Chennai. Teachers were walked through each protocol step so they could keep running the demonstration after the team left. 'Beyond Sight', an audio-only podcast, carried five conversations with visually impaired professionals (a radio jockey, a geneticist, the founder of assistive-technology company TorchIT, an ophthalmologist, and one of only three blind women to have submitted before the Supreme Court of India). A Colourblind Awareness Day Instagram series ran on 6 September.","ihp":"Stakeholder analysis walked the whole cumin supply chain, from traders who buy directly from farmers to internationally working perfumers, finding that each group holds a different version of the same problem. Hard numbers anchor the premise: India supplies about 70-80% of world cumin, the 2026 crop was projected at 9.0-9.2 million bags against roughly 11 million in 2025 (a 15-20% decline), hydrodistillation yields about 2.56% essential oil, and cuminaldehyde is a fraction of that. The decisive conversation was with Givaudan, which revealed that fragrance houses procure whole cumin essential oil rather than isolated cuminaldehyde; this redirected the team's target customer from fragrance houses to aroma compound manufacturers, where demand for isolated, sustainably produced cuminaldehyde actually exists. The inclusivity work was itself integrated rather than decorative: conversations with Vision Empower (IIIT Bangalore) identified that Indian educational structures restrict visually impaired students from science, and the team then built tactile models, trialled them with Mrs Nagarathna, a visually impaired educator, revised on her feedback, and tested with a ninth-grade student. Fixes were deliberately minimal and buildable from cheap materials: the existing DNA model distinguished bases only by colour, so each base got a texture instead (adenine smooth tape, thymine jute, guanine bubble wrap, cytosine velvet) wrapped around the existing model so it did not need rebuilding; the mitosis model's components were only drawn on, so they were rebuilt in clay with pipe-cleaner chromosomes. Vision Empower approved both the content and the models and will use them across their associated schools. A separate conversation with an Indian Sign Language expert established that, unlike ASL, ISL has no centralised place to learn it, which the team records as a gap they did not close.","realWorld":"A consumer survey found the barrier is information rather than opposition, with respondents already aware of land use, extraction and climate pressures and nearly half willing to pay slightly more for lower environmental impact, though more were worried about long-term effects of ingredients made by engineered microorganisms. Producer-side work found that Indian companies import aroma compounds from China, the UAE and France despite domestic cumin dominance, and that price is the deciding factor for buyers because raw material cost passes straight to consumers. The team wrote 'An iGEMer's Guide to Upscaling', a structured upscaling and biomanufacturing guide, after finding no iGEM-oriented resource bridging flask scale to industrial scale, and 'The Aroma Atlas', a catalogue of everyday scents, their active ingredients and current production methods, as a starting point for teams looking for compounds worth making sustainably. Six cym operon genes were submitted to the Registry as basic parts (BBa_26CFFF0H, BBa_26R7VUFV, BBa_26MJ8EI9, BBa_26SS8791, BBa_26AS8UG2, BBa_26AW7YAF).","partners":["Vision Empower / IIIT Bangalore","Givaudan","Score Foundation / Project Eyeway","TorchIT","Navjeevan Blind School, Pune","Purple Space Library Manipal","GenWise Camp","Vidya Mandir Senior Secondary School, Chennai","Manipal Academy of Higher Education","Anthropic (sponsor)","BioRender, GenScript, IDT, NEB, SnapGene (sponsors)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6145/wiki/dry-lab/software/fig-2-architecture.avif","caption":"NetFlux architecture: React/TypeScript frontend, FastAPI backend with Pydantic schemas and a bounded JobManager worker pool, over a SQLite model catalogue and precomputed Mass Flow Graphs.","credit":"Team MIT-MAHE, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6145/wiki/dry-lab/software/fig-1-pipeline.avif","caption":"The five-stage NetFlux pipeline: Mass Flow Graph construction, FASTCC pruning, SCOPE/GUDIE corridor extraction, FVA annotation, and the pooled OptKnock/OptForce design engine.","credit":"Team MIT-MAHE, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6145/wiki/dry-lab/software/fig-4-user-guide.avif","caption":"An extracted production corridor drawn automatically from a genome-scale model, flowing left to right with line width proportional to mass flow and reactions coloured by FVA annotation.","credit":"Team MIT-MAHE, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6145/wiki/dry-lab/software/fig-6-user-guide.avif","caption":"The intervention strategy suite: ranked designs with yield floor, retained growth, coupling strength and the specific gene to edit, separated from weaker exploratory leads.","credit":"Team MIT-MAHE, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"When there is no ground truth to benchmark against, define what a correct output must satisfy and measure that: NetFlux's credibility rests on three published numbers (100% corridor connectivity, 7.8% of the network retained, 0.863 recall of independently FVA-derived essential reactions), with the weakest organisms named. A research centre should also copy the ephemeral-compute design, user-uploaded models held in RAM behind short-lived tokens and never written to disk, which makes a shared analysis service usable with data people will not hand over.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/inclusivity","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/implementation","https://2026.igem.wiki/mit-mahe/contribution","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/mit-mahe/netflux"],"rank":33},{"team":"Uni-Padua-IT","id":"6151","section":"Undergrad","country":"Italy","village":"Space","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/","projectTitle":"C.O.S.M.I.C. (Cyanobacterial Oxygen Supply for Martian Inhabitant Complexes)","tagline":"A Nostoc-based life-support chassis for Mars, plus ReTIsigner, an open codon optimiser for the 35,799 hosts other tools ignore.","summary":"Uni-Padua-IT engineered the filamentous cyanobacterium Nostoc sp. PCC 7120 as a Martian life-support organism: it fixes CO2 into breathable oxygen, degrades the perchlorates that poison Martian water (via native NarB and a heterocyst-compartmentalised pcrABD plus chlorite dismutase), and carries pprA from Deinococcus radiodurans for radiation-damage repair, behind a copper-dependent MazF kill switch. Because no free codon optimiser listed their chassis, they forked TIsigner into ReTIsigner, a frequency-weighted codon optimiser that reads any of the 35,799 Kazusa codon usage tables, enforces GC bounds, actively excises RFC restriction sites and reports CAI, GC and translation-initiation opening energy. ReTIsigner was validated in silico over 100 sequences and four hosts, then in vivo: a ReTIsigner-optimised eYFP gave a 2.16-fold common-effect increase in specific fluorescence across DH5-alpha and BL21 (p = 0.002, permutation test stratified by strain). They also built an open-source acoustic (piezo + Arduino) bubble sensor so oxygen production can be followed without opening a sealed culture.","awards":["Best Software — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Space Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"SvelteKit single-page frontend built from scratch, talking to a Flask backend over a single documented HTTP endpoint (POST /optimise); a worker pool behind Flask shells out to the compiled scientific binaries. The whole stack is shipped as a two-stage Docker image (Node.js 22 build stage; Miniconda runtime pinning Python 3.6.9, ViennaRNA 2.5.1 and Infernal 1.1.5) exposing port 5050.","dataviz":"Results view compares input against each optimised sequence in a results card showing CAI, GC content and opening energy, so sequences no longer have to be analysed one at a time in external tools. A built-in guided tour walks a first-time user through every field of the run configuration. Exports in three reproducible formats (CSV, PDF, FASTA), each recording the full run configuration plus the computed analyses. Validation figures (specific-fluorescence bar charts and ratio plots with 95% CIs) are published as SVG on the wiki, and the system is explained with two architecture diagrams: the POST /optimise request path and one iteration of the annealing loop.","datascience":"In silico benchmark over 100 coding sequences optimised for four hosts spanning roughly 31-75% GC, recording GC, CAI, opening energy and failure rate with and without a codon usage table: supplying a table cut the failure rate from 52% to 2% while leaving opening energy essentially unchanged (mean difference +/-0.06 kcal/mol). Wet-lab validation used independent cultures as the unit of replication, three cultures per sample each in technical triplicate, fluorescence compared at matched optical density rather than fixed time, two detection channels, and a permutation test stratified by strain; the team explicitly states the confidence interval (about 1.6-2.8x) and what the data does not license them to claim.","software":"ReTIsigner is a free, Apache 2.0-licensed codon optimiser for non-model hosts, and it is the clearest example this year of forking an existing scientific tool rather than rebuilding one. The team started from TIsigner (Bhandari, Lim & Gardner), contacted its authors for feedback on their proposal, and then added four things TIsigner lacked: frequency-weighted synonymous codon sampling driven by any Kazusa codon usage table (35,799 organisms) instead of one-amino-acid-one-codon, an optional GC bound enforced as a feasibility filter, active position-targeted removal of restriction sites with a re-check for newly introduced sites, and in-app CAI/GC analysis per output sequence. The restriction-site work is the headline engineering result: optimising perchlorate reductase subunit A for Nostoc under RFC 10 and RFC 1000 constraints was still running after 15 hours under the original random-substitution behaviour, and completed in seconds afterwards, yielding registry part BBa_268WDVEB. Architecturally the optimiser sits behind POST /optimise so it is usable headlessly without the Svelte interface, and the Docker image removes the platform problem that ViennaRNA and Infernal binaries create on Windows ('docker build -t retisigner . && docker run -p 5050:5050 retisigner'). It ships a user guide, a development guide documenting the system and its API calls, a video tutorial (https://video.igem.org/w/rN6WnodTKYxiNitzfSUVZ3) that optimises BBa_E0040 GFP for Clostridium acetobutylicum ATCC 824, and a public repository at https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uni-padua-it/retisigner-svelte (team repo: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/uni-padua-it). It is self-hosted via Docker rather than offered as a hosted public web service, which is the one gap: there is no URL a stranger can click. They also positioned ReTIsigner honestly against the field, reproducing Table 1 of Demissie et al. (2025) and adding their own row with a tilde rather than a tick in the CAI column, and comparing feature-by-feature against the two previous iGEM codon optimisers (HebrewU 2018, MIT 2023). They published an open call for collaboration on 9 August 2026 offering to optimise any team's sequence for their chassis and received no replies, and they say so on the wiki.","stack":["SvelteKit","Svelte","Node.js 22","Python 3.6.9","Flask","Docker (multi-stage)","Miniconda/Conda","ViennaRNA 2.5.1 (RNAplfold)","Infernal 1.1.5","REST/HTTP API","Apache 2.0","iGEM GitLab","Arduino (hardware)"],"techniques":["Simulated annealing search","frequency-weighted synonymous codon sampling","mRNA translation-initiation opening-energy minimisation","Codon Adaptation Index (CAIcal/Sharp-Li definition)","GC-bound feasibility filtering","active restriction-site excision","Kazusa codon-usage-table parsing","permutation testing stratified by strain","in silico benchmarking across GC-spanning hosts","acoustic (piezoelectric) frequency-sweep sensing"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"A public survey on space research and biotechnology directly reshaped the team's communication: 76.5% of respondents rated using living organisms for oxygen and water highly while only 28.2% rated colonisation highly, so the team separated the usefulness of the technology from support for settling Mars; 42.2% knew little or nothing about GMOs, so explanations of what they modify and why were rewritten more accessibly; and 94.8% called a genetic safety system fundamental or very important, so the kill switch and confined bioreactor were given more space. Education activities grew out of the same consultations.","ihp":"Human practices is written as a sequence of questions with an explicit 'what we changed' and, unusually, a 'what we couldn't fix' for each. Consultations with photosynthesis and cyanobacteria professors (Nicoletta La Rocca, Daniela Billi) cut the cold-tolerance module. iGEM Darmstadt 2025, who had built the same perchlorate constructs in B. subtilis, warned them off pcrABCD's oxygen sensitivity, which led to testing native NarB first and then compartmentalising pcrABD in heterocysts. A presentation to NASA's Open Science Data Repository Analysis Working Group moved the radiation module from survival-at-high-dose to genome integrity over generations, and pushed them toward a terrestrial use case (perchlorate in groundwater near propellant plants) when the Moon turned out to have no perchlorate. ESA flight-operations engineers (Davide Vertuani, Paolo Ferri) made them reason about the journey, not just arrival. Philosopher Telmo Pievani and space-habitat designer Serena Crotti changed how the bioreactor is framed within a crew's daily life. Most relevant to a dry-lab centre: they took their hardware manual and code to an instrumentation professor and an electronics PhD student (Simone Del Favero, Samuele Faccin) before publishing, and as a result weakened their own claim (the sensor detects gas bubbles, it does not measure oxygen), redrew the circuit with no crossing wires, documented timing resolution, and added a table separating proven from untested.","realWorld":"The team identified a terrestrial application independent of any Mars mission: perchlorate contaminates groundwater near propellant and pyrotechnics manufacturing and is regulated, and biological removal is already an active research area. They were unable to secure an ionising radiation source (INFN Legnaro's Van de Graaff accelerator unavailable; IOV Padova's linear accelerator caps at roughly 6 Gy/min against a 6 kGy LD50), and report that the pprA radiation claim is therefore untested rather than glossing over it.","partners":["iGEM Darmstadt 2025","NASA Open Science Data Repository Analysis Working Group","European Space Agency (flight operations)","University of Padua (Biology, Chemistry, Information Engineering)","University of Rome Tor Vergata","TISIGNER authors (Bhandari, Lim, Gardner)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/new-frontend.avif","caption":"The ReTIsigner interface the team built from scratch in SvelteKit, replacing the original TIsigner form.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/guided-tour.avif","caption":"The in-app guided tour that walks a first-time user through configuring a whole optimisation run.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/results-opt.avif","caption":"The results card: input sequence against an optimised output, with CAI and GC content computed in-app.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/request-schema.avif","caption":"System architecture: the SvelteKit frontend posts a run to Flask, which hands it to a worker pool that shells out to RNAplfold and Infernal.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6151/wiki/software/optimisation-schema.avif","caption":"One iteration of the simulated annealing search, with the team's two additions: restriction-site removal before scoring and a GC-bound feasibility check.","credit":"Team Uni-Padua-IT, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Fork and extend an existing, cited scientific tool instead of building a new one, and validate the extension at the bench: ReTIsigner's credibility comes from a 2.16-fold wet-lab expression result, a 52%-to-2% failure-rate benchmark, and a published 'what we are entitled to claim' section that names its own limits. A research centre should also copy their packaging discipline, one Docker command plus a documented HTTP endpoint so the engine is reusable headlessly, and their habit of sending documentation to an outside instrumentation expert before publishing it.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/uni-padua-it/judging","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/uni-padua-it/retisigner-svelte","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/uni-padua-it"],"rank":59},{"team":"UCAS-China","id":"6157","section":"Undergrad","country":"China","village":"Therapeutics","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/","projectTitle":"PARCEL","tagline":"An inhaled, protein-based mRNA carrier that makes airway cells produce their own asthma medicine.","summary":"PARCEL is an inhaled mRNA delivery platform built from elastin-like polypeptides (ELPs) rather than lipid nanoparticles. The fusion protein ELP-Pum2-EEP13 uses Pum2 to grab mRNA carrying a PRS tag in its 3' UTR, ELP phase separation to encapsulate and protect it, an endosomal escape peptide to get out of the endosome, and a Sortase A-conjugated anti-UPK1B scFv to target airway club cells; the cargo is mRNA for SLPI (inhibiting neutrophil elastase and the mucus hypersecretion it triggers) plus a lower dose of IFN-lambda-1 (antiviral defence and Th2 suppression). It won 1st Runner-up and Best Integrated Human Practices because the stakeholder work genuinely steered the science: clinicians moved the project from rescue therapy to maintenance therapy, and an asthma mechanism researcher killed one of the two original cargoes outright. The dry lab is unusually complete, modelling the whole chain from E. coli production through nebulizer aerosol deposition to a PK-PD readout in FEV1 and FeNO, with every module feeding the next.","awards":["1st Runner-up (Undergrad)","Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best New Composite Part — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Integrated Human Practices — Winner (Undergrad)","Best Therapeutics Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Six connected modules sharing parameters and declared input-output relationships. (1) Production: a SimBiology ODE model of a quorum-sensing kill switch (LuxI/LuxRm A1 variant, pLuxRm-driven lysis) combining growth-dependent dilution with AHL kinetics, swept across Anderson promoters to find the one that lyses the culture just before ELP phase transition - this chose the promoter the wet lab used. (2) Assembly: AlphaFold 3 predicted the three fusion-protein monomers (1052-1322 residues), coarse-grained into Martini 3 and run in GROMACS for 2-3 microseconds at 298 K, 1 bar, 150 mM NaCl, 50 mg/mL protein, with pLDDT used to justify stabilising the Pum2 fold while leaving ELP flexible; alongside this they built CAPE (Coverage and Assembly Prediction for Protein Ensembles), a reduced free-energy model calibrated against wet-lab data, and used Bayesian optimization over CAPE as the objective to redistribute ELP repeats around Pum2 instead of brute-forcing every combination. (3) Pulmonary delivery: MPPD v3.04 on the adult Yeh-Schum five-lobe geometry screened log-normal aerosol size distributions, flow rates and inhaled volumes against deposition in the last three conducting generations per lobe, then ANSYS Fluent CFD simulated 3D airway transport, feeding a distal airway and airway-surface-liquid transport model and a reference cell-composition mapping that converts deposition into surface contacts per club cell. (4) Intracellular release: a hybrid ODE-stochastic model of one particle per endosome (median 560 nm, ~2,400 fusion chains and therefore 2,400 EEP copies per particle), tracking acidification, ELP disassembly, EEP-mediated membrane opening and RNA degradation to output intact cytosolic RNA over time, with intact RNA tracked separately from fluorescent label. (5) PK-PD: a four-compartment model (bone marrow, blood, airway wall, epithelial lining fluid) with 25 species, 53 assignment rules and 51 reactions, adapted from Gadkar et al.'s severe asthma immune network, giving four clinical readouts - FeNO, FEV1, relative goblet-cell count and 15-minute mucin release - used to compare SLPI:IFN-lambda-1 payload ratios and dosing frequency. (6) Response prediction: an affine mapping baseline plus residual CellFlow (flow matching) trained on public cytokine-perturbation scRNA-seq.","dataviz":"The model landing page opens with a scripted animation that walks the whole mechanism in sequence - the E. coli shell fills with protein, lyses, chains assemble into PARCEL, the particle travels into the bronchi, binds a club-cell receptor, is endocytosed, disassembles, releases mRNA, and mucus drops one step per round - with replay and skip controls and a text fallback when JavaScript is off. AlphaFold monomer structures are embedded as interactive viewers with pLDDT and protein-region colouring and cartoon/C-alpha toggles, so a reader can see directly why Pum2 was treated as rigid and ELP as flexible. The wiki also carries font-size controls (A-/Default/A+) for accessibility. On the software side, the AeroEase Android app turns raw device timestamps into an expandable-card Overview with a seven-day activity chart and a date-filtered History, and the FeroEase app plots live temperature, pH and OD600 with a falling-trend detector that flags the harvest window.","datascience":"Two concrete pieces. The target screen: using the Human Lung Cell Atlas they compared healthy adult club cells against other lung epithelial cells on fold change, adjusted p-value and detection fraction, and from 68 genes passing the criteria selected UPK1B - 2.28-fold enriched, adjusted p = 0.00559, RNA detected in 45.92% of club cells versus 3.43% of other epithelial cells. They then checked antibody accessibility (Human Surfaceome; UniProt O75841, 260 aa, four transmembrane regions, extracellular stretches at 37-60 and 108-229) and cross-tissue selectivity (archived GTEx: 0.11 TPM lung vs 21.90 TPM bladder; Human Protein Atlas showing membranous expression in urothelium), and stated plainly that UPK1B is not club-cell exclusive so the analysis prioritises an experimental candidate rather than demonstrating selective targeting. The response-prediction benchmark is equally honest: affine mapping cut mean-expression error 7.1% against predicting no change and beat the mean-response baseline, while the nonlinear residual CellFlow model improved some mean-expression predictions but worsened distributional agreement and the selected RNA panels - so they kept affine as the reference model and named cross-donor validation and RNA reconstruction as the open problems. Tasks were set up as donor-held-out rotations on GSE249307 and GSE246368 with unpaired source and target cells.","software":"Four software deliverables. AeroEase, an Android companion app for the nebulizer, syncs ESP32-C3 button timestamps over BLE through a validated handshake with checksum, session and duplicate checks, clock calibration from the phone, and acknowledgement only after durable storage, then presents them as a locally stored medication-use diary with daily and seven-day counts, date-filtered history, CSV export, and a rule-based offline assistant that answers questions without any cloud call; the team repeatedly states the records document device actions, not delivered dose or confirmed inhalation. FeroEase, written in Flutter, monitors the two-stage fermentation over Wi-Fi with temperature, pH, stirring control and a falling-OD600 trend detector that identifies the lysis harvest window a bench fermenter does not report. The bioinformatics pipeline for club-cell target selection is the third. A browser-based quiz game supports the education activities. Hardware: AeroEase is a portable vibrating-mesh nebulizer on an ESP32-C3 with rechargeable power, session logging, BLE, a gravity-fed reservoir and physically separated liquid and electronics compartments in a modular 3D-printed housing; FeroEase is an ESP32-S3 two-stage culture platform with pH and temperature sensing, controlled heating, motor stirring and transmitted-light OD600 tracking. Build resources and a user manual with source code access are published on the wiki.","stack":["AlphaFold 3","GROMACS","Martini 3 coarse-grained force field","MATLAB SimBiology","MPPD v3.04 (Multiple-Path Particle Dosimetry)","ANSYS Fluent","CellFlow (flow matching)","Human Lung Cell Atlas (HLCA)","Human Surfaceome","UniProt","GTEx","Human Protein Atlas","GEO (GSE249307, GSE246368)","Flutter","Android / Kotlin","ESP32-C3","ESP32-S3","Bluetooth Low Energy","SQLite (on-device)"],"techniques":["Coarse-grained molecular dynamics","Reduced free-energy modelling (custom CAPE model)","Bayesian optimization for sequence design","Quorum-sensing ODE modelling with growth dilution","Computational fluid dynamics aerosol deposition","Regional lung dosimetry screening","Hybrid ODE-stochastic single-endosome simulation","Compartmental PK-PD with Hill-function assignment rules","Differential expression with detection-fraction filtering","Affine distribution mapping","Flow matching for single-cell perturbation response","Donor-held-out cross-validation","Membrane topology and surfaceome annotation screening","SWOT analysis"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education was designed from two findings in their own clinician interviews and 461-person questionnaire: patients stop medication once they feel better, and parents refuse to start it for children out of fear of side effects, so compliance - not just awareness - became the target. Activities are segmented by audience. For children: a booth with biology-themed games at the Huairou District National Science Popularization Month fair (September 2026), doubling as a questionnaire collection point, plus a Life Science Classroom proposal for fifth and sixth graders in Huairou primary schools teaching respiratory tract structure through illustrations and interactive demos, published as a downloadable PDF so other teams can run it. For university students: an iGEM Winter Camp training new members with offline wet-lab tutorials (plasmid extraction, PCR, homologous recombination, protein purification) and online dry-lab and modelling tracks, and an annual Microbial Painting competition open to all majors as a recruitment funnel - which failed to produce the expected colony patterns, and the team published the three reasons why (cell sedimentation from static suspension, insufficient streaking density, IPTG induction of EGFP suppressing growth) and used it to teach participants that negative results are normal and troubleshooting is the skill. For the general public: a Public Science Day stall and a browser quiz game on synthetic biology and iGEM.","ihp":"The team formalised its own cycle, RCSF (Reflect-Connect-Share-Feedback), as a deliberate parallel to the DBTL engineering cycle, and ran the project through nine documented iterations with 20 individuals, teams and institutions across six defined communities (academia, medical professionals, industry, supervisors, patients and families, the public), 44 asthma patient representatives and 419 public representatives from under-10s to over-60s. Each community is introduced with who they are, what they know, and why their position matters. The changes are specific and traceable. Doctor Fei Wang's input moved the project from rapid-acting therapy to asthma maintenance treatment, which reset the whole target population. Professor Huaiyong Chen, who studies respiratory disease mechanisms, explained that in asthma many club cells differentiate into mucus-producing goblet cells - supporting the shift away from immune cells to airway epithelium - and told them that NKX2-1, one of their two original cargoes, matters in development but has little effect in adulthood, so it was dropped and SLPI took its place. Professor Pingsheng Liu's survey of liposome, LNP and polymeric carrier limitations fed directly into the ELP carrier rationale and the protein sequence design. Professor Yufei Xia flagged subtle flaws in the experimental design and walked them through the standard in vivo/in vitro validation workflow. Yaozhong Cao, a former iGEMer, argued with them about target specificity, which is upstream of the UPK1B antibody screen. Professor Xiaoru Huang, an ethics of science and technology researcher, reviewed the biosafety and bioethics discipline. They contacted the Huairou District Bureau of Landscaping and Forestry about local pollen and catkin management, grounding the project in Beijing's own regulation (the Measures implementing the Forest Law, effective 1 January 2026) and in observed thunderstorm asthma cases. Patient work was handled with explicit informed consent and privacy statements, and the questionnaire reports its own demographics (21 recovered, 21 currently medicated, 419 unaffected). The team states its guiding principle as 'local people solving local problems', and the HP group's standing job was to track every subteam's bottlenecks and feed external input back into each one.","realWorld":"The entrepreneurship work is a self-critical commercial assessment rather than a pitch. The team states outright that PARCEL's case must rest on defined unmet needs rather than technological novelty, and tabulates the strengths as well as the limitations of inhaled corticosteroids, bronchodilators and biologics before positioning PARCEL as a non-hormonal, lower-frequency, modular maintenance option. The SWOT names real weaknesses: delivery efficiency and duration of expression were only shown in HEK293T in vitro, so mucus, mucociliary clearance, local enzymes and immune uptake remain unaddressed; a combined protein-plus-mRNA product multiplies CMC burden across ELP molecular weight, purity, impurity profile and phase-transition behaviour and mRNA sequence integrity and stability, then payload ratio, condensate characteristics, batch consistency and storage; and the team lists the expertise it does not have (toxicology, regulatory affairs, clinical development, IP, market access). Opportunities are equally concrete - the modular Pum2/PRS-tag design means a new payload needs no new carrier, CAS institutes supply the analytical capability they lack, and China's pay-for-performance medical insurance pilot offers a reimbursement pathway for a therapy that can demonstrate efficacy. They commit to re-reviewing the SWOT at each development milestone.","partners":["Institute of Biophysics, Chinese Academy of Sciences","Institute of Process Engineering, Chinese Academy of Sciences","Institute of Chemistry, Chinese Academy of Sciences","Tsinghua University","Haihe Hospital, Tianjin University","Inner Mongolia International Hospital of Mongolian Medicine","Qingdao Municipal Hospital","Central Hospital of Kaifeng, Henan Province","Meishan Cardio Cerebrovascular Disease Hospital","242 Hospital, Shenyang Medical College","Weikang Pingsheng (Beijing) Biotechnology Co., Ltd.","Huairou District Bureau of Landscaping and Forestry, Beijing","iGEM ENTER team (Harvard Medical School)","Conference of China iGEMer Community (CCiC)","Huairou District primary schools","GenScript","MathWorks","SnapGene"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6157/wiki/humanpractices/hp-overview0.avif","caption":"The Human Practices overview: 20 individuals and institutions across six defined communities, 44 asthma patient representatives and 419 public representatives, mapped onto nine project iterations of the team's own RCSF cycle.","credit":"Team UCAS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6157/wiki/software/upk1b-volcano-2.avif","caption":"The club-cell antibody target screen: UPK1B highlighted in the differential-expression volcano against other lung epithelial cells, 2.28-fold enriched with adjusted p = 0.00559 and detected in 45.92% of club cells.","credit":"Team UCAS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6157/wiki/hardware/aeroease/pictures/fig6-hardware-2-yhz.avif","caption":"The AeroEase portable vibrating-mesh nebulizer prototype, built on an ESP32-C3 with BLE session logging and a modular 3D-printed housing separating the liquid reservoir from the electronics.","credit":"Team UCAS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6157/wiki/education/education-huairou0.avif","caption":"The team's booth at the Huairou District National Science Popularization Month fair, running biology games with children while collecting the public asthma-perception questionnaire.","credit":"Team UCAS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6157/wiki/software/app-charts-3up.avif","caption":"The AeroEase Android app turning raw BLE device timestamps into a medication-use diary with daily and seven-day activity charts, date-filtered history and CSV export, all stored on the phone.","credit":"Team UCAS-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Steal the RCSF cycle: UCAS-China gave human practices the same named, repeatable four-step loop (Reflect, Connect, Share, Feedback) that engineering gets with DBTL, mapped every stakeholder into one of six communities with a stated reason for including them, and then showed nine iterations where the loop closed - including dropping a cargo gene because one researcher said it does nothing in adults. A school research centre can run that loop on any project, and the second lesson is just as cheap to copy: publish what failed, as they did with the microbial painting that never grew, and the reasons why.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model-part1","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model-part2","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model-part3","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model-part4","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model-part5","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/model-part6","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/inclusivity","https://2026.igem.wiki/ucas-china/measurement"],"rank":212},{"team":"HFI","id":"6178","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"Therapeutics","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/","projectTitle":"TWO EcN, ONE METABOLIC MISSION - A Probiotic Synergistic Therapy for the Rare Disease Hyperlysinemia","tagline":"A dual-strain E. coli Nissle 1917 consortium, physically stapled together with SpyTag/SpyCatcher, that degrades dietary lysine in the gut so hyperlysinemia patients can escape lifelong diet restriction.","summary":"HFI (International Department, The Affiliated High School of South China Normal University, Guangzhou) built an orally delivered live biotherapeutic for hyperlysinemia, a rare AASS-mutation disorder with no approved therapy and an incidence near 1 in 411,000. They split the human saccharopine pathway across two EcN populations - an upstream strain expressing LKR and SDH, a downstream strain expressing ALDH and AASDHPPT - knocked out the endogenous ldcC lysine decarboxylase by CRISPR-Cas9 to remove background lysine consumption, and covalently coupled the two strains using eCPX-displayed SpyTag/SpyCatcher so intermediates pass between cells over a short diffusion distance. Biosafety is handled by a dual-input kill switch where an arabinose-driven Bxb1 serine integrase inverts the lysis-E cassette and an oxygen-sensing PcyoA promoter only fires outside the anaerobic gut. The project won because the dry lab genuinely steered the wet lab (NetLogo screening picked the RBS before cloning), the part collection was fully characterised with HPLC-MS, and the education and IHP programmes were unusually systematic for a high school team.","awards":["Top 10 (High School)","Best Part Collection — Nominee (High School)","Best Education — Winner (High School)","Best Presentation — Nominee (High School)","Best Wiki — Nominee (High School)","Best Village Project — Winner (Infectious Diseases / Therapeutics / Diagnostics / Oncology)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Two complementary models, both used to make decisions rather than decorate the wiki. First, an agent-based stochastic contact model in NetLogo 3D 7.0.4: 200 cells (100 SpyTag type A, 100 SpyCatcher type B) random-walk in a periodic box spanning -16 to 16 units, diffusion speed 0.17 per tick, interaction radius 1.4, 2,000 ticks; each successful A-B contact creates an undirected graph edge, decrements both cells' remaining binding capacity, and merges cluster IDs, so a bacterial aggregate is literally a connected component of a graph. They swept seven RBS parts (B0032, B0033, B0035, B0029, B0031, B0030, B0034) mapped to 5-100 surface display sites and tracked mean cluster size, max cluster size and the full size distribution, expanding the noisy 30-site condition to 60 replicate runs to characterise its stochastic variance (max 109.6 +/- 34.5). Second, a mass-conserving 13-state-variable compartmental ODE simulator of the lysine cascade: metabolites L (lysine), S (saccharopine), A (AASA) and P (AAA) across three compartments (extracellular medium, upstream SpyCatcher cytoplasm, downstream SpyTag cytoplasm), Michaelis-Menten rate laws with half-saturation 0.2, a saturating contact signal H = B/(0.05+B) converting occupied binding capacity into extra inter-strain exchange conductance g_CT, integrated with fourth-order Runge-Kutta at base step 0.001 (reported backbone mass-balance error ~3.77e-15), with toxic AASA exposure summarised by trapezoidal AUC.","dataviz":"Both models were ported to interactive browser pages embedded in the wiki, so a judge or reader can move the sliders and re-run the simulation live with no local install: the ODE metabolic simulator at https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/static/model/metabolism.html and the NetLogo cluster model at https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/static/model/netlogo.html. Outputs are dynamic cluster visualisations, metabolite time-course curves and quantitative readouts. Alongside this they reported the full cluster-size distribution (not just means), particle-weighted versus number-weighted distributions, and a table pairing each RBS part number with its translational strength tier and simulated outcome, which makes the in-silico screen auditable.","datascience":"The dry lab was used specifically to fill a measurement gap: they could not quantify the short-lived intermediates saccharopine and AASA in the wet lab, so the ODE model predicted them and exposed an explicit design trade-off - at SC:ST 2:1 lysine clearance is fastest (residual lysine 0.0519) but AASA peaks highest (0.3767, AUC 10.43), while at 1:2 AASA stays low (peak 0.1123, AUC 3.14) but 0.2980 lysine is left unconsumed. That framing (two goals that cannot be maximised at once) drove the choice of co-culture ratios actually tested. The cluster model's prediction that moderate display (B0035, 15 sites, mean max cluster 48.67 cells, about 24% of the system) sits in the usable middle regime while 50+ sites collapses 91-98% of cells into one giant component was then checked at the bench by fluorescence microscopy with ImageJ quantification of red-green overlapping cluster count and core area (3 clusters/field at 2:1 rising to 16 at 1:1 and 14 at 1:2, mean core area 15.04 to 19.41 um2), and the simulated ratio ranking matched the HPLC lysine-removal ranking. Measurement side: a cheap ninhydrin colorimetric screen was calibrated against FDAA-derivatised HPLC-MS (lysine derivative at 17.04 min, m/z 398.1, calibration R2 = 0.9985).","software":"No standalone software tool was released, but the two models are published as free-standing interactive web pages inside the wiki (metabolism.html and netlogo.html) and the complete NetLogo source (setup, go, update-cluster-distribution, avg-cluster-size, max-cluster-size, sweep-rbs, scan-catcher-series procedures) is printed in full on the Model page so another team can rerun the screen. Repository: https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/hfi. They also contributed a documented part collection to the Registry including BBa_26S7HLMM (ldcC target sequence) and BBa_269KMPAL (pEcgRNA-ldcC), plus the two models listed as community contributions.","stack":["NetLogo 3D 7.0.4","agent-based / graph connected-component modelling","ODE system (13 state variables, 3 compartments)","fourth-order Runge-Kutta integration","Michaelis-Menten kinetics","trapezoidal AUC integration","interactive HTML/JS model front-ends","ImageJ","HPLC-MS (FDAA derivatisation)","CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing","GitLab"],"techniques":["in-silico RBS strength screening before cloning","replicate-expanded stochastic variance characterisation (n=60 at the unstable condition)","cluster-size distribution analysis","strain-ratio flux trade-off analysis (AASA peak vs AUC vs residual lysine)","model-to-bench validation by fluorescence microscopy + ImageJ","colorimetric assay calibrated against HPLC-MS","mass-balance error reporting as a numerical sanity check"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education was designed from survey data rather than guesswork, and ran on an explicit five-step cycle they named CLEAR (Connect, Layer, Engage, Assess, Reinforce) in which every activity had to report a measured outcome and a change for the next iteration. Highlights: a self-written microbiology comic book issued in Cantonese and Mandarin dialect versions, with English, Japanese and German recorded audiobooks and text versions in Arabic, German, Russian, French, Korean, Portuguese, Japanese, Thai, Spanish and Indonesian - explicitly supplied as text so local educators can generate their own AI text-to-speech audio in languages the team does not speak. They ran a 100+ student conference at HFI International, a HFI Academic Summit with hands-on fruit DNA extraction, recorded lab-technique video lessons (pipetting, agarose gel electrophoresis, clean bench, plus an SMA rare-disease case study) on iGEM Video and Bilibili, an online Q&A session on Tencent Meeting, a university biology major exhibition poster, a Gobi Desert biology trip, a synthetic biology student society, outreach at the Guangzhou Social Welfare Institute, and a professional session with HSBC on financial literacy. Impact was measured with paired pre/post surveys: at the Academic Summit (n=24) correct identification of synthetic biology rose 79% to 96% with the 'synthetic biology is another name for genetic testing' misconception falling 8% to 0%, and correct understanding that DNA is in every living cell including fruit cells rose 42% to 88%. They also tracked platform metrics honestly, noting that 15 WeChat posts drew 248 likes, 955 views, 63 shares and zero comments, concluding one-way delivery was failing and pivoting to interactive formats, after which video lessons drew comments for the first time.","ihp":"IHP ran through a named six-step cycle (LYSERA: Listen, Yield, Shape, Execute, Reflect, Apply) across six sequenced phases - stakeholder mapping on an interest/power matrix, a 434-response public survey, technical validation, clinical and patient engagement, commercial and regulatory consultation, and peer review - then looped back through phases 3 to 6 a second time. The changes are concrete. Dr. Mingjun Sun (Sichuan University, synthetic biology and AI protein design) argued the lag between lysine detection and enzyme expression would make the probiotics miss the postprandial treatment window, so HFI abandoned the lysine-responsive promoter entirely and switched to constitutive expression of all degradation enzymes; he also warned that SpyTag/SpyCatcher aggregation risk scales with expression level and suggested building a computational model instead of trial and error, which is the direct origin of the NetLogo cluster model and the choice of medium-to-low strength RBS. Professor Liujie Huo questioned whether intestinal lysine concentration tracks blood lysine at all, forcing them to re-examine the physiological basis and repositioning of the product. The public survey changed the language of every outreach material: 'genetically modified' was replaced with 'engineered' across all materials because the survey showed the former triggered unease, and because 76.04% of respondents had heard of rare diseases but could not name one while only 8.29% could name three, education was rebuilt to start from absolute basics. Survey findings that 56.22% supported national insurance coverage and 59.45% preferred prescription-drug regulation validated the hospital-first go-to-market route and tiered pricing. Mr. Guodong Huo of the Illness Challenge Foundation insisted patients are not defined by their condition, which set a non-stigmatising language policy and a no-false-hope rule for all materials. Safety feedback (protease degradation risk) added simulated intestinal fluid stability tests to the experimental plan.","realWorld":"A full commercial dossier for EcN-BioShield covering unmet need, TAM/SAM/SOM, PESTEL, Porter's Five Forces, SWOT, competitor analysis against lysine-free medical foods such as Hyper LYS Anamix Infant (UK NHS ~GBP 54 per 400 g can, roughly GBP 1,500-2,000/year), a Business Model Canvas, and a 20-year financial projection. Pricing was set at 2,000 CNY per 30-capsule bottle (24,000 CNY per patient-year) after stakeholder consultation on affordability, with a modelled initial funding requirement of 1,260,000 CNY, break-even in year 16.79 and cumulative payoff of 59,020,546 CNY by year 20 - unusually long-horizon honesty for a student plan. They mapped three regulatory routes in parallel because hyperlysinemia is not in China's Rare Disease Catalog and there is no settled Live Biotherapeutic Product registration pathway, including applying to NMPA's Center for Drug Evaluation for Orphan Drug Designation. Risk management is broken out by regulatory, clinical, commercial, technical, public-acceptance, competitive and financial risk, with an early/mid/long-term (1-5, 5-10, 11+ year) milestone timeline. Ethical commitments include tiered pricing plus advocacy for national insurance coverage, no overstatement of readiness, age-appropriate formulations, and patient-data confidentiality.","partners":["Dr. Mingjun Sun, Sichuan University","Professor Liujie Huo","Professor Shengbo Wu","Professor Ran An","Professor Xiaofeng Yang","Dr. Guangquan Chen (clinician)","Mr. Guodong Huo, Illness Challenge Foundation (rare disease advocacy)","Mr. Junhao Li, Mr. K and Mr. Mao Ye (commercial / regulatory advisers)","HSBC (financial literacy and investment workshop)","2nd iGBA Exchange Event","South China Regional iGEM Exchange","HKUST (GZ) iGEM team","Guangzhou Social Welfare Institute","Cataverse Lab"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6178/wiki/model/4.avif","caption":"NetLogo agent-based output: simulation trajectories (A) and cluster-size distributions (B) under different SpyCatcher binding capacities, showing the transition from fragmented association at low display to near-global condensation at 50+ sites.","credit":"Team HFI, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6178/wiki/model/1.avif","caption":"The team's NetLogo 3D model interface used to screen candidate RBS parts for SpyCatcher surface display before any cloning was done.","credit":"Team HFI, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6178/wiki/model/10.avif","caption":"HPLC quantification of residual lysine in culture supernatant across three strain ratios, with and without the SpyTag/SpyCatcher binding module - the wet-lab test of the model's prediction (the 2:1 ratio performed best, as simulated; the crosslinking benefit itself was not significant).","credit":"Team HFI, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6178/wiki/education/figure-9.avif","caption":"The HFI Academic Summit, where a hands-on fruit DNA extraction activity was added in response to feedback that the previous session had been too academically dense; pre/post surveys measured the learning gain.","credit":"Team HFI, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy HFI's habit of making the dry lab a gatekeeper for the wet lab: a free NetLogo agent-based model screened seven candidate RBS parts and picked B0035 before a single plasmid was built, and an ODE model predicted the intermediates the team physically could not measure - both then republished as live browser sliders so a reader can rerun them. The second transferable move is their CLEAR/LYSERA discipline of refusing to log any outreach or stakeholder meeting unless it produced a measured outcome (paired pre/post surveys, engagement metrics including the embarrassing zero-comment result) and a named change in the next iteration.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/measurement","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/inclusivity","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/awards","https://2026.igem.wiki/hfi/static/model/metabolism.html","https://gitlab.igem.org/2026/hfi","https://api.igem.org/v1/teams/6178"],"rank":143},{"team":"Stanford University","id":"6278","section":"Undergrad","country":"USA","village":"Therapeutics","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/","projectTitle":"KelePatch","tagline":"A logic-gated, cell-state-specific RNA therapy for keloid scars, delivered by a microneedle patch shaped to the wound.","summary":"KelePatch builds an ADAR-based RADAR RNA sensor that only switches on inside keloid myofibroblasts, so a therapeutic payload is produced in diseased cells and not in healthy skin. The team designed a 26-part modular sensor collection across five fibrosis transcripts (ADAM12, POSTN, ASPN, COL11A1, SPP1), validated them in HEK293 cells, combined the two best into a dual-input AND gate for extra selectivity, and tested in healthy dermal fibroblasts and keloid-derived fibroblasts; they also paired a sensor with Cas12a2 to show the output is not limited to fluorescent reporters. Alongside the wet lab they built RADAR-Scout, a published web platform that takes a cell type and a gene and returns a ranked, structure-aware list of RADAR sensor candidates plus a mechanistic simulation of how it will behave. It won the Grand Prize because every layer connects: 960,518 single cells of transcriptomics pick the trigger, ViennaRNA folding energy ranks the sensor, ODE models explain the delay they saw at the bench, and a U-Net++ computer vision pipeline turns a photo of a scar into a 3D-printable patch.","awards":["Grand Prize Winner (Undergrad)","Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Model — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Therapeutics Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Four stacked layers. (1) Single-cell transcriptomics at scale: a processing pipeline integrating 26 scRNA-seq cohorts across cardiac, cutaneous, renal, hepatic and pulmonary fibrosis - 960,518 cells and 1,728 donor-population observations from CELLxGENE, CELLxGENE Census and GEO - with donor-level statistics averaged at equal donor weight so large-cell-count donors do not dominate. Rather than ordinary differential expression, they score each candidate across three populations (Pathological, Healthy, Bystander) to ask whether a transcript is abundant in the target cell and absent from neighbours, which is what an intracellular sensor actually requires. (2) RNA structure prediction: 19 predictors computed with ViennaRNA 2.7.2 (RNAfold for MFE and ensembles, RNApfold for per-nucleotide unpaired probability, RNAcofold for the sensor-trigger duplex) spanning sequence composition, secondary structure, accessibility and hybridization. (3) Mechanistic kinetics: five interconnected deterministic mass-action ODE models with a stochastic counterpart, covering plasmid nuclear entry, single-trigger plasmid, single-trigger RNA, AND-gate plasmid and AND-gate RNA delivery, built on Ligon et al.'s lipoplex delivery cascade with occupancy resolved into nine pit states and ten endosome states; 82 parameters are tabulated with provenance, and four are fitted by simulated annealing in Python against Cohen et al.'s qPCR nuclear copy-number data (p_entry = 0.107, K_sat = 232, k_TX = 0.273). A nine-state hybridization/editing scheme tracks the joint status of both AND-gate sites. (4) Device physics: finite element analysis using Fick's law, the Stokes-Einstein relation and Darcy's law for LNP diffusion through keloid extracellular matrix, plus a nociceptor-count model for pain.","dataviz":"RADAR-Scout is the visualization: a deployed web app (https://radar-scout.vercel.app/, with a companion dashboard at https://radar-scout-dashboard.vercel.app/) where the keloid view ranks 600 filtered genes across five cohorts on the left and plots every candidate sensor for the selected gene on the right, so a target and a sensor are chosen in the same screen, with filters for region type, codon variant, duplex length and isoform, and shortlist/CSV export. Changing the selected tissue reruns the isoform analysis live. The wiki itself is a hand-built site with per-page accent colours and mascot illustrations, and the modelling page is written so a reader can follow each decision: predictor correlations are shown three ways (full Spearman matrix at n = 24, per-replicate rho, then all predictors ranked) and the team states plainly that non-significant predictors are 'unresolved at n = 24 rather than confirmed null'. Parameter tables flag which values are fitted, which are placeholders, and which disagreements remain unresolved.","datascience":"The core result is a replacement for a field convention. The informal RADAR design guideline was to pick the candidate with the lowest GC content. Stanford built a 5x5 experimental library of 25 sensors, measured them by flow cytometry in HEK293 across two replicates (r = 0.94), gated the top 5% mCherry-positive cells and took geometric-mean GFP / geometric-mean mCherry as the output metric, then correlated 19 computational predictors against it. Sensor partition-function folding free energy reached Spearman rho = 0.62-0.63, against -0.29 for GC content and -0.31 for longest homopolymer - so folding energy, not composition, became the ranking filter inside RADAR-Scout. They were honest about what failed: linear, Ridge and Elastic Net regression on the same features reached only Q2 = 0.20, which is explicitly what pushed them from statistical fitting to mechanistic ODEs. Cross-organ analysis of the top 300 genes per organ found 857 of 1,136 distinct genes appeared in only one organ and pairwise Jaccard similarity of 0.050-0.145, so they rejected the idea of a universal fibrosis biomarker set. Isoform filtering uses GTEx Oxford Nanopore long-read data (~150,000 transcripts, 92 samples, 15 tissues) to check the trigger sequence is actually present in the isoforms that cell type expresses.","software":"RADAR-Scout, live at https://radar-scout.vercel.app/ with a dashboard at https://radar-scout-dashboard.vercel.app/. It solves a real engineering problem: the Gao lab's published RADAR candidate table has roughly 7 million rows and could not be held in memory, so build_database.py ingests human_RADAR_sensor_candidates.csv.gz into a SQLite database in 100,000-row chunks with an index on the gene column, and the app queries it read-only with 50 candidates per API page. The computer vision pipeline for personalized microneedle patches uses a U-Net++ with a ResNet34 encoder (from segmentation_models.pytorch) to segment keloid wound photographs, then drives the Autodesk Fusion 360 API to emit a CAD/STL file for 3D printing. Separately, SynBase (https://synbase.web.app/home.html) is a released, free, account-based online learning platform. Parts: 26 modular RADAR parts including 15 functional sensors across five transcripts, novel composite synthetic triggers that each test an entire sensor panel for one gene, a dual-input AND gate, and T7 in vitro transcribed capped polyadenylated mRNA versions.","stack":["ViennaRNA 2.7.2 (RNAfold, RNApfold, RNAcofold)","CELLxGENE / CELLxGENE Census","GEO (GSE163973, GSE181316)","Human Dermal Fibroblast Atlas","GTEx long-read (Oxford Nanopore) RNA-seq","Ensembl / GENCODE annotations","Python","SQLite","Vercel","U-Net++ with ResNet34 encoder (segmentation_models.pytorch)","Autodesk Fusion 360 API","Benchling","Firebase (SynBase)"],"techniques":["Deterministic mass-action ODE modelling","Stochastic (occupancy-resolved) simulation","Simulated annealing parameter fitting","Quasi-steady-state approximation for competitive ADAR binding","Nine-state hybridization/editing state machine","Differential expression with pathological/healthy/bystander contrast","Isoform-level expression filtering","RNA minimum free energy and partition-function analysis","Spearman and Pearson predictor screening","Linear/Ridge/Elastic Net regression (reported as insufficient)","Jaccard similarity for cross-organ overlap","Flow cytometry gating (95th percentile mCherry)","Finite element analysis (Fick, Stokes-Einstein, Darcy)","Semantic image segmentation","Parametric CAD generation"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Two deliberately linked programmes. SiBRP (Stanford iGEM Bioengineering Research Program) is a free, fully online three-week bioengineering bootcamp for high school students and incoming freshmen, with nine synchronous two-hour sessions plus three supplemental ones, progressing from molecular biology fundamentals through biodesign, genetic circuit design, plasmid design and experimental workflow to entrepreneurship and ethics. Students work in small groups to produce a full research proposal with literature review and experimental design, submitted as a draft, returned with TA feedback, then revised against a published rubric. It drew nearly 5,000 applications from all 50 US states and over 100 countries (up from 321 in its first year), with two-thirds of applicants female and over 60% from low-income and first-generation backgrounds; 230 participated. Nine guest speakers included iGEM co-founder Drew Endy. Because mentored capacity could not meet demand, the team converted the whole curriculum into SynBase (https://synbase.web.app/home.html) - 'Khan Academy, but for synthetic biology' - nine self-paced modules with accounts that save progress, knowledge checks, interactive activities and discussion boards, no application and no deadline, free worldwide. As of 17 October 2026 it had 750+ registered learners and 650+ free-response discussion contributions, with dry lab modules in development, and the team explicitly invites other iGEM teams to use or adapt it for onboarding their own members.","ihp":"The team was trained in Stanford's Biodesign process by Dr Ross Venook, who told them that biodesign means shadowing clinicians rather than searching online, and they built the whole project around that. Ideation ran as a weekly discipline: every member interviewed one person a week - over twenty conversations with professors, researchers and people in their lives - and a team member's experience with ReSurge, an NGO training reconstructive surgeons abroad, surfaced the statistic that 30% of global disease burden is surgical and reframed abnormal scarring as an access problem. Professor Fan Yang told them burns and keloids are separate research areas and doing both would muddle the work, so they picked one. At the Stanford BIOME Shark Tank they pitched to eight judges (faculty, investors, biotech entrepreneurs) who returned written scorecards; the published response table shows each criticism and what they did, including a judge's suggestion to build an AI-CAD platform for microneedles, which became the U-Net++ segmentation-to-Fusion-360 pipeline. When they looked for keloid patient advocacy groups there were almost none, and the one foundation that replied told them to talk to clinicians instead - the team says plainly that physicians cannot stand in for patients' lived experience and documents this as a second-best route. They then contacted 28 Stanford dermatologists and plastic surgeons and interviewed seven, with a standard instrument of Likert-scale ranking statements plus specialty-specific open questions. Three concerns recurred - recurrence within two years, pain and inconvenience of repeated injections, and difficulty getting drug into dense scar tissue - and each changed the design: Dr Teng's point that late-stage keloids are far harder to treat moved the target from prevention to established keloids; Dr Li's recommendation of collagen-scaffold fibroblast and hydrogel mechanotransduction assays changed the validation plan; physicians' complaint about repeated injections, plus Dr Li's endorsement of a patch, drove the wound-shaped microneedle array, held at 900 um because needles over 1000 um are documented to cause bleeding and pain. Dr Miranda consulted her own network on whether viral-derived parts are inflammatory, and the team decided against combining saRADAR with RNA export machinery as a result. With no animal model for keloids (they occur only in humans), they met Genoskin about their ex vivo human skin models and identified that as the next safety step.","realWorld":"The team was explicit at the Shark Tank that a target product profile was secondary to characterising a modular sensor platform other teams and scientists can reuse, and the contribution page is written as a handoff: swap the sensor region for a new transcript, add a second input as an AND gate, deliver as DNA or RNA, attach your own payload. The clinical path is staged and named - validate the Gao lab sensors and their own in HEK293, validate the AND gate, move to keloid and normal human fibroblast lines, add payload, then terminal assay in Genoskin's ex vivo human skin model for immunotoxicity, phenotypic and transcriptomic readouts - and Genoskin representatives engaged and advised on how to run it. The Cas12a2 payload work and the in vitro transcribed mRNA format are both framed as moving toward the form a real therapy would take rather than a reporter demo. SynBase is maintained beyond the competition as a continuing programme rather than a competition artefact.","partners":["ReSurge International","Genoskin","Stanford Biodesign","Stanford Hospital Department of Dermatology","Stanford Department of Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery","Stanford BIOME (Biological Interdisciplinary Open Maker Environment)","Bay Area Bioengineering Symposium","Stanford Arc Institute","Gao Lab (RADAR technology)","Covert Lab","CELLxGENE / Chan Zuckerberg Initiative","GTEx Consortium"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/m2-fig4-trigger-selection.webp","caption":"Trigger selection in RADAR-Scout: 600 filtered genes ranked across five keloid cohorts on the left (POSTN first at DSS 1.288), with every candidate sensor for the selected gene plotted on the right, so target and sensor are chosen in one view.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/m2-fig10-rho-ranked.webp","caption":"All 19 computational predictors ranked by Spearman rho against sensor output: the two folding-energy terms lead at 0.62-0.63 while GC content, the field's informal selection rule, reaches only -0.29.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/m3-cv1-patch.webp","caption":"A microneedle array patch generated from a photograph of a keloid scar: U-Net++ with a ResNet34 encoder segments the wound, and the Autodesk Fusion 360 API turns the mask into a 3D-printable CAD file.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/m2-fig20-nine-state.webp","caption":"The nine-state hybridization and editing scheme for the AND-gate RADAR sensor; only the doubly-edited state is translated into GFP, and the enumerated transitions are how the system was verified to be complete.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6278/wiki/sibrp-call.webp","caption":"The final session of SiBRP 2026, the team's free three-week online bioengineering bootcamp, with students joining from 24 US states and 40 countries.","credit":"Team Stanford University, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Stanford's SiBRP-to-SynBase pivot is the directly copyable move: they ran a small mentored cohort, discovered demand they could not staff (5,000 applications for 230 places), and then converted the exact same curriculum into a free self-paced online course with progress tracking and discussion boards - so the teaching asset outlives the cohort. A high-school-plus centre in Taiwan can do the same with its own course material, and should also adopt their dry-lab habit of testing the field's received wisdom (lowest GC content) against its own measured data and replacing it with a better predictor.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/parts","https://2026.igem.wiki/stanford-university/safety","https://radar-scout.vercel.app/","https://radar-scout-dashboard.vercel.app/","https://synbase.web.app/home.html"],"rank":230},{"team":"ETH-Zurich","id":"6289","section":"Overgrad","country":"Switzerland","village":"Foundational Advance","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/","projectTitle":"preCISe","tagline":"A modular tool for precise intracellular delivery using contractile injection systems","summary":"About 79% of the human proteome is intracellular and largely undruggable, and no approved protein therapeutic delivers an exchangeable protein cargo into the cytosol. preCISe engineers Photorhabdus virulence cassettes (PVCs), bacterial extracellular contractile injection systems that act as molecular syringes, into a retargetable delivery platform: a SpyTag/SpyCatcher-attached binder arm swaps the target without re-engineering the cage, demonstrated against CD20 on engineered mammalian cells with a piggyBac Cre-loxP reporter line (preCISion) that turns on GFP only when Cre reaches the cytosol. To raise specificity they added dual targeting, splitting the cargo across two PVCs against two receptors. It won Best Software and Best Foundational Advance because the platform ships as reusable infrastructure: 29 tested protocols, a part collection for evaluating intracellular delivery, and two open-source tools, deCISion and OpenSplitProtein.","awards":["Top 10 (Overgrad)","Best Part Collection — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Software — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Wiki — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Foundational Advance Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Both tools are deliberately model-free, which is the team's central dry-lab argument. OpenSplitProtein ranks every peptide bond in a .pdb or .cif structure on two separate questions rather than one blended score: split tolerance (packing density weighted by inverse squared distance, distance from the buried core, solvent exposure summed over the flanking pair) and fragment reassembly (cross-cut contacts within 8 A, size-normalised interface, interface-to-cohesion ratio). No network was trained and no coefficient was fitted into the ranking; the only constants are the direction of each measurement, two size exponents from the domain-parsing literature and the 8 A contact cutoff. Crucially they defined a structure-free baseline (the length of the shorter fragment, plus relative position i/n) and showed that cross-cut contacts flip sign once it is held fixed, from +0.174 to -0.108, meaning the naive positive correlation was an artefact of chain geometry. Validation used Tsuboyama et al. (2023) cDNA display proteolysis insertion-stability labels at 13,622 backbone positions across 293 domains in 129 sequence families for split tolerance, and 223 split-intein sites from the Int-In dataset (126 with graded efficiency) for reassembly, with feature selection frozen before the test sites were seen and no sequence family shared at 40% identity nor fold at TM-score 0.3. deCISion is a database-integration pipeline rather than a predictor, chaining the Cell Surface Protein Atlas, Human Protein Atlas, SAbDab, Cellosaurus, UniProt, RCSB PDB, NCBI, the iGEM Registry and the Kazusa codon table.","dataviz":"The benchmark is reported as a percentile table against three comparators of different kinds, and the team says so explicitly: SplitSeek-Pro (a true competitor), the 2019 iGEM Protein-split Model, and ThermoMPNN-I (not split software at all, but the only released model predicting the insertion label directly, included as the hardest baseline). On 21 published split sites across superfolder GFP, Cre recombinase, horseradish peroxidase, firefly luciferase, Cas9 and NanoLuc, OpenSplitProtein puts the working site at a median 21st percentile with 13 of 21 in the top quarter, against a random expectation of 50; a 22nd site (Cas9 713/714) is excluded and the reason given. Both tools run in the browser (OpenSplitProtein needs only the HTML file opened; structures never leave the user's machine) with a 3D viewer showing the cut and both halves, and the wiki itself is a searchable static site with term callouts, a 'cut anatomy' diagram and an interactive outreach map rendered with d3-geo and topojson over OpenStreetMap and Natural Earth data. Published figures carry explicit tables for how to read a correlation column.","datascience":"The project's data-science contribution is the separation of proxy label from real task. Insertion stability measures the cost of perturbing an intact chain, never the cost of releasing two fragments, and the wiki states that difference rather than eliding it; the split-intein set is then used as the only label that directly tests reassembly. Scores are converted to within-structure ranks (0-1, displayed 0-100) so results are comparable across proteins of different size, and selection and testing datasets are separated with structural alignment checks. deCISion turns a public mass-spectrometry atlas into an actionable ranking: it reports each surface receptor's measurement strength on the chosen cell type and how many of the 47 CSPA cell types it was detected on as a specificity proxy, counter-checks normal tissue, cell-type, blood and tumour expression from the Human Protein Atlas, and lists only nanobodies and scFvs from solved structures with measured Kd where one exists (and states that only 1,019 of 14,860 SAbDab binders, 6.9%, have one). Limitations are enumerated rather than hidden: 47 cell types only, selectivity across cultured lines is not tissue specificity, and 'not detected' is not absence because Cell Surface Capture only sees N-glycosylated protein.","software":"Two released tools. OpenSplitProtein (gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/eth-zurich/opensplitprotein) ranks split sites from a structure file, installation-free in the browser. deCISion (gitlab.igem.org/2026/software/eth-zurich/decision) turns a cell type into a PVC construct in five steps: pick one of 47 CSPA human cell types by name, disease or tissue; choose a receptor; choose a nanobody or scFv binder (whole antibodies are rejected as too large for a PVC); accept the published XTEN linker or paste your own (pointing users to iGEM Calgary 2023's LINKED database); assemble binder-linker-SpyCatcher and export protein and DNA sequences as FASTA plus GenBank and SBOL3, with the three plasmids to order (cage, cargo, binder arm) listed. It ships MIT-licensed with a web interface, a JSON API and a CLI, 28 automated tests on every push through GitLab CI, a Docker build, a 2.5-minute demo video, and documentation of the architecture, the data build, what did not work and what the software cannot tell you. The team also published 29 tested eCIS protocols and a piggyBac/SpyTag part collection.","stack":["GitLab CI","Python 3.11","bun","Docker","MIT licence","JSON API + CLI + web UI","FASTA / GenBank / SBOL3 export","Cell Surface Protein Atlas (CSPA)","Human Protein Atlas","SAbDab","Cellosaurus","UniProt / RCSB PDB / NCBI","Kazusa codon usage database","iGEM Registry (BBa_K1159201 SpyTag)","d3-geo / topojson","BioRender"],"techniques":["geometric structure scoring (packing density, SASA, cross-cut contacts)","within-structure rank normalisation","confounder adjustment against a structure-free baseline","benchmark against published split sites and a stability-prediction baseline","family- and fold-level data leakage control (40% identity, TM-score 0.3)","surfaceomics-driven target selection and counter-selection","SpyTag/SpyCatcher modular retargeting","split-protein and split-intein design","piggyBac transposon reporter line construction","SWOT stakeholder analysis"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education was structured on three pillars (outreach, media, events) with a defined target audience per group and a before-and-after survey used as the measurement instrument, not just feedback. They visited four Swiss high schools (Kantonsschule Uster, Seetal, the cantonal high school for adults, Hohe Promenade) reaching over 130 students in formats from 45 to 90 minutes, and ran a hands-on week at the Wyss Zurich mint & pepper BioSTARS summer camp with 20 students from across Switzerland who performed their own PCR and gel electrophoresis, co-hosted with iGEM Zurich; over 150 students in total. They published two scientific-journal articles including one in BIOspektrum, ran a YouTube channel, co-hosted the Swiss iGEM Meetup for five teams, and presented at the Student Biolab welcome event, with about 16,900 social views. All templates and teaching material are published for reuse.","ihp":"24 interviews across six stakeholder groups, each analysed with a SWOT grid, and the changes are concrete. The project began as an eCIS biopesticide against Drosophila suzukii in Swiss crops; conversations with Drosophila experts and BFH European Meet-Up judges (Dr. Nicole Gensch, Amelia Wei) established that this was not viable and the team pivoted to human intracellular delivery while keeping eCIS. Scientific experts in the Pilhofer and Food Microbiology labs drove the choice of CD20 as the proof-of-concept target and the design of the reporter systems. The dual-targeting split-cargo concept was invented in direct response to expert questions about off-target risk, and OpenSplitProtein exists only because that concept needed a cut site: the wiki states the idea came from those conversations, recorded under Human Practices. Dr. Max Brackmann (NBC arms control laboratory, Spiez) assessed the work case-by-case against CDC and Robert Koch Institute checklists and concluded neither the PVC work nor deCISion required additional biosafety layers; the idea of building deCISion on the CSPA came from Prof. Bernd Wollscheid, after the team found the existing SISYPHUS interface unusable for their actual question. Investors and companies (Athebio, Procavea, Nanosyrinx) pushed specificity, off-target data, IP and scalability, and advised focusing a platform on one lead application.","realWorld":"The team is explicit that this is a foundational advance handed to others rather than a product: the stated purpose is to let future iGEM teams and researchers engineer eCIS more easily, backed by 29 protocols with recommended controls for teams with no prior wet-lab experience, a part collection, and two MIT-licensed tools. Their own validation honesty is part of the case: they state that the best validation of deCISion would be wet-lab targeting of new cell types from its outputs, that they had neither the time nor the scale, and that confidence therefore rests on the validated public databases underneath. The outlook names the sequence required before any clinical path: uniform and consistent PVC expression, verification of predicted binders, validation of split cargo, an industrially scalable PVC production workflow, then in-vivo mouse work on immune response, degradation, accumulation and efficacy. Clinicians (University Hospital Zurich, Hirslanden) were approached partly to establish contacts should the project continue past the competition.","partners":["Pilhofer Lab, ETH Zurich (Adam Pinsler, David Schaumann, Dr. Charles Ericson)","Prof. Bernd Wollscheid, ETH Zurich (CSPA)","Dr. Enea Maffei, Food Microbiology, ETH Zurich","Prof. Martin Loessner, ETH Zurich","Prof. Nadia Mazouz, ETH Zurich (ethics)","Dr. Max Brackmann, NBC arms control laboratory Spiez","Dr. James Lapworth, Nanosyrinx","Dr. Tom Edwardson, Procavea Biotech","Dr. Richard Woods and Dr. Johannes Urban, Athebio","Lukas Frischknecht, University Hospital Zurich","Jan Duscha, Hirslanden medical group","Wyss Zurich mint & pepper BioSTARS summer camp","iGEM Zurich (UZH)","BFH European Meet-Up","Student Biolab Zurich","ScopeM, ETH Zurich"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6289/wiki/images/software/opensplitprotein/web1-03-ranked-v3.webp","caption":"OpenSplitProtein's browser interface showing every cut site in an uploaded structure ranked separately for split tolerance and fragment reassembly, instead of a single blended score.","credit":"Team ETH-Zurich, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6289/wiki/images/software/decision/howto4-2-receptor.webp","caption":"deCISion's receptor step: surface receptors measured on the chosen CSPA cell type, with measurement strength, how many of the 47 cell types each was detected on as a specificity proxy, and the number of PVC-compatible binders available.","credit":"Team ETH-Zurich, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6289/wiki/images/results/retargeting-v3.webp","caption":"Retargeting result: PVCs carrying an anti-CD20 binder arm attached through SpyTag/SpyCatcher act specifically on CD20-expressing preCISion cells.","credit":"Team ETH-Zurich, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6289/wiki/images/education/summer-camp-gel.webp","caption":"Students running their own gel electrophoresis at the Wyss Zurich mint & pepper BioSTARS summer camp, where 20 high-school students from across Switzerland worked through a realistic research scenario.","credit":"Team ETH-Zurich, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"The transferable move is cheap-but-honest computation: ETH beat heavier published tools by scoring six geometric quantities, refusing to fit a model, and then spending their effort on validation design instead (a structure-free confounder baseline, frozen feature selection, no shared sequence family or fold between selection and test sets). A school research centre can reproduce that whole method on a laptop, and should copy the companion habit of publishing a 'what the software cannot tell you' section alongside the results.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/software/decision/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/education/","https://2026.igem.wiki/eth-zurich/contribution/"],"rank":136},{"team":"Princeton","id":"6295","section":"Undergrad","country":"United States","village":"Foundational Advance","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/","projectTitle":"SymPleco: Making Precision Sequestration Sym-ple","tagline":"One M13 phage chassis, any cargo — bulk-capacity adsorption with antibody-grade specificity and light-triggered release.","summary":"SymPleco attacks a trade-off that runs through all remediation chemistry: activated carbon captures a lot but cannot tell similar molecules apart, while monoclonal antibodies bind precisely but need harsh pH or chaotropic salts to release and cannot be recovered or scaled. Princeton's answer is a filamentous M13 bacteriophage used as a programmable adsorbent: sortase A-mediated ligation staples engineered fusion proteins onto the N-terminus of every pVIII copy (making 'furry' phage that bypass classical display size limits), with pIII carrying a Cg-Defensin anchor for PLA filter attachment, a VHH7 nanobody for macrophage clearance, and an AsLOV2 optomonobody that holds cargo in the dark and lets go under 450 nm light. Because swapping the target means swapping only the displayed binder, the team paired it with SymPlify, a de novo binder design pipeline, so a new target does not wait on a new antibody. It won on foundational-advance logic: a genuinely modular chassis demonstrated across three deliberately unrelated applications (melanoma exosome sequestration, Parkinson's alpha-synuclein interception, microcystin/BPA water remediation), backed by an unusually self-critical ODE model and an IRB-approved patient engagement programme.","awards":["2nd Runner-up (Undergrad)","Top 10 (Undergrad)","Best Part Collection — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Measurement — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Foundational Advance Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Two distinct computational efforts. SymPlify is a de novo protein binder design pipeline that routes a target by type: a protein chain goes to BindCraft (AlphaFold2-multimer hallucination with gradient descent on logits through softmax, SolubleMPNN sequence design, AF2-monomer re-prediction as a deliberately harsh filter, then PyRosetta physical metrics for binding energy and surface hydrophobicity), while a small-molecule or macrocycle chain goes to RFdiffusion3 all-atom diffusion plus LigandMPNN (eight sequences per backbone) and an independent complex-prediction model. PeSTo supplies hotspot residue scoring, RDKit renders structures and finds pharmacophores, and freesasa computes hydrophobic patch fraction. Separately, the measurement work is backed by a mechanistic ODE model of the recursive Phage Jumpstart Assay (jumpstart.py, jumpstart_mechanistic.py, passage.py, phase2.py, recursion.py) tracking naive, infected and free-particle pools with superinfection exclusion (sigma = 0.05), saturating infection, carrying capacity, and a hybrid-coat infectivity a_h derived from real pIII gene dosage (a_h ~0.729 at m13ko7_copies=20); the 1:11 passage step samples each particle pool by Poisson rather than plain division so a lineage can genuinely go extinct.","dataviz":"SymPlify's web interface is the visualisation centrepiece: an RDKit 3D viewer colours the target by PeSTo binding probability, lists top hotspot residues with confidence scores, lets the user delete low-scoring ones with an x to tighten the patch, and recomputes the difficulty breakdown (spread, hydrophobic surface fraction, globularity) live as the selection changes — plus a warning when residues sit more than 30 A apart, because one binder cannot reach them all. A run-status page shows pipeline stage, target type, elapsed time, auto-resubmits, design count and job ID, and a final ranking screen lets the user click through designs and download .pdb files with or without GS linkers. The model page is unusually transparent as data presentation: it prints the full command reference, labels every parameter as measured, derived or assumed, and ranks assumptions by their sensitivity on the round-2 plate ratio.","datascience":"The Jumpstart assay turned a single colony-ratio readout into a trajectory: because each recursion is both a manipulation and a measurement, repeated rounds on a population being driven toward one genotype reveal behaviour a single ratio cannot. Statistics are handled carefully — intervals bootstrap over wells, leave-one-well-out checks are reported, panels yielding 0-2 Amp colonies out of 165-272 are reported as upper bounds rather than measurements, and a chi-square test (197 on 5 df) establishes that the six Run B constructs are not interchangeable while the team explicitly declines to lean on the ordering because the bottom four are indistinguishable from zero. They also report that three magnitude-based titer estimators disagreed by up to 20,000-fold on the same panel, which is why they built a ratio readout instead. Most notably the model documents its own error history: an initial m13ko7_copies=200 was simply the wrong figure (wild-type M13 rolling-circle copy number rather than M13KO7's actual p15A-driven one), corrected to 10 and then 20 as evidence improved, with the admission that the corrected model is more parameter-sensitive, not less.","software":"SymPlify, an integrated de novo binder design pipeline with a web interface, hotspot prediction, cluster job submission with auto-resubmission, and post-design filtering and ranking; released for the wider synthetic biology community and wet-lab tested on the team's own targets. Team repository: https://github.com/dianalluu/puigem2026, with an AlphaFold deployment for the Princeton Della cluster at https://github.com/09aaronl/alphafold_princeton-della. The ODE model ships as a Python package with 52 automated checks in test_model.py (~80 s full run, ~8 s in fast mode) covering mass-balance and neutrality invariants, the hybrid-coat gene-dosage derivation, the Poisson passage statistics and pinned regression values. Hardware: a 3D-printed PLA gyroid biofilter cartridge whose two winning PITCCH designs are published as downloadable source STEP assemblies.","stack":["BindCraft","AlphaFold2-multimer","AlphaFold2 monomer","RFdiffusion3 (RosettaCommons foundry)","LigandMPNN","SolubleMPNN / ProteinMPNN","PeSTo","PyRosetta","RDKit","freesasa","ChimeraX","Python (NumPy/SciPy ODE integration)","Benchling","SLURM GPU cluster (Princeton Della)"],"techniques":["De novo binder design by AF2 hallucination with gradient descent on logits","All-atom diffusion backbone generation around a fixed ligand","Inverse folding / sequence design","Hotspot (epitope) prediction and patch-tightness scoring","Target difficulty scoring (0-100, Easy to Very Hard) from flexibility, globularity, hotspot patch, size, epitope chemistry","Small-molecule scoring by logP 30%, size 20%, rigidity 20%, TPSA 20%, aromaticity 10%","ODE compartment modelling of phage-host population dynamics","Poisson resampling of passage dilutions to allow true extinction","One-at-a-time parameter sensitivity ranking","Bootstrap confidence intervals over wells and leave-one-well-out robustness","Chi-square test of construct interchangeability","Regression-pinned unit testing of a scientific model"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"PITCCH, the Princeton iGEM Team CAD Competition for Hardware, is the standout: entrants were given a real open engineering problem from the project — design a 3D-printable gyroid biofilter, 20 mm diameter by 25 mm height, maximising contact surface area — along with the team's actual print parameters. It ran free, fully asynchronous and online in a junior division (grades 5-12) and an open division (18+), explicitly permitted responsible AI use, and drew 42 registered submissions from the United States, United Kingdom and Mexico, of which 26 passed dimensional and printability screening before being sliced and computationally ranked by total surface area. Announcement emails went to thousands more via high school superintendents, undergraduate engineering directors and makerspaces, each carrying a project description and iGEM links. The team frames it as citizen science and backed that framing with credit: two winning designs appear on the wiki with the designers named on the attribution form, and the registration terms stated plainly that the team reserves the right to draw inspiration from submissions with attribution. They also postered at symposiums and presented at bioethics exchanges, and at the US-China iGEM Human Practices Summit showed their halted immunology work as a case study in how to pivot.","ihp":"This is the strongest documented chain of design changes of any team here, and several of them cut capability rather than adding it. Dr Bowen Jing's technical advice seeded SymPlify and his steric clash filter was adopted, but the team then cut AlphaFold3 from the pipeline entirely because Dr Martin Pacesa argued it often mispredicts binding sites; Pacesa also supplied PeSTo as the hotspot engine and, after the team ran competing designs to compare, AF2 monomer was implemented on his side of the argument. Dr Reynold Panettieri talked them out of sequestering IL-6 — their original software benchmark target — by pointing out IL-6's dual-sided effects on blood cell production and T-cell regulation, redirecting them toward a niche where cheaper-than-mAb production is the real advantage and toward naming one indication and working from there, which is exactly how the final applications are framed. Dr Douglas Galasko's distinction between biomarkers and toxins moved them off amyloid-beta for Alzheimer's and into the Parkinson's and rabies work. Two multiple myeloma patients, interviewed under Princeton IRB Protocol #20003, independently said the word 'virus' for a bacteriophage was a problem — one noting roughly 80% of myeloma patients are treated in community settings and take their doctor's word — which changed how the team describes the project in all outreach. A former FDA biologics deputy director, Dr Celia Witten, pointed them to CBER's phage therapy work; EPA Office of Pesticide Programs and OPPT staff sent them to the statutes; and marine microbiologist Dr Stephen Giovannoni plus phage ecologist Prof Forest Rohwer turned the Cg-Defensin antimicrobial worry into a specific planned avidity-comparison assay. Dr John Wnek, who conserves Diamondback terrapins, chose their environmental targets: microcystins and BPA, because nearby New Jersey plants discharge effluent half a mile offshore with no process for xenobiotics.","realWorld":"The regulatory path is mapped rather than asserted: phage therapy under FDA CBER for the clinical arms, and statute-level grounding for the environmental arm after meetings with EPA Office of Pesticide Programs and Office of Pollution Prevention and Toxics, with the team careful to state that nothing should be read as EPA guidance. Containment is designed for deployment — the phage stays inside a closed water treatment facility bound to a biodegradable PLA filter so it is retrievable and localised, with macrophage clearance via VHH7 as the biosafety route for therapeutic use. The commercial argument is reconfiguration cost: because capture specificity lives in one genetic part on pVIII, changing target means changing that part and not the media, housing, attachment chemistry or capital cycle, which is the case made against granular activated carbon for PFAS where GAC retains poorly over realistic service lifetimes. Work is mapped to SDG 3.4, 6.3 and 9. Continuation is concrete: further IRB submissions to interview melanoma patients and Parkinson's patients and caregivers, the Rohwer avidity assay, qPCR of M13KO7 copy number to settle the model's most sensitive parameter, and phage hydrogels as an alternative anchor that would remove the Cg-Defensin concern altogether.","partners":["Dr Martin Pacesa (BindCraft author)","Dr Bowen Jing (computational biology)","Dr Reynold Panettieri, Rutgers Translational Medicine and Science","Dr Jared Radbel, Robert Wood Johnson Medical School","Dr Douglas Galasko (neurology)","Dr Celia Witten, former deputy director, FDA biologics center","US EPA Office of Pesticide Programs and Office of Pollution Prevention and Toxics","Dr John Wnek (environmental conservation, Diamondback terrapin)","Dr Stephen Giovannoni (marine microbiology)","Prof Forest Rohwer (marine phage ecology)","Prof Ned Wingreen and Lawrence Abad (phage biology)","Prof Jared Toettcher (faculty PI, IRB Protocol #20003)","Princeton Institutional Biosafety Committee","Multiple myeloma patient advocacy organisations","US-China iGEM Human Practices Summit","IDT, GenScript, ODI (sponsors)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6295/wiki/figures/symplify-hotspots-protein.avif","caption":"SymPlify's hotspot selection screen: the protein target rendered in 3D and coloured by PeSTo binding probability, with top hotspot residues listed by confidence so low-scoring ones can be removed to leave the focused patch of three to five that BindCraft works best with.","credit":"Team Princeton, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6295/wiki/figures/symplify-filtered-designs.avif","caption":"SymPlify's ranking and filtering view, where accepted binder designs are sorted by AlphaFold confidence metrics and PyRosetta physical scores and can be downloaded as .pdb files with or without GS linkers.","credit":"Team Princeton, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6295/wiki/figures/hardware-pitcch-surface-area.avif","caption":"Ranked printable surface area for the 26 PITCCH entries that passed dimensional and printability screening, plotted against reference geometries — public CAD submissions evaluated computationally and used to pick the biofilter geometry.","credit":"Team Princeton, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6295/wiki/figures/saf-macrophage-uptake-gfp.avif","caption":"Overlaid phase-contrast and green-fluorescence micrograph of cultured macrophages after incubation with GFP-expressing phage, showing uptake by the VHH7 clearance arm. Scale bar 150 micrometres.","credit":"Team Princeton, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy PITCCH: hand the public a genuine, unsolved sub-problem from your own project with real constraints and real print parameters, judge entries computationally on a single objective metric, then credit the winners by name and actually build their design in. It converts outreach into contributed engineering at almost no cost, and it works for high-school students because spatial creativity, not synthetic biology training, is what the task rewards. Pair it with Princeton's habit of writing down which model parameters are measured, derived or merely assumed — and publishing the history of their own corrected mistakes — which is the cheapest credibility a student dry lab can buy.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/design-rationale","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/measurement","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/hardware","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/application","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/sustainability","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/results","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/parts","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/safety-and-security","https://2026.igem.wiki/princeton/judging"],"rank":205},{"team":"Baltimore","id":"6329","section":"High School","country":"United States","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/baltimore/","projectTitle":"KeraTINT","tagline":"A chromoprotein-keratin-binding-peptide fusion that colours hair and repairs it in the same step","summary":"Baltimore Biocrew, based at the Baltimore Underground Science Space community lab, engineered fusion proteins joining chromoproteins (aeblue, UM) to keratin-binding peptides so that a semi-permanent dye deposits colour while reinforcing the hair shaft. The entrepreneurship work was run through iGEM's Venture Creation Labs curriculum, the same incubator route their 2023 project RhizeUp took, and it produced an OKR table with numeric key results, a scored risk register, a US-specific patent strategy and a competitor map. Market evidence rests on two IRB-approved consumer surveys (56 then 54 responses, translated into Chinese and Filipino) plus named interviews with a cosmetics founder, two L'Oreal North America hair-colour-lab staff, a cosmetology student and the Boeke Lab at NYU Langone. They were nominated for Best Entrepreneurship in the High School section.","awards":["Best Entrepreneurship — Nominee (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"iGEM Venture Creation Labs curriculum as the scaffold, Golden Circle, Unique Value Proposition, Lean Business Model Canvas, SWOT, a seven-phase Objectives and Key Results table with business-day estimates and fixed-cost lines, a risk register with a published 1-5 impact x 1-5 likelihood scoring rubric and named owners, direct and indirect and future competitor tiers, and the iGEM judging ballot answered question by question.","dataviz":"Interactive click-through SWOT, a nine-slide pitch deck embedded as a carousel, a lean canvas, a long-term impact-assessment graphic, a 2027-2029 timeline, and a horizontally scrollable OKR and risk table. Survey results are reported as percentages and cross-tabulated by language group and age.","datascience":"Two-round survey analysis with a genuine iteration: a bimodal distribution on grey-coverage priority in Survey 1 (a quarter rating it 1, nearly half rating it 10) was hypothesised to be two age cohorts, and Survey 2 confirmed it by adding age, hair type and prior chemical-treatment questions. Reported figures include 64.3 percent rating less hair damage a 10/10 priority, 89 percent rating it 8 or above, 50 percent using at-home boxed kits and only 25.9 percent relying solely on salons.","software":"","stack":["Google Forms","chromoprotein-keratin-binding peptide fusion constructs"],"techniques":["fusion protein design","colour retention and wash-cycle testing (planned)","cytotoxicity assay development (started, not completed)","pH and thermal stability assessment (literature-based)"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Surveys were deliberately multilingual, translated into Chinese and Filipino to reach their own communities, and the results split cleanly by language and age in ways that changed the product thesis: younger English-speaking respondents wanted fashion blues, pinks and purples, which happens to be what their chromoproteins produce, while older Chinese and Filipino respondents wanted grey coverage and natural shades. They also found that the phrase 'genetically engineered' on a label worried more respondents than it attracted, with several linking it to harmful chemicals or illness, and responded by planning a consumer FAQ booklet distributed through partner salons instead of a call centre. They presented at the July 2026 Mid-Atlantic Meetup alongside eight other regional teams.","ihp":"A Closed-Loop Framework structured each stakeholder conversation into relevance, insight, integration and future question. IRB approval from the BUGSS Board of Directors was obtained before surveying, which is unusual rigour at high-school level. Specific conversations changed specific decisions: Savannah Shumaker (chemist, co-founder and CEO of Eight Count Cosmetics) pushed branding toward audience values and simplified their science communication, and suggested contacting cosmetology schools, which led to Rachel Lu; Lindsay Menzer and Yun Yong Jang (L'Oreal Hair North America, Head of Hair Color Lab) flagged patenting during early testing, which launched the US patent-strategy investigation; the Boeke Lab at NYU Langone recommended storage conditions for chromoproteins, prompting a preservation study; and a question from another team at the Mid-Atlantic Meetup about pH and temperature sensitivity, which they had deprioritised, is what sent them to the Boeke Lab in the first place.","realWorld":"This is a credible early-stage venture plan with the finance section still hollow. What is genuinely strong is the execution scaffolding. The OKR table is the best artefact: it sets falsifiable targets with costs and durations attached, including catalogue 15 salons statewide, a minimum of 10 qualitative interviews, at least 5 Letters of Intent, 10 investor pitches, 10,000 dollars from stakeholder investment plus research grants, cell viability above 70 percent in cytotoxicity assays, dye longevity of 40 wash cycles, protein stability at 50 degrees C, 1,000 sales per month and two new production labs in other mid-Atlantic states. Named funding targets are real and local (Wexford SciTech Venture Fund, TEDCO grants, Connect Labs at 4MLK as prospective lab space, youth competitions such as Blue Ocean, Paradigm and Slingshot). Crucially, every one of these is still an aspiration: the Letters of Intent are a key result, not a signed document; the grant is applied for, not won; there is no registered entity, though forming an LLC is floated inside the liability risk row. The regulatory path is the most concretely researched part of the whole analysis and is credible: they identify that the FDA Office of Cosmetics and Colors certifies coal-tar and synthetic colour additives but that a chromoprotein appears to fall into neither bucket, name 21 CFR parts 73 and 74 subpart C, name the Color Certifications Inquiry Branch as the body to ask, and note that certified and uncertified colours carry legally distinct names. The IP strategy is equally specific and actually actionable: a provisional patent on the aeblue-keratin fusion first, converted to a non-provisional within twelve months, with USPTO micro-entity status claimed for an 80 percent fee reduction, and they check their own eligibility against the 262,380 dollar gross-income threshold. Market sizing, by contrast, is the weakest link, resting on Grand View Research hair-colour market reports rather than any bottom-up estimate, with no price point, no cost of goods and no unit economics anywhere. Continuation is plausible rather than secured: they state plainly that as a high-school iGEM team they can never test on human hair, so KeraTINT must pursue third-party testing or operate independently of iGEM after the competition, and they sequence cytotoxicity, pH and thermal assays as the first post-Jamboree work. The precedent helps their case, since the same team's 2023 RhizeUp project went through the same incubator.","partners":["iGEM Venture Creation Labs / Venture Foundry (incubator curriculum)","Baltimore Underground Science Space (BUGSS) community lab and its Board of Directors (IRB approval)","Savannah Shumaker, chemist and co-founder/CEO, Eight Count Cosmetics","Lindsay Menzer and Yun Yong Jang, L'Oreal Hair North America, Head of Hair Color Lab","Rachel Lu, cosmetology student and freelance makeup artist","Dr. Jef Boeke and Aleksandra Wudzinska, Boeke Lab, NYU Langone Health","Mid-Atlantic Meetup regional iGEM teams"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6329/wiki/entrepreneurship/lbmc.avif","caption":"KeraTINT lean business model canvas identifying local salons and cosmetic companies as first customers","credit":"Team Baltimore, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6329/wiki/entrepreneurship/timeline.avif","caption":"Commercialisation timeline from 2027 stakeholder re-engagement to a 2029 salon product roll-out","credit":"Team Baltimore, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6329/wiki/entrepreneurship/pitch-deck/2.avif","caption":"Slide from the nine-slide KeraTINT pitch deck covering market sizing and distribution channels","credit":"Team Baltimore, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6329/wiki/entrepreneurship/impact-assessment.avif","caption":"Long-term positive and negative impact assessment for consumers and the wider hair-care market","credit":"Team Baltimore, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Steal the OKR table: every commercialisation objective gets a numeric key result, a business-day estimate and a fixed-cost line, including scientific gates such as cell viability above 70 percent and dye longevity of 40 wash cycles, so a business plan becomes a checklist a successor team can inherit. Also copy getting institutional IRB approval before running consumer surveys, which both protects participants and makes the resulting data citable.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/baltimore/","https://2026.igem.wiki/baltimore/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/baltimore/human-practices/"],"rank":7},{"team":"NJU-China","id":"6337","section":"Undergrad","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/","projectTitle":"Starase Atlas — Atlas-BRIDGE, Atlas-COMPASS and Atlas-EDGE for enzyme discovery","tagline":"Given a target reaction and limited experimental resources, which enzyme candidates should a researcher test first?","summary":"NJU-China built a three-part platform around one decision: which enzyme to test next. Atlas-BRIDGE (Broad Retrieval with Inference-Driven Gated Experts) is a dual-tower contrastive retrieval model that will rank any protein it can encode, then lets specialist 'expert' models and two kinds of memory nudge that ranking only where their evidence genuinely applies; Atlas-COMPASS is a natural-language agent front end that resolves entities before it computes anything; Atlas-EDGE is a MySQL/graph knowledge base of compound nodes and enzyme edges built from UniProt, Rhea and ChEBI. The model's recommendation was then taken to the bench: M5AW86 was predicted and confirmed to convert FPP to beta-farnesene by GC-MS, TLC and 1H NMR against empty-vector controls. It won Best Software & AI Project and was nominated for Best Model.","awards":["Best Model — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Software & AI Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Atlas-BRIDGE is a machine-learning information-retrieval model, not a kinetic one, and its central design idea is worth copying regardless of domain: separate *eligibility to be ranked* from *availability of evidence*. The base model ('Broad') is a two-tower encoder — one tower for protein sequence, one for reaction transformation — trained with a multi-positive contrastive objective that respects the many-to-many nature of enzyme data, L_MP = lambda*L_R2E + (1-lambda)*L_E2R, where each directional loss sums over the full set of known positives rather than treating a single pair as the truth. Because an unrecorded enzyme-reaction pair is not a true negative, they apply positive-unlabeled (PU) learning plus sequence-cluster masking to decide which pairs are even admitted for contrast, and because experimental screening only tests the top of a list they add an explicit head-ranking hinge, L_head = softplus(n_K - p* + m), pushing the best positive above the K-th admissible negative, with hard negatives mined from the candidates the current model confuses most. The final score is additive and auditable: S = S_Broad + alpha_LT*Delta_LT + alpha_epi*Delta_epi + sum_k g_k*Delta_k. The Gated Experts are plug-in models carrying functional, structural/mechanistic or family/domain evidence; each must publish candidate-wise scores *and an availability mask*, has its applicability gate, normalisation and maximum correction magnitude locked on separate validation data before any external test, and reverts exactly to the prior ranking wherever its evidence is missing — so absent data is never silently read as a low score. Long-term memory replays the training-cutoff catalytic graph at inference, projecting 16 annotated protein neighbours and 8 annotated reaction neighbours back into the Broad space with query-conditioned permission weights fitted on 5,216 annotated development associations, and it only re-sorts the top 1,000 Broad candidates. Episodic memory handles positives that appear *after* the training cutoff (new curation, new assays, a collaborator's private hit) without retraining anything: each support is encoded in the frozen Broad space and combined by parameter-free attention, a_i = softmax(zscore(q^T s_i)), m_epi = sum a_i s_i, with authority scaled by support count, internal coherence and conflict with the baseline; positives already in the training graph are stripped out first to prevent double-counting with long-term memory. Atlas-COMPASS wraps this in a DeepSeek-API-driven agent that first resolves every reaction, protein and compound in a plain-language question to a Rhea / UniProt / ChEBI record and shows a confirmation card the user must approve before anything is computed, then plans the retrieval, and can additionally run thermodynamic Max-min Driving Force analysis and E. coli iML1515 genome-scale flux checks on candidate pathways when asked.","dataviz":"The visual argument is carried by the evaluation figures rather than by decoration. Benchmark comparisons are plotted as grouped MRR / Hit@10 / Hit@20 bars against EnzymeCAGE on two external test sets, with the harder Orphan-335 set shown alongside Enzyme-405 so the reader sees where the gain is largest. The four-configuration study is drawn as a controlled 2x2 (reference, coverage-only, ranker-only, complete system) in both retrieval directions, so the figure itself separates 'we found more candidates' from 'we ranked them better'. Ablation panels remove one evidence block at a time in both directions. Seed-count sensitivity is shown both as performance-versus-number-of-positive-seeds line plots under two evaluation protocols (random-positive and the much stricter protein-cluster-cold) and as grouped Hit@3/10/20 bars for one, two and three seeds. The model page also renders the architecture itself — dual-tower geometry, anchored reranking that visibly protects the base order, and query-level gating of each expert — as labelled schematics, and the five development cycles are laid out as a descending staircase so the reader walks the DBTL history. Atlas-EDGE's own interface is the other half of the dataviz: a hand-built SVG graph in which compounds are nodes and enzymes are directional edges, with composite edges that collapse multiple enzymes between two compounds and expand on click, click-through compound and enzyme info cards, zoom, drag, filtering, path highlighting and URL-synced view state.","datascience":"Evaluation is the strongest part of this project and is unusually self-sceptical. External benchmarking used the EnzymeCAGE authors' own test sets under their protocol: 21,505 training-unseen positive relationships ranked over the full encodable universe of 185,918 protein candidates (11,081 reaction candidates in the reverse direction). Reported gains: Enzyme-405 MRR +16.0% and Hit@10 +4.55pp; Orphan-335 MRR +44.0%, Hit@10 +17.32pp, Hit@20 +18.51pp. Four ablation studies follow, including a difficulty-balanced re-aggregation that freezes every prediction, candidate set and rank and changes *only* the averaging — giving equal weight to seen, protein-cold, reaction-cold and double-cold exposure classes and then to the graph-degree strata within each — explicitly to stop frequent, highly connected, easy relations dominating a micro-average. Domain experts were tested only on queries where they are applicable, with deterministic family held-out splits for P450 and phosphatase and a frozen semantic applicability gate selecting 18 of 1,042 strict double-cold queries for terpene synthases; gains were P450 MRR 0.0394 to 0.0503, phosphatase 0.3012 to 0.3077, TPS 0.1225 to 0.3114 with Hit@10 11.11% to 33.33%, candidate sets and core scores held fixed. Wet-lab closure: M5AW86 was recommended for FPP to beta-farnesene and confirmed by a converging evidence chain (differential GC-MS peak at ~5.5 min with base peak m/z 69, fragments at 41/81/93/107/121/135 and a low-abundance molecular ion at m/z 204; TLC spots absent from empty-vector controls; 1H NMR olefinic signals at delta 6.3-6.4 and 5.0-5.3 ppm matched to a literature reference). They also publish a disconfirmation: for D9XD61 the model predicted (+)-T-muurolol but the isolated product was identified as 7-epi-alpha-eudesmol, and they say so. Preliminary GC-MS hit rate was 7 of 14 recommended candidates (50%). The interface discipline matches: the 0-100 displayed model score is explicitly documented as 'not a catalytic success rate', not activity or yield, not comparable across retrieval routes, and a high rank means 'deserves priority verification, not that it has been biologically proven'; 'unknown' in a compatibility cell means missing records, not incompatibility.","software":"Both instruments are Apache 2.0 open source in the official iGEM GitLab repository, deployable with git clone plus bash scripts/bootstrap.sh, setup_release.sh --download and --check, then manage.sh configure-key / start / status. Linux with Python 3.12 is the validated environment, ~5 GiB for model assets, WSL2 for Windows, macOS explicitly flagged as not validated — and crucially no GPU is required, since validation and inference run on CPU. The DeepSeek API key is stored in a git-ignored runtime directory and the docs tell deployers to put the service behind HTTPS with access control, rate limiting, log monitoring and API quotas, and to explain how external model services handle data. Atlas-EDGE is a full production stack: React + TypeScript + Vite front end (hand-drawn SVG graph, History API URL sync, localStorage session persistence, AbortController to cancel stale searches), FastAPI + Uvicorn + Pydantic + async SQLAlchemy/aiomysql backend on a /api/v1 prefix, MySQL 8.0.46, a four-round UniProt REST crawl plus Rhea SPARQL and ChEBI flat files, pandas/RDKit ETL into entity and association tables with a unified sha1-keyed search index, local NCBI BLAST+ (blastp/makeblastdb, run in a thread with a mutex guarding database construction and an sha1 cache key), Ketcher for draw-a-molecule structure search, and DeepSolNet run locally with original weights to generate per-protein solubility scores used as a search filter.","stack":["Python 3.11 / 3.12","PyTorch (dual-tower encoders)","FastAPI","Uvicorn","Pydantic","SQLAlchemy asyncio + aiomysql","MySQL 8.0.46","React + TypeScript + Vite","pandas","RDKit","NCBI BLAST+","Ketcher","DeepSolNet","DeepSeek API","UniProt REST","Rhea SPARQL","ChEBI flat files","Pfam","cobrapy / iML1515 genome-scale model","scikit-learn / random forest / gradient boosting (experts)"],"techniques":["dual-tower contrastive retrieval","multi-positive InfoNCE-style loss","positive-unlabeled learning","sequence-cluster negative masking","hard-negative mining","listwise head-ranking hinge loss","mixture-of-experts gating with availability masks","retrieval-augmented long-term graph memory","parameter-free attention episodic memory (few-shot, training-free update)","protein language model embeddings","differential reaction fingerprints (DRFP)","learning-to-rank (LambdaMART lineage)","ablation studies","cold-start / double-cold stratified evaluation","difficulty-balanced metric re-aggregation","MRR and Hit@K ranking metrics","graph database modelling","pathway search over a directed multigraph","Max-min Driving Force thermodynamic analysis","genome-scale flux balance analysis (iML1515)","natural-language agent with entity grounding"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Nine primary-school lessons from March to July 2026 (five plant lessons, four summer science lessons), each treated as its own Design-Build-Test-Learn unit: Design fixed audience and objectives, Build made slides and materials, Test watched what pupils actually explained and where they needed help, Learn turned the observed problems into changes to the next lesson. From that they distilled SPARK as a reusable design and review lens — See, Probe, Ask, Record and Respond, Keep improving — and then applied it back onto their secondary-school intro and lab teaching and their public science conversations at Starlight Market. The handover is concrete: slides, activity materials, a lesson design checklist, guidelines for experimental and simulation activities, a feedback and evaluation form, and 'SPARK-Edu Skill', an AI-assisted guide that encodes their principles, workflow and examples so a future education team can adapt rather than restart.","ihp":"Human Practices is presented as evidence that changed the build, with a counted audit trail: 24 evidence records, 6 stakeholder lenses, 4 design shifts, 3 intended influence pathways, across academic (8), iGEM and user (4), industry (5), technology transfer (3), safety (2) and education contacts. The four shifts are stated as before/after pairs: from a black-box compatibility score to explicit experimental prioritisation; from searching known pairs to open exploration with uncertainty; from ranked names only to explainable recommendations; from a post-hoc warning to responsibility by design. The origin is traceable: in November 2025 Jing Shi pointed out that a promising enzyme record may behave differently in a new host, Fei Zhou described the preparatory work caused by scattered information, and Long Wang warned that sequence similarity is not function — which is why the platform separates recorded associations (the factual layer) from model candidates (the prediction layer) in every view. The team also took on the dual-use question directly: if an AI tool makes finding functional enzymes easier, which questions should it decline to answer lightly, how should high-risk queries be handled, and what may private data be used for.","realWorld":"The entrepreneurship case is for a screening *service* rather than another database: computational screening carried through to wet-lab validation so the customer receives a confirmed candidate, with the pipeline and its documentation left behind as a reusable toolkit and the methods and data kept public. Numbers are stated with their baselines: enzyme-to-reaction accuracy 15.32% (up from 8.47% in the team's own historical runs), a 1.92x top-20 hit rate against CLIPZyme (15.97% vs 8.33%) over 166,202 benchmark candidates, a 50% preliminary GC-MS hit rate (7 of 14), an estimated core enterprise TAM of RMB 14.42M annually in the baseline forecast, and a proposed tiered price range of RMB 4.3k to 319.5k per year from Academic to Enterprise Advanced. They are candid that the current demo only accepts target reactions and candidate proteins and returns rankings, with traceability, version management and full decision support still to come. The stated audiences are research labs (inspectable enzyme discovery), the iGEM community (reflective HP practice) and the bio-manufacturing ecosystem (governed, compatible pilots).","partners":["Nanjing University School of Life Sciences","Nanjing University School of Intelligence Science and Technology","Nanjing University School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering","Nanjing University School of Software","Vazyme","WuXi","BioSphere"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6337/wiki/model/model.avif","caption":"Atlas-BRIDGE architecture: a shared dual-tower retrieval geometry that will score any encodable protein or reaction, with query-level gated experts and dual (long-term graph plus episodic) memory applying additive corrections only inside their valid scope.","credit":"Team NJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6337/wiki/model/7.avif","caption":"The Broad Retrieval dual-tower architecture for bidirectional enzyme-reaction ranking: one tower encodes protein sequence, the other the reaction transformation, trained with a multi-positive contrastive loss plus a head-ranking constraint so the top of the list — the part experiments actually test — is sharpened.","credit":"Team NJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6337/wiki/model/seed-performance-lines-v2.avif","caption":"Performance as a function of the number of positive seeds, plotted under two evaluation protocols — random-positive and the much stricter protein-cluster-cold — so the reader can see how much of the apparent gain survives when close homologs of the known positives are withheld.","credit":"Team NJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6337/wiki/model/9.avif","caption":"Query-level gating of the functional, structural/mechanistic, family/domain and memory experts. Each expert must emit an availability mask as well as scores, and reverts exactly to the prior ranking wherever its evidence is missing, so absent data is never read as a low score.","credit":"Team NJU-China, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Make every model output carry an availability mask, so 'we have no evidence about this candidate' and 'this candidate scores badly' are never the same number — and separate the factual layer (what a database records) from the prediction layer (what your model guesses) in every table and figure you publish. Then re-aggregate your headline metric with equal weight across difficulty strata to check your result is not just the easy cases.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/compass/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/edge/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/proof/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/education/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nju-china/entrepreneurship/"],"rank":33},{"team":"ASIJ-Tokyo","id":"6338","section":"High School","country":"Japan","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/","projectTitle":"Project 茶(Sa)kure-it-all","tagline":"Yeast cocultures that produce four plant specialized metabolites from sakura and tea - sakuranetin, quercetin, EGCG and L-theanine - without the plant","summary":"ASIJ-Tokyo engineered Saccharomyces cerevisiae to make four plant specialized metabolites tied to Japanese culture: sakuranetin, quercetin, EGCG and L-theanine. Rather than one overloaded strain, they split pathways across cocultured strains at optimised ratios (7:3 for sakuranetin and quercetin, 5:3:2 for the three-strain EGCG system), with every enzyme cassette behind a GAL promoter and a constitutive pTEF1 fluorescent reporter so strain ratios can be verified before induction. Their education portfolio is unusually broad for a high-school team and explicitly gap-driven: each activity was created to fill a named gap they had identified. They contributed 34 new parts, a no-HPLC semi-quantitative screening workflow and a low-cost DIY bioreactor with full build instructions, all aimed at other resource-limited high-school teams.","awards":["Best Part Collection — Nominee (High School)","Best Education — Nominee (High School)","Best Village Project — Nominee (Space / Foundational Advance / Software & AI / Biomanufacturing)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Stakeholder analysis structured into three domains (agricultural supply, health and consumer needs, production and commercial viability); Duke iGEM's Risk and Research Ethics Assessment tool used as an external bioethics compliance check.","dataviz":"Stakeholder map figure grouping identified issues and stakeholders into the three domains; coculture architecture diagrams.","datascience":"A semi-quantitative screening workflow designed specifically for teams without HPLC access, published as a contribution. In-silico modelling of a proposed active-transport system was explored with guidance from a Johns Hopkins biomedical engineering student, then explicitly abandoned because without HPLC the team could not measure intra- versus extracellular compound concentrations to validate it - a documented negative result.","software":"Village 茶(Sa)kura: a free Animal Crossing-style educational game built in Unity and coded in C# over two years with the Japanese technology entertainment company ABAL Inc. Players receive villager requests (for example 'bring me anti-inflammatory sakura cream'), scavenge ingredients in the Sakura Forest, read recipes at crafting stations, synthesise items in in-game labs and return them, with up to 10 encoded pathways; published free on itch.io with a game manual. Also a memory card game, a 'What is?' YouTube explainer series, and The Catalyst: Science & Curiosity, a student science news/publication website.","stack":["Unity Engine","C#","itch.io distribution","YouTube","web publishing platform (The Catalyst)","HPLC (via International Christian University)"],"techniques":["yeast coculture at optimised strain ratios","dual-plasmid selection (pRS316/URA3 + pRS315/LEU2 on SC -Ura -Leu dropout)","GAL-inducible enzyme cassettes","pTEF1 constitutive fluorescent reporter for ratio verification","modular pathway splitting across strains","DIY low-cost bioreactor build","CRISPR-Cas9 teaching lab","agarose gel electrophoresis","strawberry and banana DNA extraction"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"ASIJ-Tokyo opened its education work with a diagnostic question - who is not yet part of the synthetic biology conversation - and then built one artefact per identified gap, which makes the portfolio unusually legible. Gap: low public awareness of sakura's health properties, addressed by an Earth Day booth (22 April 2025 and 2026) where elementary, middle and high school students painted flower pots and took home sakura seeds while being briefed on anti-inflammatory and antioxidant properties, reaching 70+ participants each year, plus a Sakura & Matcha Goodies recipe book whose recipes each carry a 'the science behind' blurb. Gap: almost no synthetic biology video content pitched at high school level, addressed by a 'What is?' YouTube series running since 2023 covering bioethics, cellular transport, yeast in synthetic biology, metabolic engineering, genetic switches, cocultures, biosensors and cellular fluorescence, deliberately sequenced so concepts build on each other. Gap: little attention to memory degradation in Japan's ageing population, addressed by a memory card game run at a senior home on 13 October 2026, using six cards depicting cluttered domestic interiors with recall questions (colour of the couch, number of pencils) across repeated rounds individually and in groups of 2-3; the team was scrupulous about its limits, stating plainly that this was a small pilot and not a clinical study, and left the game with the facility and its nurses as a low-pressure cognitive exercise. Gap: no easy way for teachers outside iGEM or biology to run these activities, addressed by a free downloadable workbook of seven tested lesson plans covering DNA, natural pigment, cell modelling, chromatography, biomaterials and polymer creation, refined so that the labs connect into a holistic overview, plus The Catalyst: Science & Curiosity, a newspaper-style website that also acts as a publication platform where any secondary school student can publish science writing from 300-word pieces to 20-page experimental reports. The flagship format is Village 茶(Sa)kura, an Animal Crossing-style Unity/C# game co-developed over two years with ABAL Inc., a Japanese technology entertainment company, iterated against industry-expert feedback and published free on itch.io with a manual. Direct teaching reached a wide spread of ages: a four-year-running CRISPR-Cas9 and gel electrophoresis lab for 8th graders built into their sickle cell anaemia unit (13-14 May 2025 and 12-13 May 2026, including why researchers target the BCL11A enhancer rather than the mutation itself); DNA extraction workshops for over 30 students at Tokyo Bay International School and Montessori School Tokyo in Azabu (31 March and 22 July 2026); the same experiment with bananas for 80+ children aged 5-10 daily at the school's Summer Day Camp, deliberately reaching local and non-international-school children; a 60+ child booth with leaf-rubbing and plant-engineering discussion at Aoyama Gakuin's Global Day (21 October 2025), tied to the UN SDGs and to English-language learning; a Winterfest campus-wide CSI escape-room-style DNA mystery (4 December 2025) with three stations - DNA matching to eliminate suspects, leaf-morphology comparison to locate the crime scene, and gel electrophoresis to identify the culprit; a Community Health Fair obstacle-course race (11 April 2025) where participants acted as skin cells carrying sakuranetin past crawl-space 'constricted blood vessels' and hurdle 'blockages' on a two-minute timer; and Back to School Night in 2025 and 2026, the 2026 edition adding a hands-on flower pipetting activity and recipe-book matcha cookies for 100+ parents, students and teachers. Evidence of iteration is explicit and honest: PCR was cut from the 8th grade lab because crucial steps ran out of time, lesson plans were reworked so activities connect, pipetting was added to Back to School Night, and the team states openly that it did not systematically record feedback this year and therefore added a feedback page to the workbook so teachers who use it can report back. Collaboration was a deliberate education strategy in its own right: activities were created or joined with eight teams across eight countries (Duke, Maastricht, Manipal, Concordia, Ruhr University Bochum, St Stephen's College Hong Kong, Patras, KCIS Xiugang) plus the iGEM Community Blackboard - contributing a scent to Manipal's Scents of the World, an abstract to Maastricht's peer-reviewed MSP Vector journal, a Pokemon-style card to RUBochum's Trading Card Project, a Japanese translation of HKSSC's food-waste comic, and both of their games to Patras's downloadable game gallery.","ihp":"Four stakeholder engagements reshaped the project, each with a recorded design consequence. Tea producer Masahiro Okutomi of Sayama-cha Okutomien (10 April 2026) explained that overseas matcha orders have risen sharply while new tea plants take at least five years to mature and processing equipment three years to arrive, and that L-theanine is what gives matcha much of its quality - so extracting it from leaves for supplements would leave farmers with a degraded product. This directly strengthened the decision to produce L-theanine in yeast rather than extract it, and reframed the team's positioning from replacement to 'an extra source that works beside farms'. He also noted Japanese companies market catechins like EGCG while consumers do not know what L-theanine does, which changed the team's communication plan to explain each compound separately starting with L-theanine. Yukari Kawahito of Sakura Science Beauty (May 2026) introduced transdermal patches as a delivery format the team had not considered and opened a second business route of selling compounds to companies with existing formulation capability. Vikki Adams (Johns Hopkins) advised on an in-silico active-transport model that was ultimately dropped for lack of HPLC validation. A visit to the six-generation family-run Maruichi Mizuno Tea Farm in Shizuoka (16 June 2026) showed how the farm covers plants with sheets, harvests three times a year, processes its own hojicha and sencha and is now seeking matcha manufacturers to benefit from the boom - confirming producers already market L-theanine and EGCG benefits while demand for fermentation-derived ingredients remains untested. The visit was filmed as a vlog.","realWorld":"The project's framing is a supply constraint, not a novelty: sakura-flavoured products appear across Japanese supermarkets every spring and vanish by May because the raw material does, sakuranetin being bound to one of the shortest harvest windows in agriculture, while tea faces the opposite problem of a demand boom farms cannot meet. Contributions were deliberately designed for resource-limited high-school teams: a reusable coculture architecture where a future team can keep Strain N unchanged and only build its own downstream strain, 34 new Registry parts, a semi-quantitative screening workflow usable without HPLC, and a low-cost DIY bioreactor with full build instructions. The team hosted six students from St Mary's International School to build a biosensor, gained HPLC access through a bioanalytical chemistry professor at International Christian University, and will give an opening keynote alongside university professors at ASIJ's first semi-annual science fair on 14 November 2026 to roughly 100 students and teachers.","partners":["ABAL Inc. (Japanese technology entertainment company, two-year Unity game co-development)","Tokyo Bay International School","Montessori School Tokyo, Azabu","Aoyama Gakuin (Global Day)","International Christian University (bioanalytical chemistry professor, HPLC access)","St Mary's International School","Recolle Team Hayate (STEM Racing student team)","Sayama-cha Okutomien (tea producer Masahiro Okutomi)","Sakura Science Beauty (Yukari Kawahito)","Maruichi Mizuno Tea Farm, Shizuoka","Johns Hopkins University (Vikki Adams)","itch.io (game distribution)","Japan iGEM meet-up at Hiroo Gakuen (UTokyo, TUS, Waseda, Keio, Oakridge, HG)","iGEM teams: Duke, Maastricht, Manipal, Concordia, RUBochum, HKSSC, Patras, KCIS Xiugang"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6338/wiki/education/crafting-screenshot.avif","caption":"A crafting station in Village 茶(Sa)kura, the free Unity/C# synthetic biology game built with ABAL Inc. and published on itch.io","credit":"Team ASIJ-Tokyo, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6338/wiki/education/seniorhouse.avif","caption":"Running the six-card memory game with residents at a senior home, 13 October 2026","credit":"Team ASIJ-Tokyo, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6338/wiki/education/winterfest-igem.avif","caption":"The campus-wide CSI-themed DNA mystery stations at Winterfest, 4 December 2025","credit":"Team ASIJ-Tokyo, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6338/wiki/education/igem-backtoschool-2-1.avif","caption":"Back to School Night 2026: hands-on flower pipetting activity for 100+ parents, students and teachers","credit":"Team ASIJ-Tokyo, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6338/wiki/education/booth2.avif","caption":"The Earth Day booth, where 70+ students painted sakura flower pots and took home seeds","credit":"Team ASIJ-Tokyo, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6338/wiki/education/youtube-ss.avif","caption":"The 'What is?' YouTube explainer series, built to fill the gap in synthetic biology video pitched at high school level","credit":"Team ASIJ-Tokyo, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Start from a written list of named gaps (which audience, which missing resource) and build exactly one artefact per gap, then package the reusable ones - a seven-lesson tested workbook with a built-in feedback page, a free game, a sequenced video series - so teachers outside synthetic biology can run them without you.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/","https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/description","https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/contribution","https://2026.igem.wiki/asij-tokyo/entrepreneurship"],"rank":21},{"team":"Peking","id":"6366","section":"Undergrad","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/","projectTitle":"TermiTox — engineered yeast baits for species-specific termite colony control","tagline":"Two engineered yeast strains, modelled from molecular dynamics to whole-colony collapse, to protect wooden heritage buildings without broad-spectrum pesticide.","summary":"Peking built a genuinely three-scale model of a termite bait. Strain A delivers a pH-gated listeriolysin O mutant that lyses the hindgut protozoa a termite depends on for cellulose digestion; Strain B releases hairpin RNA under hindgut hypoxia to silence RsEG (a termite cellulase) and Hexamerin-1 (a caste-development regulator). The Molecular Model used all-atom MD to verify the H311A-LLO safety premise, the Individual Model is a cohort-ODE system calibrated by MAP fitting against quantitative data extracted from 43 papers, and the Colony Model is a spatially explicit agent-based stochastic simulation of trophallaxis through a two-layer nest network with caste structure. The colony model produced a non-obvious and testable deployment schedule that the team then designed hardware for, plus a dose and collapse-time estimate anchored to published hexaflumuron field trials.","awards":["Best Model — Nominee (Undergrad)","Safety and Security Award — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Conservation Project — Nominee"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Three models at three scales, each answering a different design question, and the output of each feeds the next. (1) MOLECULAR — all-atom molecular dynamics in GROMACS-style protocol on LLO (PDB 4CDB, residues 39-526) to test whether the H311A mutant is really pH-gated. PROPKA3 predicted pKa for all 141 titratable residues, identifying E247 and H311 as the residues whose protonation flips between pH 5.5 and 7.4; ten systems were built (four solution, two membrane, four crystal-contact dimers from ChimeraX packing) in CHARMM36-jul2022 with TIP3P water, 310 K V-rescale thermostat, 1 atm C-rescale barostat with semi-isotropic coupling for the membranes, 2 fs timestep, 1.2 nm non-bonded cutoff, LINCS on H-bonds, through minimisation, NVT, NPT and production. The result is quantitative: under acidic conditions the H311A acidic triad (D208/E247/D320) tightens to a D208-D320 distance of 3.94 Angstrom, while at neutral pH the distances open to 6.05-6.20 Angstrom and the salt bridges collapse, and in the membrane systems the protein sits 29 Angstrom from the phosphorus plane at pH 5.5 but drifts to 58 Angstrom at pH 7.4 by t = 20.5 ns. H311A is 17-18 Angstrom from the triad and never touches it, so the mechanism claimed is allosteric. (2) INDIVIDUAL — a cohort-structured ODE model. Because repeated bait feeding and in-gut yeast division create cells of different ages, yeast is divided into cohorts, each with its own hindgut-age clock; founder cells arrive with preloaded payload Q_0 while hindgut-born cells start empty and must build payload. All A and B cohorts share one logistic hindgut carrying capacity, b_s = r_s - [r_s]_+ Y_total/K_Y. A DAN1-calibrated hypoxia clock a_D(a) = l_D + (phi_H - l_D) G_2(a; tau_D) gates payload expression and Strain B's release module. Strain A uptake uses a delayed waiting function and saturates as protozoa are depleted, with a per-yeast kill probability p = 1 - exp(-Psi_A q), and because protozoan enzymes outlive the protozoa, digestive capacity follows through a first-order lag dE_P/dt = (ln2/T_EP)(F_P - E_P). Strain B's hairpin RNA feeds a hindgut pool with first-order decay, and the gut-pool-to-knockdown step is a convolution with an empirical time kernel fitted from oral RNAi studies, X_j(t) = A_j * integral u_j(s) K_j(t-s) ds with M_j = exp(-X_j); mRNA then drives protein pools through further lags, and total digestive function is a weighted sum D_E = w_R + w_P E_P + w_C C_H. (3) COLONY — a spatially explicit, stochastic agent-based model. The nest is a two-layer network: a macro graph linking the royal chamber, six wood modules and one or two bait modules by tunnels, with each module containing a micro branching tunnel network generated by a stochastic excavation process adapted from Lee and Lee's agent-based termite tunnelling model and converted to a continuous centreline graph. Colony members are coarse-grained weighted agents (2,000 worker agents at 250 workers each, 300 larval agents at 125 each, one explicit king and 50 explicit queens) with resolution convergence checked at 1,000/150, 2,000/300 and 4,000/600. Workers run a three-state FORAGE/RETURN/DISTRIBUTE behavioural loop; when two meet, trophallaxis fires with probability P_ij = p_max * normalised_local_density * |F_i - F_j| and half the food-load difference transfers, with a one-relay-only rule for food en route to the royal chamber. Each termite tracks food load, transferable A and B payload and accumulated damage, with worker damage Delta_D = alpha_A Q_A + alpha_B Q_B + gamma_AB * 2 Q_A Q_B/(Q_A + Q_B + eps) — a harmonic-mean synergy term that only fires when both payloads are active in the same insect, with coefficients ordered alpha_A > alpha_B > gamma_AB from the individual model. Strain A damage is assigned only to workers on the biological ground that queens and larvae lack the worker hindgut protist system. The model deliberately rejects a conventional SIR epidemic formulation because nest space and caste direction are what make trophallactic spread non-uniform.","dataviz":"The headline is interactivity that is genuinely explanatory rather than ornamental: an embedded individual-model explorer where the reader sets total dose and treatment duration, presses Update model, and then drags a timeline through 42 days watching a labelled flow diagram (INPUT, YEAST, PAYLOAD, ACTION, TARGETS, HOST) light up — clicking any element in the diagram highlights that quantity's trajectory. The colony model ships six embedded simulation videos selectable by schedule (nest construction, worker movement, A-only, B-only, A+B mixed, best schedule) with a draggable progress bar and playback speed, and one video follows a single tracked worker's food load, A payload, B payload and damage in an inset as it feeds, transfers and dies. Both explorers degrade gracefully, with the page explicitly stating that the static figures below show the same modelled outcomes when JavaScript is off. The spatial figures are the best static work: Figures 8 and 9 are matrices with treatment days 10-120 as columns and worker cohorts grouped by home module as rows, showing ever-exposed fraction, payload burden in a no-damage tracer run, and functional-worker fraction — making the distance-to-bait gradient and the spatial refuges immediately visible, then showing them disappear when a second bait station is added. Stochastic outputs are reported as three-seed medians with Q25-Q75 shading, and every caption lists the parameter values used (s = 1.40, home/adjacent/global target probabilities 0.65/0.20/0.15, global bait weights 4/2/1).","datascience":"This is a parameter-provenance project. The team collected quantitative experiments from 43 papers covering yeast persistence, trophallaxis, hindgut oxygen, LLO, protozoa, oral RNAi, cellulase activity and yeast release systems, recording species, delivery route, exposure schedule, observation times, units and readout for each — and then published an EVIDENCE MAP table that states, row by row, what each dataset can estimate, what remains unresolved, and an identifiability status of Composite, Partly identifiable, Structural constraints only, Scenario only, Structure identifiable but rates unresolved, or Not identifiable. For example, LLO entering protozoan food vacuoles is marked 'Not identifiable', and A+B synergy is marked 'Not identifiable' with the honest note that no LLO-plus-RNAi study exists, so independent, additive, limiting-factor and multiplicative scenarios are all carried instead of one being asserted. The model structure was then deliberately built as the SMALLEST structure the data can constrain: when several microscopic steps were only ever observed through one final readout, they were kept together as one effective process rather than split into unidentifiable rates. Parameters were calibrated by maximum a posteriori fitting, theta_hat = argmin[-sum_e log p(y_e | H_e(x_e(theta))) - log p(theta)], jointly across studies rather than one parameter set per paper, following the biological chain (yeast dynamics, then protozoa-to-digestion, then hex RNAi, then cellu RNAi, then terminal dose-response), with directly reported quantities fixed first, multiple starting points per block, and each study keeping its own dose schedule and control. Validation is reproduction before prediction: seven published experiments were reproduced by the model before it was used to predict TermiTox. Real time was anchored by simulating a reference compound through the same two models — hexaflumuron, chosen because both individual and colony data exist — giving a model-time ratio of 137/120 = 1.14 that converts Im and Han's 56-day arena collapse into ~65 days for TermiTox at ~10^4 termites and Tsunoda's 9-month field result into ~10.3 months, with the dose converted to a bait loading of 2.03e9 cells per g dry bait, or ~5.1% w/w at 4e10 cells per g lyophilised preparation.","software":"RNAiWeave, a local web application that turns one target gene sequence into ranked, traceable hairpin RNAi candidates. It does sliding-window fragment filtering (default arm 300-400 nt, GC 30-60%, 21-nt siRNA window, all adjustable) screening out homopolymers, low complexity and tandem repeats with siRNA features computed once and reused via prefix sums; an activity estimate from four features reported in the published dsRIP study (terminal thermodynamic asymmetry, antisense self-folding, antisense GC and others); off-target screening against a local FASTA or RefSeq RNA scope that retains matching positions and records; and full-precursor folding in ViennaRNA. The structure score H is an explicit weighted formula over seven quantities (inter-arm stem coverage R at weight 0.50, linker-pairing burden J, MFE-per-nucleotide suitability E benchmarked to a project reference of 0.80 kcal/mol/nt, equilibrium probability of the MFE structure P, 21-nt unpaired-window accessibility U, overall paired fraction F, and a penalty for unexpected pairs X), so a reader can see exactly why one candidate outranked another. Environmental persistence is modelled separately as first-order decay with DT50 and DT90, with a Q10 temperature correction and literature reference scenarios (15-28 h for DvSnf7 RNA in agricultural soils, 56-72 h for non-bioactive dsRNA in water-sediment microcosms). Exports are FASTA, CSV, versioned JSON, dot-bracket and SVG. The team is scrupulous about limits: without off-target screening the output is labelled a preview ranking with sequence safety 'not assessed', and they state plainly that they have not compared knockdown efficacy or runtime against dsRIP. Model code is public at gitlab.igem.org/2026/peking/-/tree/main/Model_code.","stack":["Python","GROMACS (CHARMM36-jul2022, TIP3P)","PROPKA3","ChimeraX","ViennaRNA / RNAplot","NumPy / SciPy ODE solvers","custom agent-based simulator","local web app (RNAiWeave)","NCBI RefSeq / local BLAST-style off-target search"],"techniques":["all-atom molecular dynamics","pKa prediction and constant-protonation-state MD","membrane-bilayer simulation with semi-isotropic pressure coupling","protein-protein dimer MD for oligomerisation tendency","salt-bridge and distance trajectory analysis","cohort-structured ODE modelling","logistic growth with shared carrying capacity","delay / convolution kernel modelling of oral RNAi","first-order lag chains (mRNA to protein to function)","maximum a posteriori parameter estimation with literature priors","structural identifiability assessment","minimal-model construction from evidence","literature benchmark reproduction (7 experiments)","spatially explicit agent-based simulation","stochastic tunnel-network generation","coarse-grained weighted agents with resolution convergence check","contact-process trophallaxis modelling","multi-seed stochastic replication with quantile bands","cross-scale parameter handoff (individual to colony)","reference-compound anchoring of model time to real time","RNA secondary-structure thermodynamics","multi-criteria weighted scoring","first-order environmental fate modelling with Q10 correction"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education was pitched per audience: a 'Code of Life' session at Huangchenggen Primary School on 13 April 2026 explaining DNA, genes and proteins with locally resonant analogies (the double helix as a twisted Chinese fried dough twist) followed by hands-on strawberry DNA extraction with Grade 6 pupils using dish soap, salt and ethanol; lab visits connecting textbook content to a working research environment; interactive games on termites, evolution and biodiversity; a May Day fun fair co-hosted with PKUHSC; and a Biodiversity Fair booth, reaching primary and middle school students, university students, families and the general public.","ihp":"The origin story is unusually concrete and honest: a team member looked up at a century-old campus building and saw termite tunnels inside a wooden beam no maintenance record had noted, and the team then realised every member came from a termite-affected region (Beijing, Sichuan, Fujian, Shanghai, Chongqing, Suzhou). Before any protocol was drafted they ran a questionnaire across ages, backgrounds and regions in China and overseas, collecting 539 valid responses; respondents rated heritage-building termite risk at 7.99 out of 8 and named three priorities — early-warning monitoring, low environmental impact, and target specificity — which the team acknowledge essentially outline the final project. Fifteen students ran a closed-loop review every Saturday evening from April to October 2026, feeding ~17 deep consultations with entomologists, heritage conservators, frontline control officers, RNAi industry people, PIs and past iGEMers and judges. They record what they killed as well as what they built: broad-spectrum pesticides were ruled out as leaving residues unacceptable for sites like the Forbidden City, and crude biocontrol strains were ruled out as too blunt for ancient wooden halls, each discarded idea costing weeks. Community work included six gatherings (NCIM meetup, Synbiopunk-CCiC Dalian, the 1st Grand APiC where TermiTox won Best Project, Synbio Challenge Shenzhen, the Peking-Rice International HP Summit they co-organised with 50+ universities, and the SZU green-bio roundtable).","realWorld":"The application is cultural-heritage conservation — the Forbidden City, old temples and traditional courtyard houses — where residue-free, species-specific control matters more than speed. The modelling fed directly into deliverables: the colony model's counter-intuitive finding that Strain B must be *kept* in the bait when Strain A is added (because withdrawing B lets surviving larvae become replacement queens and the colony recovers) is implementable only with a refillable bait station, which the team designed as hardware; and the finding that multiple bait stations break the distance gradient and remove spatial refuges became a stated deployment principle for placing several recoverable stations around a protected structure. The quantitative deliverable is a usable specification: ~5% w/w dry yeast loading, ~65 days to collapse for a constrained colony of order 10^4 termites, ~10-10.5 months for a field colony of several hundred thousand.","partners":["Peking University","PKUHSC (Peking University Health Science Center)","Huangchenggen Primary School","Rice University (co-organised International HP Summit)","Shenzhen University (SZU green-bio roundtable)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6366/wiki/model/quick-view/termitox-model-whole-picture-v5.webp","caption":"The whole picture of the TermiTox multiscale model: molecular dynamics of the pH-gated LLO mutant, a cohort-ODE individual model of yeast persistence through payload release to functional decline, and a spatially explicit agent-based colony model of trophallactic spread, with each scale's output feeding the next.","credit":"Team Peking, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6366/wiki/model/colony-scale/figure-08-spatial-progression-one-bait.avif","caption":"Figure 8: spatial progression with one bait station. Columns are treatment days 10-120, rows are worker cohorts grouped by home module, and the three stacked panels show ever-exposed fraction, payload burden in a no-damage tracer run, and functional-worker fraction. Distant modules are exposed later and stay functional longer, acting as spatial refuges — a result that only appears because the nest geometry is modelled explicitly instead of as an SIR epidemic.","credit":"Team Peking, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6366/wiki/project/dry-design/model-validation-seven-literature-benchmarks-v2.avif","caption":"Seven published experiments reproduced by the individual-level model before it was used to predict anything about the team's own system — on yeast persistence, protozoa-dependent digestion and oral RNAi.","credit":"Team Peking, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6366/wiki/model/molecular-scale/figure-03-acidic-triad-closeup.avif","caption":"The acidic triad (D208/E247/D320) of H311A-LLO under acidic (left) and neutral (right) conditions. The salt-bridge network holds at pH 5.5 and separates at pH 7.4, supporting the safety premise that the pore-forming toxin is active only inside acidic protozoan food vacuoles — an allosteric effect, since the mutation site sits 17-18 Angstrom away and never contacts the triad.","credit":"Team Peking, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Before writing a single equation, build an evidence map: a table listing every process you want to model, the published experiment that constrains it, what that experiment can and cannot separate, and a verdict of identifiable / composite / not identifiable — then build only the smallest model those data support, and carry multiple scenarios where the verdict is 'not identifiable' instead of asserting one. Then anchor model time to reality by simulating a well-documented reference compound through the same pipeline.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/model/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/software/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/description/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/engineering/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/results/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/peking/education/"],"rank":21},{"team":"Oxford","id":"6391","section":"Undergrad","country":"United Kingdom","village":"Agriculture","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/","projectTitle":"NECTAR","tagline":"An end-to-end platform for designing, validating, producing and deploying RNAi interventions, stress-tested on Varroa destructor","summary":"NECTAR is a programmable RNAi pipeline: FORAGER (computational dsRNA design with off-target screening against the Apis mellifera transcriptome), microbial production in yeast, and delivery to hives in a pollen patty. Varroa destructor, the single biggest threat to honeybees, is the first real-world case study. The team built HIVE, an explicit four-step human-practices decision procedure (Hear, Investigate, Verdict, Evaluate) run over six open project questions with 28+ named stakeholders including US EPA/FDA/USDA staff, the Chair of the UK Advisory Committee on Releases to the Environment, and the Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity. Stakeholder evidence changed the chassis (living engineered Snodgrassella alvi to heat-inactivated yeast), the delivery format, the off-target screening stage, the survey instrument and the definition of success.","awards":["Best Wiki — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Integrated Human Practices — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Sustainable Development Impact — Nominee (Undergrad)","Best Agriculture Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"FORAGER, a three-stage standardised dsRNA design pipeline: target gene identification (most abundant transcripts of well-connected genes, weighted by known Varroa RNAi data), within-gene region selection (endogenous Varroa siRNA sequence features, guide competence, RNAplfold target accessibility, combined as three equally weighted complementary evidence layers rather than one fused score), and off-target analysis. Also a BEEHAVE-based colony population model and a bespoke partial-equilibrium economic model.","dataviz":"Hand-drawn mechanism illustrations throughout (FORAGER stages, the five-step path of a dose through the hive, the SDG goal map), a geocoded stakeholder map with latitude/longitude per interviewee, and per-cycle engineering tables; the wiki is a custom React/Astro-style build with light and dark themes.","datascience":"Construction of a clean Varroa destructor small-RNA dataset from public sequencing, basic population feature analysis of that sRNA pool, sequence feature analysis, whole-region homology and local exact-match screening against the honeybee transcriptome (16-nt strict mapping plus relaxed whole-region similarity), BLAST-based non-target screening, and a documented limitations table stating that the three evidence layers are equally weighted only because no Varroa RNAi efficacy data existed to fit weights.","software":"FORAGER is released with one file per stage and a documented frontmatter/body contract so other teams can re-run it; the HIVE method and its full interview question bank are released under CC BY 4.0. Hive insert and PCR-tube feeding adaptor are downloadable 3D-printable hardware.","stack":["ViennaRNA / RNAplfold","BLAST","Bowtie","BEEHAVE (NetLogo)","Boltz structure prediction","molecular docking","R","Python","React / Astro wiki build"],"techniques":["functional network connectivity analysis (STRING-type)","mRNA abundance weighting","small-RNA population profiling","RNA secondary structure and accessibility prediction","MS2 hairpin array folding optimisation (6% to 67% complete-array folding)","partial-equilibrium economic modelling with supply/demand elasticities","sensitivity analysis"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Buzz In, a three-episode stakeholder podcast built deliberately to put stakeholders who disagreed with each other into contact rather than collecting views in isolation: episode 1 paired Varroa-resistance academic Prof. Stephen Martin with commercial beekeeper Paul Horton on whether to treat at all; episode 2 brought together the CEO of the Australian Honey Bee Industry Council, Amateur Beekeepers Australia and Hive & Wellness; episode 3 set the project against social, environmental and economic lenses with a social scientist, a bee researcher and an agricultural economist. GMO 101, a joint Zoom workshop with NYU Abu Dhabi iGEM on 8 October 2026, measured participation through Nearpod (24 participants, 83% took part). A beekeeper survey received a favourable opinion from a subcommittee of the Oxford Central University Research Ethics Committee (ref MS IDREC 3973787), with public task as the lawful basis and informed consent before questions; 100+ respondents, 63% reported struggling with Varroa at mean severity 5.74/10 and 70% said they would be open to a GMO-based treatment. School sessions at St Clare's summer course were run as a two-way choice exercise (present two delivery routes with honest trade-offs, ask which students would accept, measure the acceptance shift before and after) rather than a lecture. Plus street interviews, a bee campaign, Instagram and TikTok.","ihp":"Oxford's IHP is the strongest kind: a documented decision procedure with a mandatory 'How we implemented the advice to change NECTAR' field on every one of 28+ interview write-ups, and a rule that 'We considered their feedback is not an implementation'. The largest single change is the chassis. Danny Le Feuvre (CEO, Australian Honey Bee Industry Council) and Mike Allerton (VP and Biosecurity Officer, Amateur Beekeepers Australia) both preferred a persistent living engineered Snodgrassella alvi gut symbiont because it minimises beekeeper labour; the team did not simply follow them but took the question to regulators, and Laura Bowden (GM manager, Science and Advice for Scottish Agriculture), Elizabeth Frost (NSW Department of Primary Industries), Tom Hartley (Senior Certification Officer, Soil Association Certification) and the Scottish Government Honey Bee Health Team pushed the opposite way, so the team switched to heat-inactivated, dried engineered yeast and stated plainly what that cost: the persistence that made S. alvi attractive, meaning repeat dosing and more beekeeper labour, against the preference of beekeepers they had interviewed. Austein McLoughlin of the Convention on Biological Diversity Secretariat then confirmed the inactivated product is not a living modified organism under the Cartagena Protocol, but also refused to let the team stop at SDG alignment, pushing them to ask whether NECTAR produces 'measurable improvement' - which is why the sustainability analysis widened to the whole NECTAR lifecycle and built baseline comparisons into the modelling. A joint meeting with Mike Mendelsohn and Alan Reynolds (US EPA), Adam Moyer and Laura Epstein (FDA) and Alan Pearson (USDA) then 'prevented us from treating our shift to heat-inactivated yeast as a universal regulatory shortcut', forcing jurisdiction-by-jurisdiction regulatory mapping instead of one global classification. The deepest economic chain runs through Prof. Wally Thurman (Agricultural and Resource Economics, NC State, interviewed 29 July 2026) and Prof. Brittney Goodrich (Agricultural and Consumer Economics, Illinois, 7 August 2026): Thurman set five methodological commitments (start from an average commercial operation not an industry aggregate; use existing treatments as the counterfactual; assess costs and revenues not survival rates; use simple partial equilibrium; restrict to paid pollination markets), and Goodrich's operation-level cost structure redefined the cost of Varroa to include labour, colony replacement and lost honey and pollination revenue rather than just the price of acaricide. The team then ran the chain backwards - what a beekeeper can afford, to allowable cost per dose, to allowable manufacturing cost, to required mass of intact dsRNA per gram of dry yeast - converting 'high expression' into a pass/fail wet-lab specification of 0.005 mg dsRNA per g yeast for US viability and 0.05 mg/g for both markets, against a fermentation cost of about 2.33 USD per gram. They also deliberately recruited dissent: the Oxfordshire Natural Beekeeping Group (about 20 chemical-free hobbyists) and treatment-free beekeeper Mark Sandham argued that treating removes the selection pressure that would produce tolerant bees, an argument against the entire premise; rather than abandoning the project the team narrowed its target user to commercial operations and wrote that OxNatBees changed 'not the molecular mechanism of NECTAR, but the problem we believed NECTAR should - and should not - try to solve'. Smaller but equally traceable: Lord John Krebs (first FSA Chairman) and Prof. Jim Dunwell (Chair, ACRE) made environmental fate and off-target effects explicit engineering requirements, moving non-target organisms into the FORAGER off-target screening stage and adding in vivo dsRNA-persistence experiments; Prof. Paul Lam (environmental chemistry, Hong Kong Metropolitan University) drove the adoption of BLAST off-target screening and reframed public engagement as risk management rather than promotion; Dr Michael Morrison (HeLEX, Oxford Law) rewrote the survey instrument so it asked separately about pollen patties, constitutively active engineered bacteria and inducible engineered bacteria rather than about 'GMOs' in general; Dr Théotime Colin's point about secondary pests prompted dsRNA constructs for wax moth and small hive beetle; Prof. Giles Budge's reassurance kept the adult-bee oral delivery route alive and produced the 300/500/700 bp length comparison; and Melanie Teece (Head of Technical, Hilltop Honey) and Wade Ford (Hive & Wellness) turned honey purity into a reason for additional in vivo work. The published HIVE guide names its own failure modes (interviewing agreeable people, treating 'the public' as one stakeholder, letting the Verdict drift into a summary, consulting regulators too late to change anything) and requires every Verdict to state what was decided, what was rejected and what that cost.","realWorld":"The need is framed with numbers rather than adjectives: Varroa is an invasive alien species across most of its range and the most serious pest of the western honeybee; Australia's failed eradication attempt cost A$101 million and destroyed about 30,000 hives, and surveyed US commercial operations lost around 62% of colonies in 2024-25, estimated at over $600 million. SDGs are chosen, not collected: 15 (Life on Land) and 2 (Zero Hunger) are the primary goals with 17 and 4 as enabling goals, each mapped to named sub-targets (15.8 on invasive alien species is the headline, plus 15.1, 15.5, 15.9), and the analysis extends into the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework targets 7, 10, 11 and 17 at the CBD Secretariat's suggestion. The team then writes a dedicated negative-impacts section on goal conflicts - accessibility across economic status, pollution, ecological threat, and whether healthier managed colonies put pressure on wild pollinators (goal 15 against goal 2) - and admits an unresolved contradiction: stakeholders insisted cost was decisive while 48% of their own survey respondents ranked cost least important, with the honest line 'We have not resolved this'. Deployment rides on existing practice (dsRNA-bearing heat-killed yeast in a conventional pollen patty laid over the frames, plus a 3D-printable hive insert), the regulatory path is mapped per jurisdiction after the EPA/FDA/USDA conversation, and the cost model gives both a per-colony net private benefit and the titre threshold manufacturing must hit. They are unusually candid about what is not demonstrated: no dsRNA titre was measured against the model thresholds, the yeast could not be cloned and the E. coli route gave no detectable dsRNA, so 'this is a case for the approach, not a demonstration of the product'. Handover material - FORAGER, the HIVE cycle and question bank, the bee research guide, protocols and hardware files - is released CC BY 4.0 for future teams.","partners":["Amateur Beekeepers Australia","Australian Honey Bee Industry Council","Hive & Wellness Australia","NSW Department of Primary Industries","Science and Advice for Scottish Agriculture (SASA)","Scottish Government Honey Bee Health Team","Advisory Committee on Releases to the Environment (ACRE)","Soil Association Certification","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity","US EPA, FDA and USDA","Hilltop Honey","Sustainable Conservation / Almond Board of California","Oxfordshire Natural Beekeeping Group","NYU Abu Dhabi iGEM","University of Oxford Department of Biology (Prof. Geraldine Wright)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6391/wiki/assets/drawings/sdg-description.avif","caption":"NECTAR at the centre of four chosen SDGs - 15 Life on Land and 2 Zero Hunger as primary goals, 17 Partnerships and 4 Quality Education as enabling goals - each with its own justification rather than a logo wall","credit":"Team Oxford, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6391/wiki/assets/education/gmo-webinar-zoom.avif","caption":"GMO 101, the joint Zoom workshop with NYU Abu Dhabi iGEM on 8 October 2026, with participation measured through Nearpod","credit":"Team Oxford, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6391/wiki/assets/bee-research-guide/walking-to-the-hives.avif","caption":"The team walking to the hives - fifteen members with zero beekeeping experience at the start of the project","credit":"Team Oxford, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6391/wiki/assets/education/street-interview.avif","caption":"Street interviews on public attitudes to GMOs in bee treatment, run as risk management rather than promotion after advice from Prof. Paul Lam","credit":"Team Oxford, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Make the write-up template itself enforce integration: every stakeholder record must carry a 'what this changed' field, and every verdict must name what was decided, what was rejected and what that cost - then run the affordability chain backwards from what the user can pay to a numerical pass/fail specification for the bench.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/sustainability","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/economic-modelling","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/case-studies","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/beekeeper-survey","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/engineering","https://2026.igem.wiki/oxford/project-description"],"rank":47},{"team":"NFLSHS-CHINA","id":"6439","section":"High School","country":"China","village":"","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/","projectTitle":"CroTeen","tagline":"Microbial crocetin: a controllable cell-factory supplement to saffron farming, taken all the way to B2B ingredient specs","summary":"NFLSHS-China built a fully heterologous crocetin pathway in E. coli (idi, CrtE, CrtB, CrtI, CrtY, CrtZ upstream to zeaxanthin; CsCCD2 plus Neurospora crassa NcALD downstream to free crocetin), then re-engineered it after wet-lab failure from a two-plasmid system into a single-plasmid, two-T7-cassette construct (pET-Ds-Cro) for strain stability under scale-up. They used AlphaFold3, PrankWeb and CB-Dock2 to guide semi-rational mutagenesis of the rate-limiting CsCCD2 enzyme, with the N366G mutant giving a 1.13-fold yield increase confirmed by HPLC. The commercial side is unusually disciplined for a high-school team: a named B2B beachhead, a filed and accepted patent application on the CsCCD2 mutant, two physical MVPs with full formula tables, and an explicit separation of what is designed from what is still unvalidated. They won Best Entrepreneurship and Best Presentation in the High School section.","awards":["Best Presentation — Winner (High School)","Best Entrepreneurship — Winner (High School)"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Lean Canvas (problem, solution, UVP, key metrics, customer segments, channels, unfair advantage, cost structure, revenue streams), TAM-SAM-SOM with an explicit refusal to take a percentage of the macro market, SWOT, a risk-assessment matrix, a stage-gated Gantt chart from 2026 to 2032, three named exit options, and Techno-Economic Analysis plus Life Cycle Assessment named as future obligations.","dataviz":"Nineteen numbered entrepreneurship figures: pathway maps for the two-plasmid and single-plasmid constructs, the biomanufacturing-to-application three-stage chain, a capability-gap table, a partnership-network map with a disclaimer about which relationships are confirmed, a risk matrix, a SWOT quadrant and an SDG panel. HPLC chromatograms carry the quantitative claims.","datascience":"HPLC quantification of crocetin as the core analytical readout; mutant screening comparing predicted variants against measured titre (N366G at 1.13x). No fermentation kinetics model or cost model with numbers.","software":"","stack":["AlphaFold3","PrankWeb","CB-Dock2","HPLC","pET-Duet-1 backbone","E. coli BL21(DE3)","E. coli DH5-alpha"],"techniques":["heterologous carotenoid pathway assembly","single-plasmid multi-cassette expression with RBS re-tuning","semi-rational enzyme engineering from binding-pocket prediction","site-directed mutagenesis","vacuum freeze-drying and bead-beating extraction","soft-capsule and essence-oil formulation"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"An online survey plus street interviews run 12-17 March 2026 across mixed demographics found over 60 percent of respondents had never heard of crocetin and routinely confused it with crocin, and that over 40 percent would pay only 5-10 RMB per day for a mood-soothing product. That cognitive gap triggered a dedicated education page and a Xiaohongshu (Rednote) account translating biosynthesis into public infographics. They also presented at the 10th iSCRM and the 1st Grand APiC conference, and built peer resources after hearing that team members elsewhere felt unsure their assigned role mattered.","ihp":"Five explicitly staged iHP phases, each written as motivation, action and integration, with dates and named interlocutors. Phase I traced the supply chain to source: an online interview with Iranian saffron grower Ali Norouzi (17 March) produced concrete constraints (15-20 day harvest window, 1-2 g dried saffron per person per day, water shortage, currency volatility), and visits to Daofengtang Rende TCM Clinic and chain pharmacies produced a real price point of 60-90 RMB per gram and the observation that pharmacies barely stock it. Phase II is the strongest: after near-zero crocetin yield on 12 May, Dr Huang Danqiong diagnosed metabolic burden and plasmid instability and told them to collapse to a single plasmid, which they did; Prof Huang Guanqin then reviewed the single-plasmid circuit against scale-up cost on 20 June; Fapon's technical director on 22 July pushed them off blind screening onto AI-guided rational design. Phase III used Associate Professor Li Chenyang twice to kill an unstable aqueous formulation on physicochemical grounds and pick the oil and capsule routes, and a perfumer, Ms Weng, taught the actual blending process. Phase IV took the business plan to Attorney Zhang on 17 September for NMPA/FDA classification, licensing and labelling risk, which changed their claims language across the wiki and social media. This is iHP that visibly rewrote both the construct and the marketing copy.","realWorld":"This is a real venture attempt, not a canvas exercise. The business model is specifically B2B-first: the beachhead is small-to-medium functional skincare brands and boutique cosmetic R&D labs, chosen because they have shorter decision cycles than multinationals, and the first deliverable is explicitly not a consumer product but standardised samples plus HPLC chromatograms to support a customer's own formulation prototyping. Market evidence is real but qualitative: interviews across the full chain (Iranian grower, TCM clinic and pharmacies, boutique formulators, Fapon, an iGEM-mentor founder, a lawyer), a public survey with reported percentages and a willingness-to-pay band, and a visit to Shenzhen University's fermentation facility. They refuse to multiply the roughly USD 2.0 billion TAM by a made-up share and instead say SOM must be computed from reachable R&D nodes times sample volume per test, which is the correct instinct even though they then do not finish the arithmetic. Hard assets exist: a patent application, Carotenoid Cleavage Dioxygenase 2 Mutant, Preparation Method and Application, filed and accepted, and two physical MVPs with published formula tables down to percentages (essence oil at 0.03 percent crocetin, 0.5 percent vitamin E in cold-pressed olive oil; capsule at 70 percent crocetin-olive oil, 15 percent L-theanine, 13 percent soy lecithin, 2 percent vitamin E). The regulatory path is credible and specific, naming NMPA and FDA classification, production and distribution licensing, and the prohibition on medical claims, with a consequence they actually implemented, standardising functional claims site-wide. The continuation plan is a stage-gated Gantt from 2026 lab validation through 2027 customer testing, 2028 CDMO pilot and 2029 B2B commercialisation to 2030-2032 international registration, where each gate has named prerequisite resources and a numeric-in-principle pass condition, plus three exit routes (trade sale to BASF, Givaudan or DSM-Firmenich; technology licensing with royalties; VC/PE buyout). What is genuinely missing is the money: there are no unit economics, no cost of goods, no price per gram, no funding raised, no registered entity and no incubator place, and the partnership map carries its own honest disclaimer that everything except university support and paid service relationships is a prospective contact rather than a formal agreement. They also list, in their own words, the data still absent before industrialisation, including yield, purity, recovery, batch consistency and pilot-scale mutant validation. Even the downside is faced squarely, including the possibility that fermentation's carbon footprint exceeds agriculture's and that cheap biomanufacturing could destroy saffron growers' livelihoods, for which they propose market segmentation that leaves the high-end plant premium alone.","partners":["Fapon (biotech company; technical director and business development; AI platform exposure and commercialisation advice)","Shenzhen University (Prof. Wang Chaogang, Prof. Huang Guanqin, Prof. Chen Weizhao; fermentation facility access)","Dr. Huang Danqiong (fermentation expert; diagnosed the two-plasmid failure)","Associate Professor Li Chenyang (formulation and safety screening)","Ali Norouzi (Iranian saffron grower and supplier)","Daofengtang Rende TCM Clinic and local chain pharmacies","Li Shiyuan (founder, Shanghai Transformant Information Technology Co.; iGEM mentor; technical evaluation)","Attorney Zhang (regulatory and IP legal review)","Ms. Weng (perfumer; essential-oil production process)"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6439/wiki/entrepreneurship8.avif","caption":"Patent application for the CsCCD2 mutant, filed and accepted, shown on the entrepreneurship page as the project's first hard IP asset","credit":"Team NFLSHS-CHINA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6439/wiki/entrepreneurship10.avif","caption":"Stage-gated development plan from 2026 laboratory validation through 2029 B2B commercialisation to 2030-2032 international expansion","credit":"Team NFLSHS-CHINA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6439/wiki/entrepreneurship9.avif","caption":"Business exit strategy: trade sale to an ingredient major, technology licensing with royalties, or VC/PE buyout","credit":"Team NFLSHS-CHINA, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6439/wiki/entrepreneurship17v2.avif","caption":"SWOT analysis supporting the B2B-first ingredient strategy","credit":"Team NFLSHS-CHINA, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy their honesty architecture: a 'Current Validation Status' block that splits completed designs from data still required, and a partnership map annotated to say which relationships are confirmed and which are merely prospective contacts. Pair it with their stage-gate habit, where each phase of the roadmap names its prerequisite resources and the single pass condition that unlocks the next phase, so a project cannot drift forward on enthusiasm.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/entrepreneurship/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/human-practices/","https://2026.igem.wiki/nflshs-china/product/"],"rank":52},{"team":"Athens","id":"6478","section":"Overgrad","country":"Greece","village":"Conservation","wiki":"https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/","projectTitle":"ApiconIASIS","tagline":"Healing honeybees, sustaining pollinators: a precision phage-derived enzyme therapy for European Foulbrood","summary":"ApiconIASIS targets European Foulbrood (EFB), a statutory honeybee brood disease caused by Melissococcus plutonius for which the only legal response in most of Europe is burning the colony. The team mined 165 M. plutonius genomes for dormant prophages, ranked hundreds of candidate lysins and selected two enzymes (an endolysin and a GH25 muramidase), each built with and without a fused antimicrobial peptide, expressed and purified in E. coli. They showed roughly 40% loss of OD600 against M. plutonius (LMG 20360) with 50 ug/mL purified endolysin while producing no effect distinguishable from the untreated control against the bee-gut commensals Lactobacillus apis and Bombilactobacillus mellis, both Lactobacillales like the pathogen. It won because the whole chain is closed: a protein (not a replicating phage) delivered in brood food, three linked dry-lab models (protein optimisation, larval-gut pharmacokinetics, bioprocess scale-up), and an entrepreneurship case built on the cost of hive burning.","awards":["Top 10 (Overgrad)","Best Model — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Education — Nominee (Overgrad)","Best Entrepreneurship — Winner (Overgrad)","Best Conservation Project — Winner"],"dry":{"frameworks":"Three distinct model layers. (1) A semi-rational protein engineering cascade for the poorly soluble GH25 muramidase (Protein-Sol 0.411, below the 0.45 aggregation threshold, with inclusion bodies confirmed in the wet lab): a 40-residue 'freeze list' built from three independent evidence sources (structural role, ESM-2 650M evolutionary constraint, literature), validated by all-atom molecular dynamics (RMSF < 2 A), then ThermoMPNN point-mutation ddG scanning, an ESM-2 log-likelihood filter at -2.0 to drop family-implausible substitutions, NSGA-II multi-objective search via pymoo over the 17 surviving mutations, and ColabFold refolding as a final structural gate catching epistasis; AlphaFold pLDDT > 70 was a stated input requirement. (2) A deterministic single-compartment ODE pharmacokinetic model of the honeybee larval midgut treating the lumen as a sealed, expanding compartment and the pathogen as absolute cell number N, with an Emax saturating kill law fitted to in-vitro lysis data (phi_max ~ 0.006125 /min, KE ~ 0.633 ug/mL, R2 = 0.935). (3) A bioprocess flowsheet model of a medium-scale E. coli plant with a three-stage seed train (baffled shake flask, seed bioreactor, production fermenter under high-cell-density culture), solving for biomass and product mass per batch.","dataviz":"The wiki stages results as a narrative rather than a figure dump: the landing page counts down the disease (90% brood mortality, 36% of surveyed colonies carrying the bacterium with 72% of those looking healthy) before showing the funnel 165 genomes to hundreds of candidates to two enzymes to one lead, then flask to bioreactor to hive to landscape. The model section front-loads a one-sentence summary of each pipeline and marks P1-P4 as falsifiable predictions, with P4 flagged as the main untested exposure because binding selectivity was never an optimisation objective. The optimisation page carries a Venn diagram of the three freeze-list evidence sources, RMSF and DCCM plots from the MD run, an ESM-2 log-likelihood filter plot and the NSGA-II Pareto front.","datascience":"Biological measurements were converted into decision rules rather than descriptive statistics. A pH-plate experiment was decomposed into two separable effects: kill rate is essentially flat across the lumen band (pH 5.5-7.5) while stability is strongly pH-dependent (t_half ~140-280 h at pH 5.5-6.7, ~7 h at pH 4, ~12 h at pH 7.5), which showed the enzyme is about 30x more stable in the lumen than assumed and dropped the required food concentration about 31-fold, making dilution rather than degradation the dominant loss term. Because nurse bees feed roughly 4,300 times a day, delivery was modelled as a continuous infusion at quasi-steady state. Full sterilisation within 48 h holds only up to about 455 cells (a saturation, not a dose, limit; a 300-cell inoculum already needs 26.2 ug/mL against a larva-safe cap of 23 ug/mL), so the team switched to a 'recovery' criterion that raised the handleable inoculum by four to five orders of magnitude to about 10^7 cells. The model's stated output is a starting dose plus an exposure profile (peak concentration, AUC, dosing duration, clearance time) handed to the wet lab and to a companion toxicity model.","software":"ApiconIASIS: Bee Wanderer, a browser-based educational game built for the EU-CONEXUS Hackathon in plain JavaScript, HTML5 Canvas, CSS3 and Three.js with no game engine: a custom 2D platformer engine (requestAnimationFrame loop, AABB collision, sinusoidal moving platforms, parallax) in level-engine.js with per-level config files, plus an interactive WebGL globe using Three.js OrbitControls and raycasting for level selection across Athens, the Himalayas, Tanzania, Uganda and New Zealand. Collectibles map to biotech stages (DNA Pollen, Enzyme Drops, Purification Beads); progression persists in localStorage with no backend, and a Weather Station pulls live data from Open-Meteo and wttr.in. All dry-lab scripts are released at https://github.com/igemathens2026/iGEM-Athens-2026---Software-; the wiki repository is gitlab.igem.org/2026/athens.","stack":["ESM-2 650M","ThermoMPNN","pymoo (NSGA-II)","ColabFold","AlphaFold","Protein-Sol","all-atom molecular dynamics (RMSF, DCCM)","Python","JavaScript / HTML5 Canvas","Three.js / WebGL","Open-Meteo API","wttr.in API","GitHub","GitLab"],"techniques":["multi-objective Pareto optimisation (NSGA-II)","protein language model constraint profiling","hard-constraint freeze lists from multi-source evidence","ddG stability scanning","structural validation gate (refolding)","single-compartment ODE pharmacokinetics","Emax saturating kill-law fitting","prophage mining across 165 genomes","bioprocess flowsheet simulation","seed-train scale-up modelling"]},"good":{"publicEngagement":"Education was run as a programme rather than a set of visits, across age groups and education levels, and deliberately bilingual (every pamphlet and deck in English and Greek). At NTUA Open Days (17 March 2026) they reached over 100 STEM-track candidates at an engineering school where students have largely dropped biology, pairing an interactive presentation on industrial biotechnology and its ethical framework with a tour of the Laboratory of Biotechnology and Industrial Biocatalysis; the team recorded that the students were most engaged by ethics, and built that framing into every later event. The portfolio also includes a multi-day summer programme at Villa Aimilia (design-a-microbe, strawberry DNA extraction, bees-leaves-cells), Second Chance Schools in Peristeri and Piraeus for adult learners, the 4th Panhellenic Synthetic Biology Riddle Competition, ChemExpo, the Pasteur institute and iGEM Fest. The Bee Wanderer game extends the same material into a free browser format.","ihp":"The human practices page is written as a ledger of what the project gave up. Two requirements came from beekeeping, not from the team, and now define success: the protein must reach the brood, and it must survive the larval gut. Delivery was moved from spray to feed because adjuvant surfactants are documented to harm bees and because the person holding the sprayer inhales it; protective equipment therefore appears in the protocols, not in the product. They chose a purified non-replicating enzyme over a live phage knowing it is weaker and more expensive, because a protein cannot spread, establish or transfer genes. They counter-screened against Lactobacillus apis and Bombilactobacillus mellis, the closest relatives of the pathogen in the hive, instead of easier distant organisms, and they used a reduced-virulence M. plutonius lineage although a virulent strain was available, accepting a weaker efficacy claim. Advisor Anastasis Galanis and beekeeper Peter Kasztelewicz (whose own hive footage is on the wiki) shaped these choices while they were being made; Peter's gentler methods, requeening and partial clean-out, are what motivated the feed route.","realWorld":"ApiconIASIS is positioned as the first target-specific sustainable EFB treatment, with the value proposition pinned to a quantified loss: quarantine and hive burning halt honey production for about eight weeks on top of the destroyed colony. Unlike antibiotics (not authorised in the EU, and resistance-breeding where used) or fungal extracts, the enzyme is argued to leave the bee microbiome intact and to leave no residue in honey, preserving market value. The business plan carries a pitch deck and an investor one-pager; the entrepreneurial analysis includes a customer map built from interviews with commercial beekeeping companies and individual beekeepers, and the scale-up model gives the costing basis (105 L final working volume, 51.62 g/L maximum biomass, 2.55 g product per batch) with the explicit target of pricing below the financial loss of hive burning so that no beekeeper is excluded.","partners":["Peter Kasztelewicz ('Beeman'), beekeeper, UK","Anastasis Galanis (team advisor)","National Technical University of Athens, School of Chemical Engineering (Prof. E. Topakas)","Laboratory of Biotechnology and Industrial Biocatalysis, NTUA","EU-CONEXUS Hackathon","TechnosetBee","Greek beekeeping associations","Villa Aimilia summer programme","Second Chance Schools, Peristeri and Piraeus","Hellenic Pasteur Institute","Twist Bioscience","SnapGene"]},"images":[{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6478/wiki/optimization/fig7-pareto.avif","caption":"Pareto front from the NSGA-II search over the 17 mutable positions, trading predicted solubility against thermostability (ddG) for the GH25 muramidase; variants A, B and C were selected along this front.","credit":"Team Athens, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6478/wiki/optimization/venn-freeze.avif","caption":"Venn diagram of the three independent evidence sources (structural role, ESM-2 evolutionary constraint, literature) used to build the 40-residue freeze list; a position was frozen only if at least two sources agreed.","credit":"Team Athens, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6478/wiki/optimization/fig6a-rmsf.avif","caption":"Root-mean-square fluctuation from the all-atom MD run, confirming that the frozen residues are genuinely rigid (RMSF below 2 A) and turning the freeze list from a heuristic into a tested claim.","credit":"Team Athens, iGEM 2026 wiki"},{"url":"https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6478/wiki/education/day2-strawberry-dna-villa-aimilia.avif","caption":"Strawberry DNA extraction with children at the Villa Aimilia summer programme, day two of a hands-on sequence that began with design-a-microbe and ended with bees, leaves and cells.","credit":"Team Athens, iGEM 2026 wiki"}],"gemsTakeaway":"Copy the freeze-list discipline: before optimising anything, lock the residues (or variables) that at least two independent lines of evidence call functional, then prove the lock with a simulation, and only then search aggressively over what is left; it converts a student ML pipeline into a defensible claim. Equally transferable is Athens' habit of writing down what each stakeholder conversation cost them (spray to feed, phage to enzyme, virulent to weak strain), which is far more persuasive to judges than a list of meetings.","sources":["https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/model","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/model/in-silico-optimization","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/model/pharmacokinetics-model","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/model/software","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/human-practices","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/education","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/entrepreneurship","https://2026.igem.wiki/athens/entrepreneurship/business-plan"],"rank":136}];
